stion/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d5198c-6b6f-5632-b7b7-465246d70d80" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186236237} {:answer/id #uuid "454bcc72-bca7-50bf-94e8-db8856ee234a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186236261} {:answer/id #uuid "59aa4dc4-fbd8-53da-a93e-59220bf8788b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186236379} {:answer/id #uuid "8b431f2b-c47b-5178-af9e-b06c1a7995b5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236689} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ae423f-08fb-5fee-bfcc-a310ea68b8f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186237067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5ae423f-08fb-5fee-bfcc-a310ea68b8f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186237067} :answer-field/id #uuid "2db3e535-a6f3-5416-bf52-4dcd1389dc12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c9e4a51-c450-52e6-9d4d-f69fe8fdfc95" :question/math-academy-id "q-102673" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n- $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n- $C={A}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true. As a result, $C={A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186237922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b9ef325-2358-5041-8a8d-b459e8ed9f09" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08427311-55df-5fca-8c81-b3d43755834b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186235853} {:answer/id #uuid "2353948c-bc8b-5f0b-98ab-1abe270ac07d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186236042} {:answer/id #uuid "a4476340-bf39-5aaf-a573-7229498e24af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186236846} {:answer/id #uuid "b7773c7d-3bdb-5acf-be2b-51eef75585ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236982} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd62949-9c60-508b-903d-59d06baacfbc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186237109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08427311-55df-5fca-8c81-b3d43755834b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186235853} :answer-field/id #uuid "83dde5d1-ffc2-549e-a497-8373df71d13d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98689422-75b7-52ec-a8b8-4a4a4c165a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-102674" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n\nII. $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n\nIII. $B={A}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true. As a result, $B={A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & -5 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186237923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b9ef325-2358-5041-8a8d-b459e8ed9f09" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d32226b-4a5c-5ca1-a94c-eb0a19d093db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186235889} {:answer/id #uuid "1c0d83e4-9c45-5545-a833-b798417f2329" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186235996} {:answer/id #uuid "621f1133-add7-5cb0-b027-86691e81ad44" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236439} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4d8c1d-193e-53ee-8b93-897b2c4bda25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186236711} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d75378-7dcf-50aa-9f3b-e25d4b3f4372" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186237143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1d75378-7dcf-50aa-9f3b-e25d4b3f4372" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186237143} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5de887-4a19-59e0-af28-660cabc75781" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7fa0b65-e3f9-5815-a23d-64900cd2d836" :question/math-academy-id "q-102675" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n- $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n- $B\\ne {A}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false. As a result, $B\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186237974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a354e63-8613-53db-87e2-25a664b37cdc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186236080} {:answer/id #uuid "2b12a7fb-4d6f-544a-a96f-ed35aacbc53d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186236086} {:answer/id #uuid "5a87be2c-5994-5264-a776-dae0ae4f4cbd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186236383} {:answer/id #uuid "866e96f5-4b4a-5959-b121-cdb20b47a301" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186236653} {:answer/id #uuid "b84cde27-376f-5d07-afc4-85d5372677dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186236993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b12a7fb-4d6f-544a-a96f-ed35aacbc53d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186236086} :answer-field/id #uuid "6701e43f-c4b2-5b9b-813e-73e8f05c1bee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "343b3c1e-da90-580a-91f6-9c9756fee1a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-103150" :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+3)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-1,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=-5$\n- The series converges at $x=-2$\n- The series converges at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=-3,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $2,$ i.e., $R\\ge 2.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-5,-1].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-5,-1].$\n\n![](images/7d/7d68a4bd81717c3ad663fc85c45dfce62217a046f683cc92a6869f87ff3b166a.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-5\\notin (-5,-1],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $-2\\in (-5,-1],$ the series is convergent at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $1\\notin (-5,-1],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186237975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c9471e0-137a-536a-bdea-8ab035f96e89" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186236304} {:answer/id #uuid "56e12010-1575-5bf0-9418-58ea47ea235b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186236367} {:answer/id #uuid "b7810ba6-1de7-51b9-81a4-8ad9b2127163" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186236983} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ce66c2-6b9a-5bcb-9a04-c36c7ad048e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186237189} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8eb5cb-fe1d-5506-ad9f-b52d18520a1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186237292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7810ba6-1de7-51b9-81a4-8ad9b2127163" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186236983} :answer-field/id #uuid "330cfe04-73b4-5fe7-9498-423d1a019e86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21ccde81-8669-5255-b2ed-8bdddd923bd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-103152" :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-5)^{n}$ converges at  $x=2,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series diverges at $x=6$\n- The series converges at $x=0$\n- The series converges at $x=8$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=5,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=2,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $3,$ i.e., $R\\ge 3.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[2,8).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[2,8).$\n\n![](images/df/dfd59d22892764c4da387d48b07a6a1069ea59e13be75cd099d21ce53975d64c.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $6\\in [2,8),$ the series is convergent at $x=6.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $0\\notin [2,8),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $8\\notin [2,8),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the listed.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186237976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4deaa6-1045-5a3c-a073-139ae1ce42c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186235882} {:answer/id #uuid "57a89d03-252b-5807-a5c8-42501698e359" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186236372} {:answer/id #uuid "b624e040-e7fb-58e5-825e-152465176a19" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236968} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e2031a-2dc6-5a9f-99ef-9b3f95c11fdf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186236986} {:answer/id #uuid "fd67b321-1d90-5bda-a2d0-26a61617da7a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186237425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c4deaa6-1045-5a3c-a073-139ae1ce42c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186235882} :answer-field/id #uuid "4de57f02-8a1e-5f60-8335-889ffbcc2c06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "93c0e2ef-7f5e-5e9b-aac8-f784a726661a" :question/math-academy-id "q-103169" :question/problem "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{n}$ converges at  $x=-1.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=2$\n- The series converges at $x=1$\n- The series converges at $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $\\frac{5}{2},$ i.e., $R\\ge \\frac{5}{2}.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-1,4).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-1,4).$\n\n![](images/d0/d01788625c7b429a15caf441c6a651fa92281dc544f99c544bdb12f9d8b6f032.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $2\\in [-1,4),$ the series is convergent at $x=2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $1\\in [-1,4),$ the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement III is not necessarily true. The given information guarantees convergence for points strictly between $-1$ and $4$, but it does not guarantee convergence at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186237977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d7d6e3d-fae5-5c77-8600-1c3cd9256323" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186236313} {:answer/id #uuid "7028432e-24cd-5f8a-a92a-aec62cef0c14" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186236526} {:answer/id #uuid "706dafcd-5568-5572-8b00-971cb5925392" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236527} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb443a9-a2ba-5536-919e-1755e5d3718b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186236595} {:answer/id #uuid "ff30d3ae-e93f-52a5-8c4c-c20e0596c02e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186237443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d7d6e3d-fae5-5c77-8600-1c3cd9256323" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186236313} :answer-field/id #uuid "33a22350-f913-543a-a22a-50b96be455dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "76850d0b-0e5d-5926-80f5-97253aff4b0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103170" :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+7)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-3,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=-11$\n- The series converges at $x=-8$\n- The series converges at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=-7,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-3,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $4,$ i.e., $R\\ge 4.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-11,-3].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-11,-3].$\n\n![](images/47/4764256e5f00e7a11d688bd99d5fa1c22db819e7a188113f0ef4cd987a9b7595.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-11\\notin (-11,-3],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $-8\\in (-11,-3],$ the series is convergent at $x=-8.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $1\\notin (-11,-3],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186237978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147499 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24115d7a-5a6a-5584-a664-89b0856ff10a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186236048} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0f403e-4aee-503b-bfb5-277bc79a0f32" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186236183} {:answer/id #uuid "3c15596e-43ae-585e-8959-c82bca97affb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186236192} {:answer/id #uuid "72bf4c08-6663-5e3d-b09c-5f3e53e1f0e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186236544} {:answer/id #uuid "cb25a744-60e0-5136-bf9b-d043a4bd32eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186237107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a0f403e-4aee-503b-bfb5-277bc79a0f32" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186236183} :answer-field/id #uuid "54c67153-b5bb-5f13-b369-17f7dd25f4e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59bba89f-5fae-5f25-a8be-5a25060edfd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103172" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)$\n- $3{y}^{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂x}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $Q(x,y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y).$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2})=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)$ gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186237979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147499 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c73fb72-7bad-55b7-8df0-3564802eedd0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236002} {:answer/id #uuid "3c78c80b-7c7c-538e-a803-cd22fbfa240a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186236196} {:answer/id #uuid "54af3b0f-3606-50e6-9b43-8824c1d473f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186236357} {:answer/id #uuid "8030e282-4115-5ea7-98a9-7a6ebe8548d7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186236614} {:answer/id #uuid "c4cde532-81c0-5acf-b2b1-d8e6bf3bed05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186237061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4cde532-81c0-5acf-b2b1-d8e6bf3bed05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186237061} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f790423-e118-5521-8bd6-4906f2ebe62c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d411ceca-268e-5683-adfb-764387aebbdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-103174" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n2x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $6x{y}^{2}$\n- $3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$\n- $2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{3})=6x{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂x}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{2})=6{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6x{y}^{2}=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''II only''."}] [{:db/id 17592186237980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147499 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f28d1f-9ec9-5aae-972b-44e57c0e305f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186236168} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcb5535-d83e-5bca-9145-03d3fe4428f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186236314} {:answer/id #uuid "6da1fdd4-3e85-5519-aa71-65b5414883f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II  only" :db/id 17592186236510} {:answer/id #uuid "e673ed0b-4cea-54f6-a6d2-9141063186e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186237265} {:answer/id #uuid "fdea57f7-2fa9-5ebc-a831-3f35becaa981" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186237431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dcb5535-d83e-5bca-9145-03d3fe4428f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186236314} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd3af6b5-6b6a-5486-986b-25a843b240ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ffb478e-6d0e-5395-bbb6-0e1bbace63ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-103175" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\n- ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}$\n- $-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$\n- ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2})=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2})=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''I only''."}] [{:db/id 17592186238005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04cfe56b-d41d-51e7-8a6d-278c93be391f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186235829} {:answer/id #uuid "72c9d30a-fc47-570c-aebe-123fa087f2a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236545} {:answer/id #uuid "73694786-288a-5bb0-834f-c0ec16d90edd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186236549} {:answer/id #uuid "a09bc388-6b9b-5207-9904-7b971e3a9fbd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186236820} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5ffece-f9f3-562c-aaf1-b82a6d0a8255" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186236912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5ffece-f9f3-562c-aaf1-b82a6d0a8255" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186236912} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccb96764-d461-5bc7-bf26-9228be058bcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f635edc1-2f85-538f-89d8-4edf62a22ccc" :question/math-academy-id "q-103437" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb61c048eb7eac87f82c53717f8f67251b59610817ddf1f267e80543c0c86959.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nI. $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-1$\n\nII. $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=-1,$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a critical point at $x=-1$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=-1$ since $f(-1)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false."}] [{:db/id 17592186238006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13dd7a1d-6e1e-5c4e-8ffc-a3153e3eb708" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186235937} {:answer/id #uuid "20152c00-5ca8-5ea9-ac9c-73e7dd073575" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186236024} {:answer/id #uuid "65094498-88d0-5ef7-b624-a7ad99681c2b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236458} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbbe92a-6675-56bd-8cbb-0662f54c8816" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id 17592186237308} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a629c9-8a66-5a06-a5d7-0337b5d8cec0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186237355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13dd7a1d-6e1e-5c4e-8ffc-a3153e3eb708" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186235937} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c06429b-063d-5dd6-90e0-86d2c9ed815b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2c02942-de68-547a-8bfa-7932a2f390a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-103438" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5bbb0cacbe8dca2facfd49e757ba9b7aa40ee91ae820f001c72d637186d57b79.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=-1.$ In this case,  ${f}^{′}(-1)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement II is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=0,$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=1$ since $f(1)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186240855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d03821-e8cc-52f3-93ab-ad3c305cc8a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186238258} {:answer/id #uuid "75f31ce3-8a84-5578-bc64-4329a88af693" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186239074} {:answer/id #uuid "781e360e-d1b8-5389-ae6e-0343b5376d80" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186239095} {:answer/id #uuid "edac4ef1-a0ae-5e43-a915-14ff95b9c4af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186240147} {:answer/id #uuid "f5651e57-e1d9-5d3f-845c-4c24a55d8e53" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186240221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75f31ce3-8a84-5578-bc64-4329a88af693" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186239074} :answer-field/id #uuid "c66e2d1e-413e-5c63-b78c-4afc8c4f0b4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d00cc33e-d21d-57ae-828a-16fa83891b51" :question/math-academy-id "q-104357" :question/problem "![](images/30/30df82b4c47905fd8277067f9207a5e970a96d8ec386879d2872b5f5c3d8dd3c.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 0=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 0=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186240856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0231ad89-5342-57ca-9d53-32d772e1889f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186238098} {:answer/id #uuid "467d4ffe-4683-59b2-977b-d353f6b4bb5e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186238669} {:answer/id #uuid "60607ebd-2ad4-55af-a90b-4623005e6e6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186238899} {:answer/id #uuid "a402837d-0060-5017-9153-e0d361735054" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186239479} {:answer/id #uuid "fa979380-dfc8-5f1d-b3eb-5f41cc72f76e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186240270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60607ebd-2ad4-55af-a90b-4623005e6e6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186238899} :answer-field/id #uuid "66ae6d06-50b2-5267-8241-314cfb5773ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc756662-eff5-518b-9ac2-39ac467a0419" :question/math-academy-id "q-104358" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c95fb061d6b860655e934f2691d03e53f7bb77a617200f523f1695ee49083fe.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=4=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false.  The function is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=2\\ne 4=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement  I is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186240899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129086 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fb24d2b-8efd-586e-a253-a50fc2629cfa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186239019} {:answer/id #uuid "80307242-8b68-591c-9255-4570a325b2c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186239171} {:answer/id #uuid "8baf082b-20a6-5070-a666-31e515517c98" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239267} {:answer/id #uuid "c13d07a3-eb6f-5324-82a9-e4ec44e5194f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186239745} {:answer/id #uuid "c18ace05-44b9-5c33-bcd4-98266f99d2a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186239751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8baf082b-20a6-5070-a666-31e515517c98" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239267} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5fab5aa-bc0a-5388-893b-7f8a16910152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bdde4d8-daa6-55ef-bcc6-21f99b29d77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104575" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-34, & x<-3 \\\\ -25, & x=-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2, & x>-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=g(-3)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(-3)=-25$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}({x}^{2}-34)=(-3)^{2}-34=-25, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}({x}^{3}+2)=(-3)^{3}+2=-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=-25.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=-25,$ and the value of the function is $g(-3)=-25.$ So, statement II is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=g(-3)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=-3,$ and statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186240912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc6b3ee-0251-5c9c-9358-bd192c434dd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to Scripted Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4be1bc93-8493-5e66-a47d-621415d1ec39" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id 17592186238710} {:answer/id #uuid "7da10354-414b-50b2-b592-50e606d3f904" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239145} {:answer/id #uuid "96baa967-179a-51a6-83c6-74754869b43c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186239358} {:answer/id #uuid "f03e1a9a-feb4-56fb-be3f-1df092c37e42" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186240173} {:answer/id #uuid "f23a0055-8221-5ffa-a1cf-fc4a5d521d86" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and IV only" :db/id 17592186240193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4be1bc93-8493-5e66-a47d-621415d1ec39" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id 17592186238710} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4546f66-8f1e-512d-a2f5-ed2460098882" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "016b340f-7aa5-5001-aeb3-927982c5cf50" :question/math-academy-id "q-104694" :question/problem "Which of the numbers below belong to ${Q}^{-}?$\n\n- $47$\n- $-\\sqrt{12}$\n- $\\sqrt{10}$\n- $0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${Q}^{-}$ is the set of all negative rational numbers.\n\n- $47$ is positive, not negative, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\sqrt{12}=-2\\sqrt{3}$ is not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $\\sqrt{10}$ is positive, not negative, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $0$ is not negative, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None of those listed\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186240914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc6b3ee-0251-5c9c-9358-bd192c434dd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to Scripted Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b20d257-4a95-5abf-b2e5-2514c08ed019" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186238567} {:answer/id #uuid "76f14fbf-0cd2-58d6-af80-e3ca89528cbc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186239080} {:answer/id #uuid "b4808275-bb4d-5d9e-813a-bca4fd59aaf1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239634} {:answer/id #uuid "cd98c5f5-3abc-54bd-beca-f2b3b5b8d3a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186239853} {:answer/id #uuid "d003736f-66bb-5503-8242-6a9fbd78c4d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186239874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d003736f-66bb-5503-8242-6a9fbd78c4d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186239874} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0ab0db5-b1d9-58c1-a116-377a898c022e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c84fa181-6eb1-5e84-b542-36ff40a06a4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104700" :question/problem "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${R}_{0}^{-}?$\n\n- $-14$\n- $\\sqrt{19}$\n- $-\\frac{2}{7}$\n- $0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${R}_{0}^{-}$ is the set of all negative real numbers and $0.$\n\n- $-14$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $\\sqrt{19}$ is a positive number, so it does *not* belong to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{2}{7}$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186240915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc6b3ee-0251-5c9c-9358-bd192c434dd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to Scripted Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290ee5b8-cbf7-5232-b509-85b70d336edc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id 17592186238419} {:answer/id #uuid "5d98a1c9-ea74-5d5a-9235-9cb7b2ccd0f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186238871} {:answer/id #uuid "939ad9f4-b5dd-56c0-9292-76f77d15c465" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239330} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e613a5-28c7-5ab0-88dd-d53e6c4d9408" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186239739} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c8f4ee-acc5-5ce3-8962-6b1e968a9d3a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186239766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "290ee5b8-cbf7-5232-b509-85b70d336edc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id 17592186238419} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fe985a1-5ce0-54a3-83a9-d5b67f4adb67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e62d29f-2834-5b4e-b720-bc361e2a5dc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104702" :question/problem "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${Z}_{0}^{-}?$\n\n- $-62$\n- $0$\n- $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- $-\\frac{56}{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${Z}_{0}^{-}$ is the set of all negative integer numbers and $0.$\n\n- $-62$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{56}{4}=-14$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186240916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d132801-b9cc-5c0c-a8fa-0c8b840c05bd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f508bc1-5c8c-5dac-b648-e683e6948a72" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186238320} {:answer/id #uuid "97344af5-4d0b-50ac-8b3e-e8c06cd07b1f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186239364} {:answer/id #uuid "aca5e5b6-04b0-5494-8936-3a1199e5007a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186239555} {:answer/id #uuid "b65deb58-d812-5ef0-9eed-c2321a16ab5a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186239648} {:answer/id #uuid "fe32c1df-bb7d-58a9-84d1-726cffbc66e9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186240301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97344af5-4d0b-50ac-8b3e-e8c06cd07b1f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186239364} :answer-field/id #uuid "b72163d1-46c0-5c21-9c83-5ecf0b5030da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1da25f7d-182b-5d2d-b5c8-9d3f66bb445c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104703" :question/problem "To which of the following sets does the number $\\sqrt{121}$ belong?\n\n- $N$\n- ${Q}^{-}$\n- ${Z}^{-}$\n- $Z$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $\\sqrt{121}=11$ is a natural number, so it belongs to $N.$\n\nSince every natural number is also an integer number, $\\sqrt{121}$ also belongs to $Z.$ \n\nOn the other hand, the sets ${Z}^{-}$ and ${Q}^{-}$ only contain negative numbers. Therefore, $\\sqrt{121}$ does not belong to either of these sets. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186240922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dfa0d1d-6451-5387-be02-629f948a0f1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186238176} {:answer/id #uuid "11413563-1bf2-5444-8474-5e5cf301d8b3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186238195} {:answer/id #uuid "19f29985-57b5-5076-80ec-5cd7f22a238f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186238269} {:answer/id #uuid "97d4f97f-b63c-51b3-ad86-5cecad9eefc6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239370} {:answer/id #uuid "ebab29aa-9a93-58e7-aefd-391feb8f233c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186240129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebab29aa-9a93-58e7-aefd-391feb8f233c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186240129} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac216de7-0aa2-506b-ba09-2a8b5ef341b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "95a9cd65-803f-5d19-875e-716631b2f8a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104734" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}+t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $2$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}+t,{t}^{2}-t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,+\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,(-1))\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,+\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,+\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}+t,{t}^{2}-1⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{-1}\\,+\\,({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,+\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,+\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,+\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Let's check if ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-t,{t}^{2}-1⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, $S$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186240923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d3d4890-6f13-5dde-8095-3e3fced3ec6e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186238867} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7da710-4947-5407-a417-4e130b1e610b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186239007} {:answer/id #uuid "771614d9-5d43-5f3d-ad3a-5323c7edc76b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186239081} {:answer/id #uuid "aa902358-41c8-51c0-b7ed-68bafa845a0e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186239547} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a18bf8-4f0e-5c76-ac9a-9b37b5796acf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3a18bf8-4f0e-5c76-ac9a-9b37b5796acf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239913} :answer-field/id #uuid "873915d1-3cba-59f7-b3bd-b2e37c5ee6c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d2cd958-2383-51a8-8ef7-f3c1ff9a5b43" :question/math-academy-id "q-104736" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}-4,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{2}-2t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $2$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-4,{t}^{2}-t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2(2)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-t,{t}^{2}-2t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,2(0))\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,2(1))\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}-2(2)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-4,{t}^{2}-2t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,2(0))\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,2(1))\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2(2)) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186240931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172031 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6ab324-e5d5-54bc-81f0-75fea5bb330d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186238311} {:answer/id #uuid "3a08ac4a-4091-58c6-92af-1125cbea741c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186238559} {:answer/id #uuid "3acac4f5-4282-5572-8280-d6cef3c271a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186238565} {:answer/id #uuid "a162af6d-c1e0-55bb-8110-b150da1039b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186239459} {:answer/id #uuid "c80ddecd-e80b-5833-b146-c2c88d4a543a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186239817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a162af6d-c1e0-55bb-8110-b150da1039b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186239459} :answer-field/id #uuid "c284c296-c6a6-52f7-9741-e3dc22502ff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f571336-e197-53b2-942f-7691710c5fd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104778" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain ${0,1}?$\n\n- $p:{0,1}\\to Z$\n- $q:[0,1]\\to {0,1}$\n- $r:{0,1}\\to (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is ${0,1},$ the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $1$ only.\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $(0,1),$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (excluding both $0$ and $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186240932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172031 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "444c9197-bf9a-595e-9928-a00d60bf51ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186238646} {:answer/id #uuid "48ab53f8-8b1f-598c-b301-1fb5d97fd076" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186238690} {:answer/id #uuid "98c80c08-b713-5407-b264-97db66af82b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186239381} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f96900-f753-561b-97e8-5b406d478b53" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186239939} {:answer/id #uuid "daa8044c-f7da-5f1d-b8f2-e0a8751f5b15" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186239962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7f96900-f753-561b-97e8-5b406d478b53" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186239939} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae58ed1-7247-5583-af27-e33f0f49a422" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00f5a8c6-457a-5b26-87c0-0c588245cf9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104780" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the domain $Z?$\n\n- $p:Z\\to [0,3]$\n- $q:(0,7)\\to Z$\n- $r:{Z}^{+}\\to Z$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $(0,7),$ the interval from $0$ to $7$ (excluding $0$ and $7$).\n\n- The domain of $r$ is ${Z}^{+},$ the set of positive integers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186240933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172031 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16e11ce7-772d-5167-aacc-c50653d1da8b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186238252} {:answer/id #uuid "669e1527-0fe0-5c06-b32f-423993073528" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186238950} {:answer/id #uuid "8f15fd0f-7424-5e92-b313-a15ec5b93ffa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239301} {:answer/id #uuid "af228750-6768-51c1-8675-12a42677d2e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 1759218