orname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/e7/e79856c97ab7e4dd27bb1f476fd911c2e7d77aac6ab54fe50597b3aafbca720c.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. This function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III are equals $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186265783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cefff60-8b5a-5e6b-9e52-f35453d404f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263940} {:answer/id #uuid "72297846-23e3-55fb-b49d-2016ee961011" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id 17592186263988} {:answer/id #uuid "b17137ba-a39f-5351-bf5d-ac9b76a0c325" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264527} {:answer/id #uuid "c573ea7b-2439-51c4-b299-30120bf6c0a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264732} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b5de7f-f5db-5245-8a64-60266e3ac04d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186265124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1b5de7f-f5db-5245-8a64-60266e3ac04d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186265124} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ed46d99-55db-5652-860e-3ffa744aad41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2768639-7882-5bba-9579-987ae66a0169" :question/math-academy-id "q-129904" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f8cb975b622bfe420d9418587293e83de60c297019f15e1b379bc118e079d35d.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/89/8944afb608465e49c7ae253f8e2518aa3d43b1c6e121dd91178ca1a67116a508.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function II equals $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186265784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e444d65-5846-5cad-b906-cede3d21aa27" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263551} {:answer/id #uuid "3f38dc4b-ed3f-53b5-adc5-7e439f122828" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186263558} {:answer/id #uuid "69fbf7ed-f266-519a-8e22-cdaa037e0e25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263911} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fb4f0b-6912-552e-ad9b-d967cd25a3d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264409} {:answer/id #uuid "c34bec27-8fd0-5c37-9161-51e57a5f1591" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4fb4f0b-6912-552e-ad9b-d967cd25a3d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264409} :answer-field/id #uuid "c31cbdf0-ff7e-51ed-9f95-e439f2b3d9f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36b8ecd0-d692-515d-8583-af8deca52b58" :question/math-academy-id "q-129906" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b1663fc54591