riable III follows a geometric distribution. Randomly picking out a crayon is a binomial experiment, and the number of crayons picked out before a blue crayon is picked can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186265919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06c1ce9a-bad7-50d6-b6c7-a1423ccce4bb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Geometric Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8a11de-70be-5512-8443-ad2858c3551f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186263109} {:answer/id #uuid "84e2cadf-c3a1-5405-a5ad-4f0ad653da36" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264140} {:answer/id #uuid "88d3d19f-b980-5af5-b99d-82bbf489dccc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264181} {:answer/id #uuid "ac30c812-3f6c-5d8e-af34-6f21589a82d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264478} {:answer/id #uuid "da75ab2f-d4e2-5f64-9922-f5aba86ae19b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84e2cadf-c3a1-5405-a5ad-4f0ad653da36" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264140} :answer-field/id #uuid "90f750e7-0c9c-5279-a364-e81c0c3d003f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b122ef40-830b-573f-ad8c-2fa029e286ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-130490" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of people examined at a clinic during a day\n- The average age of the people infected with a virus in a certain city\n- The number of people examined at a clinic until a person infected with a certain virus is found" :question/worked-solution "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Being examined is a binomial experiment, but the number of people examined cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. The average age of the people infected with a virus in a certain city is not a binomial experiment.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Being examined is a binomial experiment, and the number of people examined until a person infected with a certain virus is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186265953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73d976dd-42f3-555d-91ce-795583288ab5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264007} {:answer/id #uuid "880ba593-982d-5113-a46e-02bdba462868" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186264174} {:answer/id #uuid "8e12edfb-a68f-5909-b7a3-62d16d2d70ac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264215} {:answer/id #uuid "b5187789-1746-5985-b1f6-bcfcb53de5bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264562} {:answer/id #uuid "eeff8d8c-29a5-5ba4-91bc-8018157e4e32" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186265093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73d976dd-42f3-555d-91ce-795583288ab5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264007} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a2bf95a-dd4d-50fa-b6bb-2d7d80ce98a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "114cf182-dccf-5764-a411-6c6c3328247d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130670" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $2{y}^{″}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){y}^{′}-y=1$\n- ${y}^{″}+y{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=0$\n- $x{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){y}^{′}-y=1\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=2,b(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,c(x)=-1,$ and $f(x)=1.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{′}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=x,b(x)=0,c(x)={x}^{2},$ and $f(x)={e}^{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186265954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12282991-5937-510a-bdff-d7ac30128549" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263171} {:answer/id #uuid "2c0b7305-0b03-55d9-991e-9cfb5713d20f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263372} {:answer/id #uuid "8adeebb0-5776-506d-9b68-6d8789e3d696" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264189} {:answer/id #uuid "93043e6d-2ca1-5a86-84cc-182840f36d10" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264259} {:answer/id #uuid "e27a4fd6-d94b-508a-9d8f-4bb9ea87b4c8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8adeebb0-5776-506d-9b68-6d8789e3d696" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264189} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf3b59bb-2d4a-5ac7-a84e-d4a1444aae7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7e46e30-10b0-546e-b7af-e3d72616cbd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-130671" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=2$\n- ${y}^{3}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+xy{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=2\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,b(x)=-x,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)=2.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-xy=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term ${y}^{3}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+xy{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $xy{y}^{′}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186265955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72a41b4d-d78b-5859-b216-08e76cdc9e5c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263993} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcea926-a8b9-513b-906e-8d02930cfdb9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264212} {:answer/id #uuid "9100e9b9-5db5-51d7-aec7-c3e6d66f1b1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264245} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc056f3-9c03-55f1-8037-6a0aa15d75d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264924} {:answer/id #uuid "ff09c2f9-a455-50f3-9f76-c7798dac150a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186265242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff09c2f9-a455-50f3-9f76-c7798dac150a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186265242} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b7d4941-b9ea-5f9d-8d62-cdf635c06c45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e471b0b-273f-5999-b584-35af7a25a9a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-130675" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- ${e}^{2y}{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+3=0$\n- ${e}^{x}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2$\n- ${y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+5y=x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{e}^{2y}{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+3=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term ${e}^{2y}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)={e}^{x},b(x)=4,c(x)=0,$ and $f(x)=2+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+5y=x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=-2x,c(x)=5,$ and $f(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186265956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54dd7239-8155-550f-b323-3bc657f00d81" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263730} {:answer/id #uuid "b5edd95b-6555-529a-9906-5c7341743b02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264577} {:answer/id #uuid "bcaf4159-003f-5da3-92a9-ac16b66e20c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186264635} {:answer/id #uuid "be92b258-502c-5535-b3e6-22f15c3799c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264655} {:answer/id #uuid "c8984020-b182-5104-bb63-ecaf05b62a48" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be92b258-502c-5535-b3e6-22f15c3799c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264655} :answer-field/id #uuid "c97171ab-7660-54fa-a8fd-2c48ead3823d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66d63369-22c5-5a2f-adb6-cc0d49c2dc04" :question/math-academy-id "q-130677" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,{y}^{′}+{y}^{2}=0$\n- $2{y}^{″}+{x}^{3}{y}^{′}+2xy=0$\n- ${y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}=x{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,{y}^{′}+{y}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term ${y}^{2}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}+{x}^{3}{y}^{′}+2xy=0\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=2,b(x)={x}^{3},c(x)=2x,$ and $f(x)=0.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}=x{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=x,c(x)=0,$ and $f(x)=x{e}^{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186265973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "777afc62-08b0-5aa7-b0ac-c7b9d427d228" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186264038} {:answer/id #uuid "9be2e810-8183-552f-8417-2844d48d9d79" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186264333} {:answer/id #uuid "adf43be7-864c-5837-875b-a182e7de738e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186264495} {:answer/id #uuid "b263a001-df89-53c0-9fa5-978e3c21d771" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186264534} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c7ce0d-eb3c-5340-8250-9dfc0b9bb40c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186264844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3c7ce0d-eb3c-5340-8250-9dfc0b9bb40c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186264844} :answer-field/id #uuid "c91dd783-8508-53eb-87a5-410eec6ed547" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ec5e472-dc1d-5891-bb93-866a6d9b3914" :question/math-academy-id "q-130774" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are elementary matrices."}] [{:db/id 17592186265998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f42791-6076-59d7-9616-c1c6e6600e9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186263347} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0171e5-e789-542f-a5f7-eba1924afa10" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186263921} {:answer/id #uuid "83c09255-43ef-5f68-9b54-ce7b76d1d6f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186264131} {:answer/id #uuid "f1261446-d7b5-56d0-983f-d6cc1794f353" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186265118} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a9befa-627b-5dd7-9453-949d4370abbf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186265168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b0171e5-e789-542f-a5f7-eba1924afa10" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186263921} :answer-field/id #uuid "0743c94a-34a3-5797-8003-0db16035e4b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4fc937e-2b7d-5514-a470-2dba13ab48f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-130868" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I,\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186266032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01eb852c-2f90-54fc-91a8-50d23d4e99cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263016} {:answer/id #uuid "09c478fe-d049-5505-8c62-1e6fc24ec2f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186263091} {:answer/id #uuid "55e7be22-7937-575b-a70a-bffae28c9cea" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186263739} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5e9c24-1ee4-508a-a3f8-9111e975a5da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264616} {:answer/id #uuid "cb93fcde-2180-51a4-821c-2f16f62275f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09c478fe-d049-5505-8c62-1e6fc24ec2f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186263091} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcd4002a-bb35-5edb-b511-94f5ee632e69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e2ad896-b5d8-56df-9f2a-960d9cc29ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130997" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+y=x,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1$\n- ${y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=xy,\\,y(0)=3,\\,y(1)=4$\n- $2{y}^{″}+{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is combined with two conditions, but both conditions are given at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$). This is an **initial value problem.**\n\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=1$).\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$)."}] [{:db/id 17592186266033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b5d9d00-e02f-5196-aff6-b455c74a0649" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263529} {:answer/id #uuid "4d0078ea-39e6-5fb1-8d23-c2e31c0310e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263669} {:answer/id #uuid "b6625b93-5b45-5e19-89ef-a8937cd81409" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264580} {:answer/id #uuid "dc58afa9-80fb-51e6-8988-59936e54e1fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264920} {:answer/id #uuid "fa18aa04-6e7a-57a8-9547-52a4a9c468f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186265189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc58afa9-80fb-51e6-8988-59936e54e1fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264920} :answer-field/id #uuid "a256bf9f-782a-57a0-b444-9f9f53ab16da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d793473-d655-59e4-94f6-f213bc81d597" :question/math-academy-id "q-131001" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+y=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=1,\\,y(\\pi )=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=x{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=\\pi $).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified."}] [{:db/id 17592186266034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f65d86-e7d1-534d-88d7-27dad4c24b9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186263353} {:answer/id #uuid "68b2ae9d-2e7c-56b3-9fc6-2148193aab8c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263902} {:answer/id #uuid "9053a2f4-b132-5a1a-9027-55af380cda2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186264239} {:answer/id #uuid "d6999677-4fc3-553f-9115-dbd8d33e8e02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264865} {:answer/id #uuid "fe048cfa-d6eb-50a2-aa53-5e3fef46e01d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186265235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6999677-4fc3-553f-9115-dbd8d33e8e02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264865} :answer-field/id #uuid "21bbdc5c-849c-50cf-acda-2723d0192fa8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bddcd08-8ec0-5aef-80c9-79dc52612a17" :question/math-academy-id "q-131013" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{4x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-3x}}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{4x}+{e}^{-3x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-3x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{4x}+{e}^{-3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}={e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186266035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d6ab156-7a37-591c-a19d-c6c06a8780b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263671} {:answer/id #uuid "5a746180-9978-5123-ae04-f8c2e2ca1b20" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id 17592186263779} {:answer/id #uuid "96882b31-2609-5a6b-86ab-37d6904bfa99" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186264287} {:answer/id #uuid "d9987f0b-ade7-55b4-9350-d99f8bdb0bdb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264898} {:answer/id #uuid "e9de53c4-ef7a-5024-8034-fd865bba92ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186265036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d6ab156-7a37-591c-a19d-c6c06a8780b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263671} :answer-field/id #uuid "cef675b4-cf96-5891-9d9d-3f64164679ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df1f63a2-8822-5dfd-aa6c-f1c9c0adcac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-131014" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{6}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{6}={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $4x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})={x}^{2}+{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186266036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06954260-fde4-54d3-ae49-191ceddb7742" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263060} {:answer/id #uuid "31fadc19-619a-572c-81ee-d73567995879" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263428} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f55a4c-b1f3-5d05-884b-ecf33d32bc2c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264451} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5bdec9-b10f-52a2-8dac-4da3bda9e156" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186264780} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a4a956-6130-5dac-9ef2-6fb55ad730d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186265111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0a4a956-6130-5dac-9ef2-6fb55ad730d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186265111} :answer-field/id #uuid "96ecc9c1-dc49-5f9a-b22f-054bcce060af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a36da13-eb76-5bc5-81fc-b75234c7173d" :question/math-academy-id "q-131016" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ are solutions to the equation $4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$\n- $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186266037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f2ff38-391f-5736-81a8-93ead73b870e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186263035} {:answer/id #uuid "0ccc59d0-926c-5caf-9e66-e9ff007945fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186263121} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb56906-a29a-5021-ba41-e5d2dafe8ca7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186263531} {:answer/id #uuid "590297e8-5fa4-5cba-9644-b98912267daf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263766} {:answer/id #uuid "d54e5a0b-a535-50ae-b5b9-ad1946d6ff2b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186264858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "590297e8-5fa4-5cba-9644-b98912267daf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263766} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c3e708d-a7b2-52a1-989a-77afd50adc8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fca8233-9802-5cda-93e4-fba4e6ea4800" :question/math-academy-id "q-131019" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=x$\n- $y(x)=x{e}^{2x}$ $$\n- $y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x=\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x{e}^{2x}=x{e}^{x}({e}^{x})$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.  The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}={e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186266038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "020db8db-6c51-5099-8bcc-a50e3dd281b3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263017} {:answer/id #uuid "33a96736-39f3-5fd1-9a4b-729c84727efb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186263450} {:answer/id #uuid "53972a96-f9f7-5a3e-9024-16c0ac2903b3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186263721} {:answer/id #uuid "b41d9f5c-5baf-579a-80e1-6441ecf6bc83" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186264547} {:answer/id #uuid "f200711c-f7cf-5f02-b325-7edbb0bba667" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186265128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "020db8db-6c51-5099-8bcc-a50e3dd281b3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263017} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b471435-8e23-5e55-9076-3e8f727af21a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d36ff0c1-6dbb-5187-9838-81e30505ca16" :question/math-academy-id "q-131020" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-3.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186266039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14558f68-0db1-5d15-8ebf-35ef28f19739" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186263190} {:answer/id #uuid "7c715433-deb6-564e-9ca1-9b7057ec2364" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186264074} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e13f9f-dfa5-5c0c-b92a-42996d02756b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264557} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5b444b-6491-53ac-9205-939da1431819" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264769} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e4e12c-7621-54b8-a0c0-dca584d8d174" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2e4e12c-7621-54b8-a0c0-dca584d8d174" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264970} :answer-field/id #uuid "30f12629-88e3-509d-a1d4-9a39c45496df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfa49172-fd9a-5f39-9f7a-58af40e909ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-131022" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{4}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-7x{y}^{′}+16y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotient of a known solution and a non-constant function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186266041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02521fb7-e77f-5486-b7ad-fe251be00536" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186263019} {:answer/id #uuid "1accd78f-f342-5106-931f-184349aefe64" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263252} {:answer/id #uuid "54ea6314-c9cb-5d15-971a-ac5209134277" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186263731} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5a706b-3993-5215-95c1-22e1bc77abc6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264490} {:answer/id #uuid "c53da94c-2020-58b9-920d-a83cb5f0773f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1accd78f-f342-5106-931f-184349aefe64" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263252} :answer-field/id #uuid "e16d37ef-1010-5aff-9f3e-ab4a5490ac12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55d7974d-e6ac-57d3-85a4-a96c054848c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-131036" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}+4{e}^{-x}$\n- $y(x)=5{e}^{-x}$\n- $y(x)=({e}^{-x}+1){e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{e}^{x}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}+4{e}^{-x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=1,{c}_{2}=4.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=5{e}^{-x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=0,{c}_{2}=5.$\n\n- The function $({e}^{-x}+1){e}^{x}=1+{e}^{x}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because $1$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id 17592186266042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ecce21-550b-523f-b8b9-aeef7fbf3bae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263605} {:answer/id #uuid "72477dd1-b33d-54df-b872-6f36021971cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263990} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5449cc-5239-52bf-9b1a-7218973f90ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186264184} {:answer/id #uuid "a810da3d-3505-5c14-82f7-8bf490141215" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264436} {:answer/id #uuid "ed55bf29-39a9-50eb-89ad-464d2079ee83" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186265073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a810da3d-3505-5c14-82f7-8bf490141215" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264436} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ca2a258-074d-5301-bd96-9a6250fb613b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d91387a3-da34-5d28-af8d-36338cffabf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-131066" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$\n\nIII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}2=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186266043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dc5b71b-d30a-5b69-a449-075c1ccbb784" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263385} {:answer/id #uuid "2e02b866-9caa-52cb-a16b-e6a447da2557" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263388} {:answer/id #uuid "8ede9ec6-3ec3-5679-841d-beda689a8d23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264224} {:answer/id #uuid "f7043937-bfc3-5694-b811-4b941c7f898b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186265162} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7235bc-ad8e-5154-bcc4-72af494ee4f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186265226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ede9ec6-3ec3-5679-841d-beda689a8d23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264224} :answer-field/id #uuid "61c5378d-c503-53af-b5e2-84496db751eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f0ecd5f3-9f3c-5ba8-8557-dd384f7804ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-131068" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[2,4]$\n- $[0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2x)=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[2,4].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,4].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[0,1)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186266044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16553c75-97b9-5cc8-81cd-3d07f900fc00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186263210} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5fe824-7d6b-5aa2-aa5d-beac070bd9e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263649} {:answer/id #uuid "7e790ed3-a5e4-5dfd-8870-6de6fb7bee58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264094} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e730cd-e4b4-52e4-bfa9-c77c1b371ad7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264407} {:answer/id #uuid "cda62d67-0b6b-5216-b9f4-2c7044f11d57" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b5fe824-7d6b-5aa2-aa5d-beac070bd9e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263649} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e20df67-cc9c-5126-8317-2ad940ca7006" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f88ffa9-46f8-5b9e-b471-a9dfa9a9c40b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131069" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{8}^{x}-61, & x<2 \\\\ {3}^{x-1}, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[3,5]$\n- $[1,3]$\n- $(-1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[3,5].$ In this interval, $f(x)={3}^{x-1},$ so the function is continuous on the interval $[3,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider $[1,3].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}({8}^{x}-61)={8}^{2}-61=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}{3}^{x-1}={3}^{2-1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=2$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[1,3],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186266059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2153fc-188e-5366-8dee-c288973c3627" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263107} {:answer/id #uuid "7613b3a2-396c-52be-b4e4-67bfb380cef8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186264027} {:answer/id #uuid "7e736d25-50b7-56d5-b5fc-c446d75dc518" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264093} {:answer/id #uuid "883a536c-ef7a-5bb0-995a-86a1be6b3a05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186264176} {:answer/id #uuid "98c4bf7b-c364-5417-b7a7-752586e4d096" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98c4bf7b-c364-5417-b7a7-752586e4d096" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264314} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a99a5c-b105-5dbf-a39b-ebd80571b461" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d989e0b-f261-53e6-891f-e36529fecf99" :question/math-academy-id "q-131180" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}, & -1\\le x<1 \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $(-1,1)$\n- $[-2,0]$\n- $[0,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[-2,0].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}(-1)=-1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}({x}^{3})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=-1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-2,0],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}({x}^{3})=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186266090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea60336-8c85-583a-988b-0feeaac800be" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186263131} {:answer/id #uuid "664c5194-4fe7-54dd-9515-7ff5dc6c9e3c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186263885} {:answer/id #uuid "72f48748-f04a-51e4-be35-bdc0cf605e40" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186263996} {:answer/id #uuid "771fba46-a454-50bf-b09e-58c3ea4313d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186264034} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b65499-ea66-5ec7-86ee-f720b26ce4c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186264952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "771fba46-a454-50bf-b09e-58c3ea4313d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186264034} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b881fd1-1130-5738-b1c0-9446892f3811" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "675c49ee-f80c-5132-87b4-3f13525060ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-131460" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $C$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186268926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a8df045-f6bd-568e-87e8-8e1f2a4ff801" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186266367} {:answer/id #uuid "31ca1f55-b38d-57f6-b27b-6af7eb864f73" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186266583} {:answer/id #uuid "84209361-266f-56a2-90fa-973bc7afcdb7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186267307} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad26291-e462-58d0-a659-c844150d9ad4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186267385} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a59cf5-a6cf-570e-a6fd-760929f3b02f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186267767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5a59cf5-a6cf-570e-a6fd-760929f3b02f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186267767} :answer-field/id #uuid "a974d11a-8dca-5ec8-b4db-241f08aa75bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53d0afb1-1358-5254-8618-617a2b1cbc7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-133250" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=8xy-5{x}^{3}$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=6x{y}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2})=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy)=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y)=8y-15{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy-5{x}^{3})=8y-15{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=18{x}^{2}y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{3})=6{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186268927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0696b464-e7e1-5e14-ad3f-58af1c679e0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186266200} {:answer/id #uuid "6e647995-93cf-50e2-a8d6-4a1bc5d37b90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186267112} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cef443-4a55-511f-bf84-9c27d22cf307" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186267944} {:answer/id #uuid "cd003b1d-8434-5f8d-84c9-311daa61d020" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186267978} {:answer/id #uuid "ff008aef-110d-534c-9bc4-341ba11d079b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186268389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd003b1d-8434-5f8d-84c9-311daa61d020" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186267978} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bb79740-02b4-52ed-8451-4a3fd7335d7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27d730e5-f1a8-51a8-a220-f1ae1c972c84" :question/math-academy-id "q-133331" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{x}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x{e}^{y+1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y+1}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=7x-3y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3x-7y$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7x-3y)=-3 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-7y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186268966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35453324-c2ff-5be4-86aa-70b3085154dc" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Information From a Scatter Plot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26880641-9ae3-5a00-99f6-a099c745e038" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186266487} {:answer/id #uuid "46eac317-64e2-574a-b499-cf8287dee720" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186266775} {:answer/id #uuid "637c5ed4-425b-5f85-8a84-cfddccb3694d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186267019} {:answer/id #uuid "977b1c07-80bf-5377-bb10-82d2c20d6640" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186267500} {:answer/id #uuid "bee40643-591e-5eaf-be5c-334334782d7f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186267854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46eac317-64e2-574a-b499-cf8287dee720" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186266775} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d38f5b2-079a-5d94-a360-7098c459e98b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b463b24-e0d7-56c4-b957-08fba1ac548b" :question/math-academy-id "q-134105" :question/problem "![](images/57/5768d8f90d3dd754471a69d25582eca6cdb2f4993b9b22020dc9f2038aa61954.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above summarizes data collected by a Midwestern high school showing the relationship between the number of absences in a month and the class size. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Only one class reported a total of $6$ absences in the month.\n- Two classes have a total of $20$ students each.\n- Only one class has more than $24$ students." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is false. The scatter plot has two points whose vertical coordinate is equal to $6,$ namely, $(16,6)$ and $(20,6).$ This means that two of the classes each reported a total of 6 absences in the month.\n\n![](images/a6/a62b1d5a63764b38013fdbdba9ba85a4495cd996a2d9f975dbe3d7eb89166234.png)\n\n  \n\n- Statement II is false. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $20,$ namely, $(20,6).$ This means that only one of the classes has a total of  $20$ students.\n\n![](images/3c/3c41f2e111ef141511ec4f2faa655ddcea8f2b7e0b73bd37f0b1e142d5d60f14.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. From the scatter plot, only one class has more than $24$ students. This class is represented by the point to the right of the vertical line.\n\n![](images/ad/adcba37370fdac69b152a8d18ef710d5f665d9a3252376ea312808be7d08a407.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186268967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35453324-c2ff-5be4-86aa-70b3085154dc" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Information From a Scatter Plot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21b2397a-916b-5684-89a4-5e08cbc05da7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186266445} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9513b0-cfab-5737-8ce2-8932d661ce06" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186267113} {:answer/id #uuid "7007b133-2108-5332-8018-e0c312d229fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186267127} {:answer/id #uuid "d167dcdb-5c5d-5b55-a666-dca5ed26aaf9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186268016} {:answer/id #uuid "dda59fc0-1396-5a55-b015-9ad4fa059b08" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186268100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d167dcdb-5c5d-5b55-a666-dca5ed26aaf9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186268016} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d62ffc9-5895-5694-9250-e2aaaf98f4ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a188568f-e246-50ee-bc75-14d95b670306" :question/math-academy-id "q-134128" :question/problem "![](images/69/69517e814dae28db96c64a662ce97b5fc40ea10355cb5a02561a0d12e1a3f845.png)\n\nA math teacher asked his students how many hours of sleep they had the\nnight before a test. The information he received is summarized in the scatter plot above.\n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Only one student obtained a score of $90.$\n- Only one student slept for $6$ hours.\n- None of the students obtained a score lower than $75.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $90,$ namely, $(7,90).$ This means that only one student obtained a score of $90.$\n\n![](images/ef/ef915015fa9c7685f45f26ee9cd2c10dfce5a4253aed22b6ea2ea57f0c3c26e2.png)\n\n    \n\n- Statement II is false. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $6,$ namely, $(6,80)$ and $(6,85).$ This means that two students had  $6$ hours of sleep.\n\n![](images/31/3139ca71a780b76ca7355ddd119d6502f1cd390d67064db3a4a57801e33f2917.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, three students obtained a score lower than  $75.$  Those students are represented by the three dots below the horizontal line.\n\n![](images/a6/a675a94a9183a86d221bb2199dae38f9f55b9f91dff9868320b0b8f4d38b3fe9.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186268968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35453324-c2ff-5be4-86aa-70b3085154dc" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Information From a Scatter Plot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a4a7e57-06f9-5425-b741-63c935927fec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186266512} {:answer/id #uuid "3f095521-ad57-5a78-bdb3-c7e15eabcd17" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186266710} {:answer/id #uuid "59f79973-c36f-52d5-b088-9dbce824e96d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186266943} {:answer/id #uuid "911d53e2-3292-54a7-8c94-5464e1f37461" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186267435} {:answer/id #uuid "9148833c-d117-52e6-bbc8-856489ace019" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186267436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a4a7e57-06f9-5425-b741-63c935927fec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186266512} :answer-field/id #uuid "28e93047-1b86-5999-909f-db1c9471a91d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26f51f13-3f23-54c9-b7ba-52010958ad0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-134139" :question/problem "![](images/37/376bd65db6b1d0a91c6013462e686ffad4d8acceee4948e61cf291d6403a12cd.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above is based on data collected from several homes in a given community. It shows the relationship between the age of a house and the number of people living in it. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- There is only one house where precisely $3$ people live.\n- Two of the houses are $16$ years old.\n- Only one house is older than $24$ years old." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $3,$ namely, $(18,3).$ This means that there is only one house where $3$ people live.\n\n![](images/7a/7a56236f0c5649a64436809b2a5e1fb8f00ed3d345cd81c571f96d579d6c19b6.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement II is false. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $16,$ namely, $(16,6).$ This means that only one house is $16$ years old.\n\n![](images/6d/6d9a60cd10c6d7cc9f9d51d0be44c32cb688e19da988b3e84b30f0c4be32a2a8.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, two houses are more than $24$ years old, represented by the two dots to the right of the vertical line.\n\n![](images/44/44b7395bb2e5ee0a71899869e6caedb949e8744adde314134f6a09e4c8f8a35d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186269024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336927bb-f78f-50d8-8fe2-982859025c83" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186266594} {:answer/id #uuid "43599c17-db18-5f59-943b-f6694b64536e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186266746} {:answer/id #uuid "87491b44-a44a-5b51-a101-c994b4dd87c8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186267340} {:answer/id #uuid "abe5b2d8-e67f-5b94-b699-664e59eb79c2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186267697} {:answer/id #uuid "ea568682-c450-5973-a372-84ea8bcbfbcf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186268234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abe5b2d8-e67f-5b94-b699-664e59eb79c2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186267697} :answer-field/id #uuid "78c806ba-7f98-5519-bb9b-eacf4165ea18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c393953b-f9cd-55bd-bdae-3a3a7283cff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-135306" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.85\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.85\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.4}{7}+\\frac{0.6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{0.6}{7}+\\frac{3.4}{7}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186269025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d94ca1-83de-5255-a442-d605b33d0335" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186266196} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef8a373-66ea-59d5-8721-8e644338d5ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186266561} {:answer/id #uuid "7adcd171-00c5-558b-9120-e22896e2b96c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186267227} {:answer/id #uuid "bda1712b-2eb0-5c41-8699-4465a948ee1d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186267844} {:answer/id #uuid "efe0b863-81e4-5b74-bf72-465eb29529eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186268278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05d94ca1-83de-5255-a442-d605b33d0335" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186266196} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bedf448-8bec-50c9-b4fb-6d6e26a6b8d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56012d7d-0dc5-5803-80a1-167efecb090a" :question/math-academy-id "q-135307" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.02+0.24 \\\\ 0.18+0.56\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.26 \\\\ 0.74\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186269026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0215741d-6fdb-50ce-9c18-9f5e7cbb8815" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186266162} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4ca1fe-5d82-59fb-89d6-a97b689701e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186267998} {:answer/id #uuid "db50f611-eda9-5fa8-869e-928079078a98" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186268085} {:answer/id #uuid "f02b0d56-0c59-52dd-b1be-ed071f9703e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186268280} {:answer/id #uuid "f1925279-27df-52d1-b85d-ec17b6bb9785" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id 17592186268292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0215741d-6fdb-50ce-9c18-9f5e7cbb8815" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186266162} :answer-field/id #uuid "838c5850-6740-5375-96a2-f0b5cb71573e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0393af0a-1987-5803-9300-833b177eed7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-135310" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 & 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 & 0.3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1.2}{9}+\\frac{0.5}{9} \\\\ \\frac{0.6}{9}+\\frac{1.2}{9}+\\frac{0.2}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2.4}{9}+\\frac{1.6}{9}+\\frac{0.3}{9}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4.3}{9}\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186269027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e0b9844-73f6-5e23-8163-b914b076bb38" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id 17592186266554} {:answer/id #uuid "3c09471a-0067-5f14-90cd-cf33ee232ea5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186266670} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba8357a-f4a7-53ad-b9c2-6266ec35f846" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186267096} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1e0ab1-f58e-5fcc-94e3-b0b2ec3950bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186267985} {:answer/id #uuid "f71b468a-3aa2-52a6-b6a3-47a27498261d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186268333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ba8357a-f4a7-53ad-b9c2-6266ec35f846" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186267096} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b8e533f-21a1-5986-ae72-7ac63280f4f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05952a75-ee49-5c6e-985c-55f5554bd3f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-135311" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.15 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.7 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.15 & 0.05\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.15 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.7 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.15 & 0.05\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.03+0.105+0.12 \\\\ 0.06+0.49+0.07 \\\\ 0.01+0.105+0.01\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.255 \\\\ 0.62 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186269073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124614 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Stochastic Vectors and Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04dde5de-291d-5d7a-ab58-2b34e5fbdb96" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id 17592186266188} {:answer/id #uuid "08d57faf-8683-5457-926a-17017f7019d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id 17592186266225} {:answer/id #uuid "11eee0fb-37f6-56ae-ad49-4d70b6f72528" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186266292} {:answer/id #uuid "786a538d-7672-56a6-8d44-fe689a21bcdd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186267202} {:answer/id #uuid "a82e5b9b-1d4e-59df-8f41-441fa243cd0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id 17592186267667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a82e5b9b-1d4e-59df-8f41-441fa243cd0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id 17592186267667} :answer-field/id #uuid "99885d5c-06cc-5a79-9a5e-f4b785659c96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7cb13afc-7824-57e2-abcc-e880dfaafeec" :question/math-academy-id "q-135488" :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0 & 0.9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.9 & 0 & 0.1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $U$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its second column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0 & 0.9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.9 & 0 & 0.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $V$  is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $W$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are non-negative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.5+0.5=1 \\\\ 0.3+0.7=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186269221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3368d843-8603-5c4c-b4cb-25b74f31633e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186266593} {:answer/id #uuid "36ff86bd-1ede-55d5-a859-e58fb94f85e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186266617} {:answer/id #uuid "71805fe1-5e3d-5b09-ac37-2600f1102822" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186267141} {:answer/id #uuid "7202d3e8-c960-597b-a8c2-21c29a071989" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186267144} {:answer/id #uuid "ee06a8e1-51fc-53ed-bd87-fae6a1608ac5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186268261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36ff86bd-1ede-55d5-a859-e58fb94f85e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186266617} :answer-field/id #uuid "223d714a-e779-5321-ae85-c5fc8fa4e232" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30844de8-e636-5e8f-ba19-823c3f8b1cf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139455" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* negative semi-definite since all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ is positive).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186269223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02126ae9-f3b8-53eb-ad4f-409cfcff5fb0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186266161} {:answer/id #uuid "45ffec10-ec53-5719-87c3-efda8153a53c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186266769} {:answer/id #uuid "4d45cc36-3b81-57bd-a710-7ff416585a19" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186266828} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b38899-fa5e-5f1d-84c8-4f2ef25127d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186267653} {:answer/id #uuid "d485bcf4-5498-5b63-a595-81ab0e925eb3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186268042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45ffec10-ec53-5719-87c3-efda8153a53c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186266769} :answer-field/id #uuid "4177a06e-6006-5e5c-be24-81a0aba0b4f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80bdad61-ebf7-546e-ab4f-0e8486894231" :question/math-academy-id "q-139458" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive semi-definite? \n\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=10$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-negative.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* positive semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ is negative).\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186271977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b52156e-913a-5de2-a794-91999e64e3bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186269490} {:answer/id #uuid "398d644a-ad1c-5b35-ab05-7d0d212bd5ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186269792} {:answer/id #uuid "5f656659-28c8-5de3-ba39-06241a0c7984" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270114} {:answer/id #uuid "cbeae5f3-7e46-54d5-a50f-b08ce65d16f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271059} {:answer/id #uuid "f68a27ff-793d-5e4f-bd44-233ef37fb985" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186271431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b52156e-913a-5de2-a794-91999e64e3bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186269490} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c0c2c23-b649-5839-b71c-7052c63f3cd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a127128-cb22-56be-ba7d-5b1447ce81f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-139957" :question/problem "Suppose $a\\cdot (a+b)=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $a\\cdot a<0$\n- $b\\cdot a>0$\n- $b\\cdot a=5$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II and III are false. Simplifying the expression $a\\cdot (a+b)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (a+b) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $b\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186271990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4d184c8-0bd2-597e-9f52-d467a1379446" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270741} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf57269-cf34-5bc0-87a7-29587dde8040" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270933} {:answer/id #uuid "eba3d099-e4c6-5ebd-9f52-ee8c8c6e2d6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271339} {:answer/id #uuid "ff03a777-dcd9-5248-813a-477b910d2d6a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271498} {:answer/id #uuid "ff98c885-c7e6-5a89-827b-7c7ca0ba1eb1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186271503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcf57269-cf34-5bc0-87a7-29587dde8040" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270933} :answer-field/id #uuid "a04d5514-5b37-56fd-aa92-29d4ff83f34c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "09aa5cfb-0ccb-582a-bfa8-b6151a1b73a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-139979" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,3⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}kx+3y=1 \\\\ 7x-2y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nI. $-3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$\n\nII. $3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$\n\nIII. $|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=3|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$" :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ 3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186271991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31c4fdf7-c46a-530c-a71c-14c56651b492" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186269718} {:answer/id #uuid "3c485c53-cb4c-5476-8b1c-aa0cd5cd9666" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186269819} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa0f0ae-b6e2-53a5-9c84-de5353f70624" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270216} {:answer/id #uuid "bddefd59-382b-5e8e-ba2e-d6adc8315f59" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270944} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4d8ca4-c11a-5891-ad37-3bcc21d34a99" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6aa0f0ae-b6e2-53a5-9c84-de5353f70624" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270216} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b9d4a45-bd99-5ff1-8095-ab37bfea3c7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddc658b6-e0a7-590f-af72-c80671fc917f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139980" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,-2⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}15x+8y=-1 \\\\ kx+13y=-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\n- $|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=2|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=-4|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$\n- $-2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$" :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ -2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186272012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dc653f0-653a-51c8-9dde-134e1ea1131e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186269831} {:answer/id #uuid "63282b85-24f9-5737-88f6-e544d18bead1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186270146} {:answer/id #uuid "d283f88f-ed57-5c59-963f-f49777d28ae0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271122} {:answer/id #uuid "db49a675-ce1e-57fa-a849-aff9de416a49" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271188} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb23ab9-9422-51b8-8613-a343d4227bf0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186271348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecb23ab9-9422-51b8-8613-a343d4227bf0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186271348} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfceea30-a8b2-5f6c-aa6e-a6e0d02cdbfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f60fdb9d-cf89-59bd-ad65-e9ec096b82df" :question/math-academy-id "q-140049" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\n- $A$ is a triangular matrix\n- $A$ does not have identical rows\n- $A$ does not have proportional columns" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2=5\\ne 0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ is not a triangular matrix.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\nHence, if $|A|\\ne 0$ then $A$ doesn't have proportional nor identical rows/columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186272013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4277cba2-c049-5ea5-856e-2ff6513db5dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186269868} {:answer/id #uuid "72da4e3f-61a8-5e34-b36c-55d3e99b038d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270284} {:answer/id #uuid "9abb2b39-bec9-5be0-99bf-81a5af073417" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186270633} {:answer/id #uuid "9afef988-3992-5f75-bb57-47478f3265c2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186270637} {:answer/id #uuid "a39e8d77-7621-59cd-b046-86dcbd4ec894" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a39e8d77-7621-59cd-b046-86dcbd4ec894" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270727} :answer-field/id #uuid "f058dbfa-a132-5778-8502-105f66b5f7f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14e63fd3-62b7-575e-b489-4cda5c20ed53" :question/math-academy-id "q-140050" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns\n- $A$ has identical columns\n- $A$ has identical rows" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e440973-55fe-51fa-9da0-f4d52cf2e1f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269685} {:answer/id #uuid "6564e814-315d-5d94-906f-1b6ff52f2ea8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270172} {:answer/id #uuid "af1a5bdd-ecdf-550d-aa85-b91ee12261f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270825} {:answer/id #uuid "cc38f4d0-c9ec-5bea-9900-3e87852c1726" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186271062} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8a718e-676e-5690-ab51-91d91cda239d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af1a5bdd-ecdf-550d-aa85-b91ee12261f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270825} :answer-field/id #uuid "bab21a93-10c6-5a81-9fe7-a6f973ee6db2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1cac76f2-abeb-58d4-87e9-5f3619bedd4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140085" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $T$ is invertible\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dbab242-24fe-5c03-852d-2eb615913781" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270370} {:answer/id #uuid "89b74ed6-8548-5297-8f9f-660681b98c71" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186270460} {:answer/id #uuid "9de2dfb5-c1f9-5f6c-b906-4cc61c38d798" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270667} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a2cdd4-2029-5723-81cc-f1c11dbb08be" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186271006} {:answer/id #uuid "d0dffa0e-a0ed-5ee7-ae33-b94be7866f93" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89b74ed6-8548-5297-8f9f-660681b98c71" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186270460} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b675c94-5368-5806-8976-ba02b13c17a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0e37cf1-8e7e-52da-9921-f2cfa10fc56d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140434" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,0]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,0],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}$ and $g(t)=3{t}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{4}$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u={t}^{3}+t+1,$ $\\text{d}u=(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{4}]-[\\frac{1}{4}(-1)^{4}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "001c26e6-fb2a-5123-a23a-c6fe6c178c5d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id 17592186269258} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbc32ca-217a-5c44-ab30-9e62ad2048fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id 17592186269830} {:answer/id #uuid "5313ba94-5a47-5645-9e76-9f46ce8302ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id 17592186270006} {:answer/id #uuid "9d603cce-fb2b-52c8-bb52-08e08084f50e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U,$ $V$ and $W$" :db/id 17592186270662} {:answer/id #uuid "bcdc9516-9cc0-57a8-9a6c-f29d58f44264" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186270931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcdc9516-9cc0-57a8-9a6c-f29d58f44264" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186270931} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ce9eea1-f0fc-50cb-933d-e3ae585d23d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b412ef2-b86f-5688-ad69-1945a2870af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-140480" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=10-9-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=4-4+4=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=10-9-1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=0+3-3=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=0-3+3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186272106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43749ce2-e780-5676-bb45-6925677458ac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id 17592186269877} {:answer/id #uuid "74320017-1113-5e96-92ae-f49ff4ac56dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U,V,$ and $W$" :db/id 17592186270296} {:answer/id #uuid "b20691db-9c02-5118-962a-22f06eebd1c2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ and $V$ only" :db/id 17592186270862} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c50a9f-02a1-56cd-81ca-f7974fea67c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id 17592186271028} {:answer/id #uuid "fdca36a0-0082-5d07-b30e-0c3eaadd6d16" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id 17592186271491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdca36a0-0082-5d07-b30e-0c3eaadd6d16" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id 17592186271491} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5c4dc67-f948-53ac-801a-382cfa00851a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d09c8cc-56af-54e4-b26f-33ef3a4b7c45" :question/math-academy-id "q-140483" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}+0-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=-1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $V$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186272107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4176e5a5-f3e3-5b6e-9a01-e183549a0659" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id 17592186269860} {:answer/id #uuid "4761f8a5-2ece-54ae-8bf9-29d721b95fbb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186269916} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5f7f80-b91e-55cd-95cc-3b053fce1582" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186270485} {:answer/id #uuid "ed15f76f-cb2c-5ddf-9398-e0999218fa40" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id 17592186271352} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a61a68-1fe2-5984-b5fb-d97021d24541" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id 17592186271453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed15f76f-cb2c-5ddf-9398-e0999218fa40" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id 17592186271352} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ebc996f-c4f2-5d87-89ff-091ab407dc08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "762f53a4-6eba-533a-aefb-847dcb22ecb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140484" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{6}{13}-\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=4+9=13\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{4}{29}-\\frac{25}{29}=-1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186272112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2018f499-871d-52eb-9dcb-6eb74af91b57" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186269536} {:answer/id #uuid "28183480-cfb9-5dd9-820e-7245cf6418ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186269624} {:answer/id #uuid "988d29b3-3a9b-5bcf-a853-66f2587681a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186270613} {:answer/id #uuid "a0144298-79ed-5a85-956d-a14aa98d90c8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270691} {:answer/id #uuid "be2dc30f-2e10-5e59-921e-fc1967641251" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "988d29b3-3a9b-5bcf-a853-66f2587681a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186270613} :answer-field/id #uuid "89d564bb-a546-5fa5-a0f0-f3c6a4a67e09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0aa2e7ba-284c-52fb-a0ea-9a38be5541e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-140500" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(e+{e}^{-1})-{e}^{t}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(e+{e}^{-1})-(e-{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-1}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=-{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{-t}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-1}-e)-{e}^{-t}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-1}-e)-({e}^{-1}-e) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d7e32e1-d561-5693-adb9-5951da0681df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186270100} {:answer/id #uuid "6d40da93-f481-564b-96cd-9482db582939" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270237} {:answer/id #uuid "8332a5db-afd8-508f-a213-afa6bb52b52a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270403} {:answer/id #uuid "d1412055-1f36-50a9-869f-3b2d0276b8b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271112} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a6b8f1-4197-52b7-bdc4-c12a32c65321" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1412055-1f36-50a9-869f-3b2d0276b8b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271112} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2dd264c-2679-51b0-aa2f-f68ee4606671" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3d61daa-c943-5ecf-a98c-7f9bcd210491" :question/math-academy-id "q-140565" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrix $Q$ and the $n\\times n$ matrix $P?$\n\n- If $\\lambda $ is a real eigenvalue of $Q,$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n- If the determinant of $P$ is either $1$ or $-1,$ then $P$ is orthogonal\n- $Q-I$ is singular" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $\\lambda $ is an eigenvalue of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n\n- Statement II is false. Every orthogonal matrix has the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$, but not every matrix with the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$ is orthogonal. As a counterexample, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe determinant of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P)=2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n however, $P$ is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the following orthogonal matrix:\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nQ-I=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand $Q-I$ is not singular since, for example, it doesn't have proportional rows or columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a8f5c37-787f-5301-988e-881177dba05b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186269942} {:answer/id #uuid "70fd7dd5-e9f9-5e84-8182-d272b1a83d5a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270272} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ec7d76-5885-5730-82c1-783eb5e8767a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270775} {:answer/id #uuid "a90a1400-8cf7-5327-84a6-b23eeef373c5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270776} {:answer/id #uuid "bd969ae2-808e-586d-99a0-ec6d4ec6bd79" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70fd7dd5-e9f9-5e84-8182-d272b1a83d5a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270272} :answer-field/id #uuid "161805ab-30d2-5ed4-b289-e0037d8b336b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27c4d64e-3a6c-52f2-bb70-03acd0cf6bbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140834" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\n- $\\lambda (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)=0$\n- $2{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4=0$\n- $5{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33cf5e4e-0cbf-5768-9b98-a3e3b69116cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id 17592186269742} {:answer/id #uuid "4d383d45-4c02-5376-a1d2-94606118fb79" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id 17592186269964} {:answer/id #uuid "7caeac7e-597d-5341-8a6b-261132fa12f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id 17592186270361} {:answer/id #uuid "c05018a0-89fa-5bed-b3a1-8ebd55b0f67b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $S$ only" :db/id 17592186270972} {:answer/id #uuid "ed384a5b-fa80-587a-85ae-ce6d0680f465" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id 17592186271353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d383d45-4c02-5376-a1d2-94606118fb79" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id 17592186269964} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d37585f-1e97-5d22-a32b-6ec88d1ca95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d68a509c-d790-5483-ab3b-63aef29c3b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-140870" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $3\\times 3$).\n\n- $S$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $S$ is not symmetric since ${s}_{23}\\ne {s}_{32}.$\n\n- $T$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Three-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ffeb00-a92e-56bf-93a8-fa4ad00e1bbb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186269295} {:answer/id #uuid "0dfe8132-1976-54f4-a5ba-6797e3443c0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269390} {:answer/id #uuid "27430119-3953-5959-8c3c-19cd82247a39" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186269614} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea27a89-75a9-59bf-a4c5-de0126835372" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269844} {:answer/id #uuid "b01b23bf-d234-5d0f-bc2c-5b3707b41d25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186270838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ea27a89-75a9-59bf-a4c5-de0126835372" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269844} :answer-field/id #uuid "47e0013a-858e-5dd4-84f3-c7915bebcebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7059aa8-79d3-5da6-b8bc-f64a765f9c2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141014" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}, & x<0 \\\\ -1, & x=0 \\\\ x+1, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=0$\n- $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=0$\n- $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $g(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & =\\frac{1}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =g(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=0$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne g(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Three-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04acee2f-7e36-55d8-af85-9d00b145e222" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186269311} {:answer/id #uuid "3e9b1282-6cc9-5d2c-bb98-b6960d4351da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269841} {:answer/id #uuid "4887b9da-4ae1-55e5-81c2-6c730fa8cf9e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186269932} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba73fad-7a10-59ac-9d89-7542d2b369fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186270357} {:answer/id #uuid "aa93b0ab-7385-52a6-9c9a-fdde1cb5b5e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ba73fad-7a10-59ac-9d89-7542d2b369fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186270357} :answer-field/id #uuid "a134005c-2718-5147-bc73-3c3851d6e560" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a7b393e-0e8e-512c-933e-c05c7c1e88c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141016" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & x<\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 1, & x=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 2x, & x>\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is *not* left-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ since the left-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}h(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne h(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}h(x) & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi  \\\\  & \\ne h(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Three-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ace00e-28b4-5eb3-a5ee-9b81b0aad320" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186270152} {:answer/id #uuid "a59765a1-9493-5117-8263-3fd722a0b90b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186270751} {:answer/id #uuid "bee5f5db-b60e-57c9-a3e0-66019d07b791" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270957} {:answer/id #uuid "d038cee2-720b-59d0-b5b4-7548d4d8268e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186271099} {:answer/id #uuid "d8951eb1-320f-5122-be61-f0267f4479bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d038cee2-720b-59d0-b5b4-7548d4d8268e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186271099} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3941ee1-c43e-57bf-a530-dfdd0c1a87d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3376f0ce-d656-5952-8ec5-482d4a788f05" :question/math-academy-id "q-141017" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x+1, & x<-1 \\\\ 1, & x=-1 \\\\ x+2, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is *not* left-continuous at $x=-1$ since the left-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}h(x) & =2(-1)+1 \\\\  & =-2+1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & \\ne h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}h(x) & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & =h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186083303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a4ad32-a873-582b-96ae-ed02f26f7e8f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186269609} {:answer/id #uuid "6514b6a0-1a44-54a4-a5cf-1f9f398648a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186270169} {:answer/id #uuid "668a240d-1631-510d-a9b4-c9d7bbdd0f99" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270179} {:answer/id #uuid "79ea0306-d45d-5d31-b36c-d44f29d74ce1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270348} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2dadc1-bee4-5e05-a544-04e4b92d8545" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26a4ad32-a873-582b-96ae-ed02f26f7e8f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186269609} :answer-field/id #uuid "c371ee98-f872-5961-b48b-e16e995f5c79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a9082ef-b5c5-508a-ad30-c16427769a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141209" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}-8}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186083303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32879a09-f2f5-5307-852d-bdcc80ac28fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186269732} {:answer/id #uuid "5193b80b-aad5-55dd-8026-5fcce1aaa215" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186269999} {:answer/id #uuid "7321fe4c-6365-5e6c-9321-5a1e32c712c2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186270288} {:answer/id #uuid "e05f83bc-9cbf-50fa-8e83-a22bd0a5bf8b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271240} {:answer/id #uuid "efacb9a9-087a-548a-a494-9ae0ca07c85a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e05f83bc-9cbf-50fa-8e83-a22bd0a5bf8b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271240} :answer-field/id #uuid "b230196c-4db7-51b1-b127-98b23dc5d317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c6c3c56-7a5d-5137-ba4b-48f3b706f731" :question/math-academy-id "q-141212" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x}-1}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\sqrt{x}}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x-1}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral II is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {1}^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186083303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0ef079-5f06-5e7c-adef-068bae5538ea" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186269519} {:answer/id #uuid "357e3f3b-92ad-5333-b803-0f7d4d33b7d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269758} {:answer/id #uuid "55026d12-8574-5fbe-8c07-f7e5bd6d4ff3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270025} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc10ea1-5dc4-5c43-8e23-bce8bb264142" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186271090} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b0eda8-710f-5ddd-b5e0-4b4ab8b7f985" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186271164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "357e3f3b-92ad-5333-b803-0f7d4d33b7d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269758} :answer-field/id #uuid "57dc7a29-f195-55f8-9696-8efd8402e367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c19bd8b-6180-555e-84ee-4d1df41e83f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141218" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{-}.\n$$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce55ae1-51e7-5113-8b9b-9cc54aa7d0de" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186269380} {:answer/id #uuid "46da9874-30bc-583c-a863-5aae9560a9cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269913} {:answer/id #uuid "59a1700f-ccf6-5e35-9e43-a7880fc832ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270065} {:answer/id #uuid "75df7d2e-780f-56cd-92ad-5720fda4a706" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270310} {:answer/id #uuid "c456bd0a-afad-5d98-8f69-257b595ed130" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186271001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ce55ae1-51e7-5113-8b9b-9cc54aa7d0de" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186269380} :answer-field/id #uuid "903f2292-1c52-539d-84aa-b1a6a93b0960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6faa06e9-6d50-5778-8c1a-50c6b3712803" :question/math-academy-id "q-141234" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(x)+(-{x}^{3}-1)$\n\nII. $(2{x}^{4}-3)÷({x}^{2})$\n\nIII. $({x}^{2})-(2{x}^{5}+4x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023f4ad7-4e27-58df-b9cf-7d7dff9ec1d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269287} {:answer/id #uuid "67f707f9-a11f-5cd5-b66d-da214b7f66c6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270192} {:answer/id #uuid "d00b4f12-2503-53b5-ad0e-43dad5b4ff65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186271096} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e53e33-d114-5a0d-8f9d-97c29350e249" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id 17592186271178} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc39fd6-b0b1-5463-86d7-17f95b8035dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186271475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d00b4f12-2503-53b5-ad0e-43dad5b4ff65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186271096} :answer-field/id #uuid "2352a387-0098-58a2-9edc-386e6c666edd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6242aae-9378-55e8-b137-ddfe7f38d97f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141236" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(4{x}^{6}-2)÷(2x)$\n- $(7x+1)\\cdot (2{x}^{3}+3)$\n- $({x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1)÷({x}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{6}-2}{2x} & =\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{2x}-\\frac{2}{2x} \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "576103fa-ed39-5cc1-b3dd-aa1c41c6e6b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270040} {:answer/id #uuid "850b62e1-b775-571c-9058-04cc09e464b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186270419} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d1b938-e87f-5462-aecb-d2ec3dd3795d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270910} {:answer/id #uuid "d48c0194-9812-5494-b598-95ff7ec91143" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186271137} {:answer/id #uuid "d736a809-e9dd-5b1c-8eab-1c5a18819085" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "850b62e1-b775-571c-9058-04cc09e464b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186270419} :answer-field/id #uuid "86c7e2f7-2db6-5d5d-9fb1-1b41e12897f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a3f0c75-73fd-5349-840a-8899dcbd367a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141240" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(4{x}^{6}-x)+(3{x}^{5})$\n- $({x}^{3}-1)\\cdot (3-{x}^{2})$\n- $({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x({x}^{2}-4x)}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}-4x)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e78141c-5b1b-5383-9436-dd7961708aa4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186269398} {:answer/id #uuid "377e209c-dca0-560e-b0e9-3dd954d6f5d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186269771} {:answer/id #uuid "b9af0254-5aa7-512b-b479-10e85a3c90b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270908} {:answer/id #uuid "eba4ea75-16bf-56cf-b877-0a5e9c421d89" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id 17592186271340} {:answer/id #uuid "f61c3215-545e-5c9e-9d54-4f213478cea5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e78141c-5b1b-5383-9436-dd7961708aa4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186269398} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ded575-1ef1-5611-9129-6853eed9845c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78e46443-6b0b-52be-80af-856b64b460d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-141245" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(3{x}^{3}-2x)÷({x}^{3})$\n- $(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3})$\n- $(2x-1)\\cdot ({x}^{3}+6x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{3}-2x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3}) & =\\frac{10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}} \\\\  & =5{x}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "429117a3-6e54-516f-90dc-8b3c46852d73" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269870} {:answer/id #uuid "540ff262-b6d8-55d6-bdff-7a8f7374caf0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270017} {:answer/id #uuid "953e5319-0e76-5d91-be9e-97acbe17adc5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270577} {:answer/id #uuid "c637029f-ce11-54ac-92f8-b41a82831cf4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186271015} {:answer/id #uuid "f27f32f7-f9d9-50bf-b772-90b1d8509143" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f27f32f7-f9d9-50bf-b772-90b1d8509143" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271401} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f70c062-2c20-514b-a050-6ed7cd19ecf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "496f9381-a057-5d31-a929-726bf731d70b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141495" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}.$  Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$ for all $k\\in R$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3}$\n- $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and $T$ is a linear transformation, we have $T(2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0$ which means $2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$   Since ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$,  we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the transformation $T$ is not a bijection. Since $T$ is not a bijection, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the corresponding matrix $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09aa63a2-fcd6-59c7-b8d5-c7ae94cea07b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269357} {:answer/id #uuid "222c1fca-f9be-5ece-b1ab-40086be18680" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186269563} {:answer/id #uuid "a1454b89-eae8-5cc9-b299-541dbb47ae01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270698} {:answer/id #uuid "cebbe3cb-717f-56f5-b65c-230ba3342707" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186271082} {:answer/id #uuid "f48fdfad-2d33-560f-be43-a5d4c13ef17b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "222c1fca-f9be-5ece-b1ab-40086be18680" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186269563} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddf7ad5e-33c5-5362-84f5-c267e5402f28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c4507db3-2027-5030-88fd-955381171b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-141569" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{4}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $\\text{nullity}(T)=4,$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4}$\n- $T$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b3cb5a-341a-59e5-a715-d12977c5bed1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186269708} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe66b4f-fc34-5672-a3db-4734e66a55a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270116} {:answer/id #uuid "96a19ab7-cc61-5293-86a8-bafa6581e8e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186270594} {:answer/id #uuid "cb05382b-ecd7-547d-89c0-4aef033b29c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271053} {:answer/id #uuid "da7eed7b-ee2d-5936-b766-061ccc59fa6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186271181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96a19ab7-cc61-5293-86a8-bafa6581e8e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186270594} :answer-field/id #uuid "32ad95d2-baf4-5b0d-81cc-ba23c5a39a70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2805dfb0-8d9a-5775-8051-a005b74b03a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141584" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{6}\\to {R}^{6}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2}$ vectors such that $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}$\n- $T$ is not a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible. Since $T$ is not invertible, by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093de82c-3c10-509e-a115-10c02890e9d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186269353} {:answer/id #uuid "138068fd-1aed-52e6-ac5f-add922c4dca9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186269443} {:answer/id #uuid "3638d5d5-8aa0-56ce-b0f5-e70107346880" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186269762} {:answer/id #uuid "f79af8a6-d754-5246-a9ad-95b33a49ce2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271441} {:answer/id #uuid "fdae7495-8e53-5c68-9f1d-812a9ee8c272" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186271490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdae7495-8e53-5c68-9f1d-812a9ee8c272" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186271490} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd64f202-1cd1-5be2-bcac-f3c8e7039c15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bb3b4f0-a153-5b1f-93aa-f136a6132e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-141751" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d5ea7e13fa03ac553893aa78aa7ee32ac7b67f2eb19dff32228d53bf2010aa2.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave down?" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graphs I and III curve downward (like a \"frown\") among the given graphs."}] [{:db/id 17592186272307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5177ea3a-9272-590f-8f16-d605b524db47" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186269998} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3a782a-c7a0-5f39-9b30-7e1f9ae49e1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270090} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc2623b-ee3b-5cc2-863d-41dcce18a077" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186270363} {:answer/id #uuid "a640d8d9-7217-514b-aa3a-b01bab9dbd46" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186270757} {:answer/id #uuid "be7733e3-82d9-51c4-9a92-5cf1de7b00b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cc2623b-ee3b-5cc2-863d-41dcce18a077" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186270363} :answer-field/id #uuid "6372289f-4413-56d4-a466-f00fa976e952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13227862-4183-5d5b-b484-99ef3e29fd6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141754" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2b904027ec6319fe5857f81089469568322d148e49cdf31bb20c07f1f31daf4.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave down?" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nAll graphs I, II, and III curve downward (like a \"frown\") among the given graphs."}] [{:db/id 17592186272338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c27800-0300-5249-b4a0-b6e008f8e83d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186269558} {:answer/id #uuid "44d71b0a-4454-5106-ad0c-ed629eabb1a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269900} {:answer/id #uuid "708d14c2-579e-5e65-9cd2-447b05f510fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270265} {:answer/id #uuid "a29c9c7d-1f79-5b6d-b1d6-1b0537c578cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186270720} {:answer/id #uuid "f7830376-f651-56ea-b56d-c7df40cee1ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186271439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44d71b0a-4454-5106-ad0c-ed629eabb1a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269900} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3f46adb-5d16-505a-be52-2235f2f08ca3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c320bbb-0961-56b0-99e4-beaa75aa8f10" :question/math-academy-id "q-142358" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}?\n$$\n\n- $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n- $A$ is  invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ccbab42-afa2-5f66-ac0e-c308c66e8202" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186269668} {:answer/id #uuid "82c7cc8b-3d11-56b8-a528-bbb1a33f1d5e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270399} {:answer/id #uuid "952d8149-19cf-533c-af96-872b8df55506" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270576} {:answer/id #uuid "d8826fb6-eeca-5b0d-8d8b-f2147e992c35" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186271170} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d12440-aa2a-5690-b9a2-07e79a593ee7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186271262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2d12440-aa2a-5690-b9a2-07e79a593ee7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186271262} :answer-field/id #uuid "10bb68a7-51a0-5f0c-b2b1-a21badc3475a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "795129ab-7204-5692-a97c-e32525213e2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-142475" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(A)<n$\n- $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, according to the invertible matrix theorem, \n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A).$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)<n.$* $\\,\\times $ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$*  $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0895ab25-c3a3-5de5-8c7f-b29240d396fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269347} {:answer/id #uuid "70dbe989-e5af-54e0-b64e-b2b8f149ec2a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270271} {:answer/id #uuid "8abda789-acc5-5411-8952-794fc73da646" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270468} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e8d21b-4e62-5248-a3d7-8afaad5786f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270745} {:answer/id #uuid "be53ddb2-3789-59ea-8a22-87a097d55924" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0895ab25-c3a3-5de5-8c7f-b29240d396fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269347} :answer-field/id #uuid "afe26b88-241f-530c-8ccf-982cdb7e7d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7036dc73-9ca4-59f2-b983-11eaacfb5a4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-142476" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $det(A)=0$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{16}$\n\nIII. $A$ has $8$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ is an $8\\times 8$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{8} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=8=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times $  \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{16}.$*  $\\,\\times $\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{8}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $A$ has $8$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=8,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $8$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186096691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca6964b-f001-50a6-962e-37568669ccd2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186272499} {:answer/id #uuid "38adbd90-9f47-5cfa-b9c1-7dcb290c0580" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186272883} {:answer/id #uuid "759af798-3374-5082-9e33-2c081c92dd51" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186273389} {:answer/id #uuid "c73c83bf-34b1-563e-83a1-c83796722f15" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186274119} {:answer/id #uuid "fa69b46f-7365-5071-aefe-c9610d1635b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186274583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c73c83bf-34b1-563e-83a1-c83796722f15" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186274119} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c232438-e4d9-548e-93c1-3bdeab4b5cac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6378e369-5031-5af4-8366-576c75d82fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143001" :question/problem "![](images/67/67706db50684aef52269d20065caa21e4301834835e10e1fd3e456646ae2b32e.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCDEF$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $\\overset{\\to }{DF}?$\n\nI. $\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BF}$\n\nII. $\\overset{\\to }{FB}-\\overset{\\to }{DB}$\n\nIII. $\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF}$" :question/worked-solution "- For the $1$st expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BF} & =(\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB})+\\overset{\\to }{BF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}+\\overset{\\to }{BF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DF}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $2$nd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FB}-\\overset{\\to }{DB} & =\\overset{\\to }{FB}+\\overset{\\to }{BD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{FD} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{DF}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $3$rd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF} & =\\overset{\\to }{DF}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186275089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186096691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039f4640-df8c-5f58-8473-a42a9d3b80e8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186272417} {:answer/id #uuid "4dffa4f0-5259-5130-adb8-cb6e99c26fb1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186273042} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2716f3-ecca-55e3-979f-f0158825f290" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186273160} {:answer/id #uuid "c042c4d0-435e-5e30-98e5-20c5ed9387ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186274051} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb3ddd5-6976-5815-aaaa-8715e648bc5d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186274327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c042c4d0-435e-5e30-98e5-20c5ed9387ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186274051} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1ed2a0e-62c9-5160-8b40-423a8e0dfc90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186275030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32cdac5a-ca17-59c1-8641-8b3b47e09944" :question/math-academy-id "q-143006" :question/problem "![](images/15/15f4bd7f8ecf8f61ce84858e33cdfa468b0de360fac19b0713b6b2dbc4af8d2e.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCDEF$ shown above, which of the following equalities are true?\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AB}-\\overset{\\to }{AF}=\\overset{\\to }{BF}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EA}-\\overset{\\to }{BA}=\\overset{\\to }{DB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BE}-\\overset{\\to }{DE}-\\overset{\\to }{CD}=\\overset{\\to }{CB}$" :question/worked-solution "- Equality I is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}-\\overset{\\to }{AF} & =\\overset{\\to }{AB}+\\overset{\\to }{FA} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{FB} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{BF}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equality II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EA}-\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =\\overset{\\to }{DE}+(\\overset{\\to }{EA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}) \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EB} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equality III is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BE}-\\overset{\\to }{DE}-\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =(\\overset{\\to }{BE}+\\overset{\\to }{ED})+\\overset{\\to }{DC} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD}+\\overset{\\to }{DC} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{CB}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186275114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025d6b48-01e5-5f01-8d65-c71247ac2c9d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id 17592186272402} {:answer/id #uuid "33299f41-5d8f-547d-ad5c-48ed2d2cc7f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186272842} {:answer/id #uuid "4db325db-0d80-5c94-8d47-63cd60da22e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186273039} {:answer/id #uuid "b708c468-1a5f-5884-843d-d4de1dfc18a0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186273974} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc201f5-22fa-5ca2-81f0-aa15cf93f039" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186274314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b708c468-1a5f-5884-843d-d4de1dfc18a0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186273974} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bc06cb9-a10b-5ac4-80d0-be2806f417c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a347e7c2-3db8-5058-926f-eeec20d91744" :question/math-academy-id "q-143331" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=2,Y=1)=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.75$ | $0.25$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=2,Y=1)=f(2,1)=0.15.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.2\\cdot 0.75=0.15.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.6=0.8\\cdot 0.75={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,2) & =0.2=0.8\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(2,2) & =0.05=0.2\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb511a6-9e3f-5fab-80ba-b266dbff4317" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186272500} {:answer/id #uuid "0f80c1c4-b8b0-5d20-8f58-ee939af16ade" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186272524} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4845ad-be2d-5904-9060-d4ff681ee8d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186273616} {:answer/id #uuid "baf4e5bc-43d2-59fe-b996-7b9f861daebb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186274009} {:answer/id #uuid "fda572d8-a7cb-50d0-afbf-d1498df635a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186274618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cb511a6-9e3f-5fab-80ba-b266dbff4317" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186272500} :answer-field/id #uuid "a47cbf0e-0dfc-5b44-a81b-f3239de9f23e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e48e8cd-fab9-56d8-9673-3c5737feeff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143333" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=2,Y=2)=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=2,Y=2)=f(2,2)=0.4.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.5\\cdot 0.6=0.3.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=2,Y=2)\\ne P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0546ac24-d2f1-5953-9176-6d38e1f738e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186272437} {:answer/id #uuid "36567941-4a76-538e-b399-81b828902e4d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186272866} {:answer/id #uuid "adcc90b9-7b62-57f4-b547-8bcf79edd9d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186273886} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b0d5ca-bc00-5e71-b084-1b238b1ba8f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186274219} {:answer/id #uuid "f2536e7d-d2f7-5f66-9627-aed4d7e02129" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186274528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36567941-4a76-538e-b399-81b828902e4d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186272866} :answer-field/id #uuid "96723ab9-6095-5228-8b7f-012e24dab80f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16b71f57-df8c-5727-afc2-cdadae9185c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143334" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.6$ | $0.15$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=0,Y=3)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=3)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.6$ | $0.15$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=3)=f(0,3)=0.15.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=3)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(3)=0.25\\cdot 0.3=0.075.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=0,Y=3)\\ne P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=3),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a8f8d7-4d6f-5a08-8231-327845ba4d85" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186272758} {:answer/id #uuid "610da49c-8865-5852-b87b-a49c0a223ca9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186273227} {:answer/id #uuid "87a51537-6779-5081-93a5-5585b9685064" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186273549} {:answer/id #uuid "cef4a47c-100b-5941-af4b-e9103ef31cba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186274188} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ec2394-8af4-54b2-b440-029010d2bb5a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id 17592186274568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29a8f8d7-4d6f-5a08-8231-327845ba4d85" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186272758} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a58c3f2-6a19-560b-9e61-46106b8cc5d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "743eb09d-bf29-57ce-b05b-46da6dccd3f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143338" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.16$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.64$ | $0.09$ | $0.07$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=3,Y=2)=P(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.16$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.64$ | $0.09$ | $0.07$ | $0.8$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.8$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=3,Y=2)=f(3,2)=0.64.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.8\\cdot 0.8=0.64.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =0.16=0.2\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,4) & =0.01\\ne 0.2\\cdot 0.1={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(4) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21bb620a-f093-5caa-b1a9-20fc5f8cfee9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186272687} {:answer/id #uuid "80870de8-5e3b-5b1b-9028-7729c2eaea11" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186273489} {:answer/id #uuid "8c12813a-38d4-579d-a91