ed}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a50740e-24d8-5780-9497-19c6ece3d12f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186272613} {:answer/id #uuid "c5bd4857-ac42-501a-afee-68ed1f090a51" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186274103} {:answer/id #uuid "d470a61d-9b60-594f-8de0-64bd4e327f55" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186274250} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ed8770-d0c9-5fd8-874c-51bd1000938a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186274290} {:answer/id #uuid "e46caf60-f48c-5c19-a408-c2d5469c2e65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186274409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e46caf60-f48c-5c19-a408-c2d5469c2e65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186274409} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b48ef03-f856-526f-b739-6f66d9042d61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f120679-716b-5edd-8658-50352ee4a1a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158414" :question/problem "![](images/49/495b8aaac849e90ffd46f34f35dd4d3783c2c4fa35c3887c339387d31215d7af.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4},\\, & 0<x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x+1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+2]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+0]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ba54c3f-064a-505e-a601-3acf3faeb1a0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186272623} {:answer/id #uuid "2687fa37-7cd9-57df-ae31-bf65e49d2f47" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186272723} {:answer/id #uuid "29886336-d545-55a2-be6c-36709fde9ec8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186272756} {:answer/id #uuid "91f1887b-7e63-57c3-941d-9dd481993654" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186273659} {:answer/id #uuid "c66381cb-1bde-5b2c-a1e5-601c5da32b36" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186274107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29886336-d545-55a2-be6c-36709fde9ec8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186272756} :answer-field/id #uuid "1939075e-3ac3-5a0b-88b8-649ddaf11e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ced5dff-e7df-54bc-a45c-2aa3418fd883" :question/math-academy-id "q-158419" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4acac98ef7276cb1e90557ca161666a33ccf0a7627b9ebfcbbe44508b9e5f5bb.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{3}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109d13ff-3bfe-5465-8e92-ac9059d764ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186275624} {:answer/id #uuid "29e60057-5cb4-5015-95b0-91bee1f5e649" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186275856} {:answer/id #uuid "392e6a3e-2941-5807-afa5-d592600d687d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186275984} {:answer/id #uuid "b23aee92-8125-55d2-a4af-7c2cd02f7163" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277072} {:answer/id #uuid "e071ea78-d1da-5cc3-8380-acd11f8d0e0a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186277437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "109d13ff-3bfe-5465-8e92-ac9059d764ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186275624} :answer-field/id #uuid "b498bc2c-917d-5d5a-9ad4-b1ae4a48ec03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bac4e6a1-90fc-5ecd-80e4-898747c5b764" :question/math-academy-id "q-158424" :question/problem "![](images/81/819fa17f44f669746ba0c82d2d986f1cd5955a1d7d8144eda6922c0cb7813c9c.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3},\\, & 1\\le x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{15}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{15}]-[\\frac{{1}^{4}}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fedb2c-bf2b-5eef-8ec0-bc146ad8d61c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186275821} {:answer/id #uuid "3848fe11-38a7-5fe9-90cc-d571fae7f82f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186275975} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6c5a79-0bcb-5037-be35-12c21b765dc3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276010} 