t III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe4c108-0c0a-5e29-911c-d1646ab97905" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186275771} {:answer/id #uuid "40c140df-3b9c-5298-903d-5b271568acb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276042} {:answer/id #uuid "535322b9-be17-54df-a7a2-0c2483ef3419" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276217} {:answer/id #uuid "ade0f0bb-07c8-5c24-b4d4-641372fd68b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277038} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0393b2-a850-5c48-8662-d0108f8af66c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186277274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40c140df-3b9c-5298-903d-5b271568acb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276042} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3c08baf-5e84-59ed-8178-0fc243f082fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ba85730-5b8d-5d4c-8799-b386e641d64f" :question/math-academy-id "q-158427" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae6d25fae4127a74b38bcb9c0de8f44705dca81353c89557047e5d35a3465c5b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "427c9c68-1ef6-5faf-85f0-b60b26c9e8e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276061} {:answer/id #uuid "67cf8a35-513b-5da7-851e-d389efdea9a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276407} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc1d034-4ce7-53ad-86ed-b2170e1c8f19" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186276486} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2f50e6-13cd-5008-8539-204ab7524eba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277281} {:answer/id #uuid "e4dc87e7-b7df-5745-bf8c-e4290eee23fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb2f50e6-13cd-5008-8539-204ab7524eba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277281} :answer-field/id #uuid "81eec413-638f-5e83-9478-74f78dad803b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50d76297-0f50-5089-b83a-de45e6a36bc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-158428" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e540dc9309f2a5d1753760b4050c049e380705914d7289b47319de0b0696a9a.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{3}-1),\\, & \\frac{1}{2}<x\\le \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. $f(x)$ is negative for some value of $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}4({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-x]}_{1/2}^{3/2} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{81}{64}-\\frac{3}{2}]-[\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{2}]) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{80}{64}-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{4}-1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement I is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f6fe045-70ea-5894-a90b-388f3bedbcf2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276027} {:answer/id #uuid "420f62d1-9078-5c1f-8386-e2b504eb643e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276054} {:answer/id #uuid "62138cea-0ce0-5fe0-a43e-c434297442e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276362} {:answer/id #uuid "ba70cada-15bf-5af8-9de3-0cf37e32754a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186277132} {:answer/id #uuid "d2944c44-2492-51ff-b8ac-439157fc2325" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "420f62d1-9078-5c1f-8386-e2b504eb643e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276054} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e5737c-8be4-59c6-b944-061e18639622" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0715ac14-20ca-5be9-8d09-d1a1ccc33941" :question/math-academy-id "q-158429" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c30c392223857c77cfc8619f1fa0d4b7fca20af618bdd424570cc3ed712f38b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-1,\\, & 1<x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}-x]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[9-3]-[1-1] \\\\  & =6-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "892a8ae7-7816-5ec8-abd7-0f193b914c86" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276716} {:answer/id #uuid "994734ca-d6aa-5641-8d0e-63d406e741fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186276853} {:answer/id #uuid "cef0cd2a-9051-554c-a6f5-91d00529319a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186277308} {:answer/id #uuid "e65fda98-7388-56e8-99ca-9bfe1594e4dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277488} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc62238-282a-5ff2-b84f-201b8a5eed6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186277541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "994734ca-d6aa-5641-8d0e-63d406e741fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186276853} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd602e17-33cb-5cf6-97db-92756d77aaff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed33ac2f-6299-5342-b520-1399b2a77786" :question/math-academy-id "q-158684" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5dccdb1f8957bb15d7b2215320c6e632819a647bf4dc350ff850876bae6298ec.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-3,2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/d5/d595e29d31743694c404b99c79c60e40b152d30083e633d4269ad4444be3f826.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/23/2336ff8a65dd397e8324384b9f9536f3c7d4ad2d76a81caeba28d7fe3ec229ef.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/26/26903667d45cecf81539025c4cda333129cf89600821b383f20d574c75e537b0.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"}] [{:db/id 17592186278220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0243a0f1-2e03-5947-a203-a74798f52121" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186275498} {:answer/id #uuid "1d167fdd-7c58-55ea-9621-e7c510872897" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186275743} {:answer/id #uuid "227d62f9-8ba3-5bb9-80bd-c1da5585045a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186275786} {:answer/id #uuid "f68d95d4-11a1-5b68-a4f8-df5901c443ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277634} {:answer/id #uuid "fed55126-d8c5-5c16-a6ce-4064c6e9eb5a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186277714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f68d95d4-11a1-5b68-a4f8-df5901c443ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277634} :answer-field/id #uuid "487c91f9-e32d-5423-97c7-0f4657cf1eca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1861489-bfb5-5c9b-ba84-97990e733cb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-158685" :question/problem "![](images/73/73f61de1a716545449439ed8875b465dc5aec6f3148e520ad028a96725f2a4f0.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[0,1]\\cup {2}$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained in the set $M.$\n\n![](images/84/84afb5331ef9244120d9f3d9b14e25fa2d19da31f8eb685c5d74b089e0d99289.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/c0/c0b35fafc6e628c36e41fa8df42e3a477d8aac4af0b4c3819fa0063e5bd014df.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ Notice that $C$ is a point of $M$ and $C$ belongs to any open neighborhood of $C.$\n\n![](images/e5/e54fa85543a79c18e00d3b03281d6774c98fdb03e9b5440b8f179ce5a0d09519.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only.'"}] [{:db/id 17592186278224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3585e5cf-97a2-5a9f-9483-1363128dc2ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186275955} {:answer/id #uuid "72c53d80-bb03-50ef-96b9-e5708290dc29" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276517} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffb6950-d856-55a4-a552-464de9c420fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276779} {:answer/id #uuid "bd749509-056c-5495-87c6-08d0205808fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186277167} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bad058-e92c-5e6a-94cb-829624887a12" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186277325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0bad058-e92c-5e6a-94cb-829624887a12" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186277325} :answer-field/id #uuid "a49af98b-856c-574a-b392-0ae041c650d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94c1ba54-750c-5dd8-b08c-c465652d3c9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-159264" :question/problem "![](images/07/07463b409d54d1970ee9d47cad5de7188ea44e682a58e7e941b3cd4bf7f5efa6.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-1,1)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $A$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside $M.$\n\n![](images/73/734ff08a25e25486dc65f919ce99bc5422ca8ce264bb7d044479b1eb3b6a716b.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/28/286f8b397b3c908d29678640d691558a784e31751d09c68e6b6da855c272231c.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/7e/7ed7726fa1a139ded4a9226bcb01c17d0ad46ef4bd03a820f033640c68778453.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only.'"}] [{:db/id 17592186278225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dcda877-ce32-54bb-86fa-841eb47431e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186275590} {:answer/id #uuid "acc485f4-60b3-5514-a8d1-316701fb6d4f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277031} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a14746-068d-52e8-8dbf-99390fdab2fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277088} {:answer/id #uuid "d25a5ddc-5600-5cdb-8e5a-06d78eaba4be" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186277333} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c7f128-018d-5267-98fd-8c3e16aa45a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186277381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acc485f4-60b3-5514-a8d1-316701fb6d4f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277031} :answer-field/id #uuid "e72f9d85-496b-5a8f-a884-7d71c4e09859" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae427c1b-c208-5c4a-a164-5e046d8d2ea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-159274" :question/problem "![](images/43/43380e1382df5db66c4a9ab0ee385e98073da7fb684ccdf93a665e223fb1a554.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-\\infty ,1)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/c6/c66edbc427dbd1e970a5c25422fbfca39296b0a90b6ca26dacb3280551105784.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/7b/7b8beea4c575e89b2c66f363271a90068e912422ad8991a333375f6894523a57.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists any neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained *outside* the set $M.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a06851f25bf80edcc934fe7f341a6a43a065e744691164d46f77d116d4bf047.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id 17592186278226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100300ab-7a48-59a3-8f5c-174ca08e7765" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186275616} {:answer/id #uuid "15c34ad8-fa0b-5a75-8eb4-85368db54d14" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186275679} {:answer/id #uuid "34416daf-38dd-57f1-8f28-56a3e47bcfcf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186275942} {:answer/id #uuid "501c4afd-a964-52cf-bd2c-92914657e867" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276195} {:answer/id #uuid "87df0387-b2a0-58e7-ac29-1982d729669c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15c34ad8-fa0b-5a75-8eb4-85368db54d14" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186275679} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d8f0289-9a31-5539-9fae-2ed2de52f41c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "951baf6c-974c-552a-a084-e87ba6cba5d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-159485" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4ad8b759d3f2781b1dc6adf64e58a230218655249f21fdd8f6a852568554ead.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/56/56a640baf27e1acb5c3428e1bef25a87b3ff965dff71762b94fb1eff94aaf24d.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'."}] [{:db/id 17592186278227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048011b9-ffee-53bf-ac13-2e97743e225c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186275519} {:answer/id #uuid "0efa31d6-31db-55c2-9b88-0a9a3a3453fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186275607} {:answer/id #uuid "417cc1c5-3d24-5fc5-a84c-b4dc4ea116ac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276052} {:answer/id #uuid "4305747f-a88f-529f-a758-988093ddc9b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276069} {:answer/id #uuid "eda63547-3ece-56ee-9327-e038a1f457a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "417cc1c5-3d24-5fc5-a84c-b4dc4ea116ac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276052} :answer-field/id #uuid "77bae57d-74be-5ed9-b597-76e04905579c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e85ebcb1-0815-545b-a7ea-7afc898a561a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159502" :question/problem "![](images/79/79fc33355e32fc3b57a129e83fdf35ecdd1e5b0f3275c433a49d85642cf40f3f.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/a7/a7f99060032c4f79df8b2ee68e220f31bb14f29f99e9190c9b385d073f3274d9.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is false. Every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $A$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."}] [{:db/id 17592186278228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a87d209-f254-5e99-94a3-0ddb685b4606" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186275867} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab69bab-cd54-568d-a211-6e10a07499f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186275992} {:answer/id #uuid "6eef0fc7-bda9-5526-bdb2-5aeceb0f1904" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276472} {:answer/id #uuid "90955a99-dd82-5134-a08a-bdd5c1bddd84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186276787} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2f0be8-be9d-5c84-a2ec-b3ae58f612fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90955a99-dd82-5134-a08a-bdd5c1bddd84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186276787} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7b53aa9-c9a6-5c2c-9e5b-5a42d90582fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9430b4bf-f1ad-5467-9486-5b90e2e64bb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-159550" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e999290971eed02d7f12f9154bdb4d04f3328fd250b385059d94e83e3f195b2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/f1/f10ff87f64dc07adb8e9535926186341bab88f2d0c783475ef8fd57b06b35fc0.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."}] [{:db/id 17592186278265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dee7594-767a-521f-928c-9f8f15efe4fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186275750} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb2e03d-9953-5eb9-a536-ad69732a1f40" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186275903} {:answer/id #uuid "5e17f6c4-abcb-5795-b673-23aac5d88fb1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276316} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad87c08-849b-5dd3-b088-825965a6f51d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276436} {:answer/id #uuid "ef719bcd-1195-59b2-a5bc-eeccf0b0f347" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ad87c08-849b-5dd3-b088-825965a6f51d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276436} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5e3bd56-16a0-5dc4-9fa7-2fc1cdf50250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13406d62-3495-5617-8c59-07fc2d20bdc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-159977" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,4),(0,2),$ and $(7,10).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(2,3)$\n- $(1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,1),(-1,2),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "130ffd37-1468-59cd-96b1-b10e7bdf65ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186275651} {:answer/id #uuid "6859ae0e-d44d-5274-9b9e-d7e68347e033" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276410} {:answer/id #uuid "9abd85c9-71b3-56c3-8aa1-d23dcc5bc7cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276863} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ea5b85-addc-5034-aa5a-a73a7c6981d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276992} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7813b3-c467-5163-8ec3-ddced4315606" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186277712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9abd85c9-71b3-56c3-8aa1-d23dcc5bc7cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276863} :answer-field/id #uuid "55beb8be-bd4d-5ffb-803e-4528647a6e74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0b402cc-9d61-5ea9-b280-42fd73163096" :question/math-academy-id "q-159978" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,2),(7,5),$ and $(2,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(0,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{9-8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(1,1),(-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e96e870-a8af-5662-990f-06837d1c1870" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276021} {:answer/id #uuid "a084d05c-5854-5b46-818f-33c21866514c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276923} {:answer/id #uuid "b88e3577-8da0-530e-96a8-50f04a54528a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277112} {:answer/id #uuid "d86d5412-4be8-5eb1-9303-99785d92c929" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186277380} {:answer/id #uuid "db95dfe7-00c7-5ec1-9e3f-e75c11dc2fd3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b88e3577-8da0-530e-96a8-50f04a54528a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277112} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9639c91-0add-57a7-80f2-ad69ca23cf47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf3cb2bb-bb5c-564c-af6e-ef65a9ae1e35" :question/math-academy-id "q-160368" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,0),(-1,0),(2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(-1,0)$\n- $(2,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,0),(-1,0),(-2,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289d7999-7a6b-5687-a2ef-02f8b473d1a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186275845} {:answer/id #uuid "81c07be2-1172-50f7-a04c-94df6d7a277e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276647} {:answer/id #uuid "b18abb20-e495-5103-863f-119ae8db6fd5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186277066} {:answer/id #uuid "d44f9e45-37ec-5a84-b576-6c21b41c0a47" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277347} {:answer/id #uuid "e74a3358-871b-50ec-b11f-4fa504902a11" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b18abb20-e495-5103-863f-119ae8db6fd5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186277066} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5b5dc04-4d52-5d46-b88e-b20b9902f553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fb94a38-de9a-5a83-b5a3-84ca0f8fdab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-160369" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,5),(4,-3),(3,2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(2,5)$\n- $(-4,-3)$\n- $(-3,-2)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(2,-5),(-4,3),(-3,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5de2521b-1eec-563c-85f4-ea0a7a5e2ac5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276315} {:answer/id #uuid "68e23fa0-c706-5d3c-9dea-e5bffba2edd6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276419} {:answer/id #uuid "6f26ba0c-8043-55c7-bcd3-aba9f943f97c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276474} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6081c3-c38a-5b5d-925d-c7f55c991451" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186277698} {:answer/id #uuid "ff041318-9ffe-52c8-84b2-c84d717421f5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186277718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5de2521b-1eec-563c-85f4-ea0a7a5e2ac5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276315} :answer-field/id #uuid "656c65fc-9e17-5d9c-8874-1ca316880de0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "213f7139-636c-56af-8c2a-7163cc744332" :question/math-academy-id "q-160371" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(6,-1),(-4,1),(3,5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-6,-1)$\n- $(4,-1)$\n- $(3,-5)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-6,1),(4,-1),(-3,-5).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b97fdd-ec15-5024-a675-6f18d78a504f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186275937} {:answer/id #uuid "72a79a05-3ff8-5e96-bcb2-e8a8ff867510" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186276515} {:answer/id #uuid "7711ff19-0075-5799-8216-046f734fa934" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276553} {:answer/id #uuid "8e00bb97-add7-5acf-8a4e-5a5680f62f91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276766} {:answer/id #uuid "a6968b4d-0033-5df2-9d13-63334bda3e6a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72a79a05-3ff8-5e96-bcb2-e8a8ff867510" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186276515} :answer-field/id #uuid "379ed9a7-417d-594f-ba8d-c45f27b16100" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff0f96a5-d30c-508e-bdde-b67be8bf18d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-160374" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,-3),(2,-2),(1,3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(-1,-3)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,3),(-2,2),(-1,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4b612ef-ce7f-5783-848d-d8ef210c9f47" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Argand Diagram Corresponding to a Complex Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b4da5d-1547-5e75-a0c1-db44d993e711" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186275541} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc3aa1f-7b6e-539c-8ca0-13d548dcfd38" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186275905} {:answer/id #uuid "774a27e9-495e-5ec6-a4c4-eb40083b9df9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276557} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2bcb50-dc39-55fc-813b-bc57e9fba2e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276860} {:answer/id #uuid "a196150c-05e4-526a-aa0e-20293c61db43" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "774a27e9-495e-5ec6-a4c4-eb40083b9df9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276557} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b74de4-a3f0-5fa5-9815-5929160c53c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ef2dcb7-ec09-542d-a947-352534dabf78" :question/math-academy-id "q-160399" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers lie in the fourth quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n\n- $3+4\\text{i}$\n- $-2+3\\text{i}$\n- $1-2\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "- The complex number $3+4\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=3$ and the imaginary part $y=4.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(3,4)$, which lies in the first quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-2+3\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=3.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,3)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $1-2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=1$ and the imaginary part $y=-2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(1,-2)$, which lies in the fourth quadrant.\n\n![](images/a4/a4f87e680d46b099b9543b552191c776ac7145fba4e1b51b10c44a3685907d46.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186278294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74fece1e-fbf6-5fd9-9e1a-5416ec62c323" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276536} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b6c80a-c282-540a-bd6d-4542edbded5e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276953} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb85f95-c186-5d26-b937-d9f1355f0b41" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277432} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fa639f-56f1-511f-917a-859bb168c38d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186277444} {:answer/id #uuid "fe514759-fa62-5f81-9d49-4904ecb413e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74fece1e-fbf6-5fd9-9e1a-5416ec62c323" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276536} :answer-field/id #uuid "52eacd48-b72d-58aa-9a61-50f4f444fd02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7b0ec58-a026-543f-8622-101c6f9cdc96" :question/math-academy-id "q-160419" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\pi .$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=0$\n- $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- $y=\\pi $" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi ,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi .$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-0+\\pi =\\pi \\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi =1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\pi $ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\pi +\\pi =0-\\pi +\\pi =0.$$\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5754d888-a19f-5662-85cd-97ec227085bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276256} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2a7f7a-ec83-5bd9-b208-4cfc18ee1a89" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277128} {:answer/id #uuid "c32b2070-b78b-5733-ba17-565798a45b90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186277212} {:answer/id #uuid "d39eeda1-d2e0-53db-b284-9cca13530481" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277344} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f8f6ed-bbe6-59b5-b0f0-7bcd01850f52" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5754d888-a19f-5662-85cd-97ec227085bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276256} :answer-field/id #uuid "8edd4a5b-896c-56d5-b92c-2582116c958d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09328241-3b39-5707-a62b-8ca38e3a3a9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160422" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}=1.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=-1$\n- $y=1$\n- $y=2$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=-1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1)=1-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)=1-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{4}\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77f9743d-0bae-54e2-ac3f-9a8ef0ec7c2e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186276565} {:answer/id #uuid "7da50905-a243-5398-a4ae-0453d1b93806" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276622} {:answer/id #uuid "93e136cf-9d3b-5f55-8a6c-8a359eec69bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186276817} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f674f1-c1e7-55df-9493-3907af552827" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277466} {:answer/id #uuid "f54f13cc-6d1a-55a9-951b-1e16008b0a36" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da50905-a243-5398-a4ae-0453d1b93806" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276622} :answer-field/id #uuid "6faf48e5-84a2-5798-ac83-b8aebb344e49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b24e08c4-4299-5d2f-a3af-dcce0689b7c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-160447" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x/2},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 0,$ we have $f(x)={e}^{x/2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x/2}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0} \\\\  & =2({e}^{0}-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5839b9b4-19d4-5b18-aa1e-51a34ceb3a19" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186276268} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc1e03a-b7b1-5552-a83a-8ea6f725ba18" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276603} {:answer/id #uuid "87bbf739-19e8-5b24-94ae-cdbc161472cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276703} {:answer/id #uuid "9d20deef-0bff-545f-9d9a-18d430a2a6a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276888} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ae06da-6e7a-526b-81cc-04db2c9ea087" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186277635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d20deef-0bff-545f-9d9a-18d430a2a6a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276888} :answer-field/id #uuid "17bfa705-14a0-5ebd-95e5-81dc96aef48f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0b61c31-2e3e-5b9a-9684-a3b763cdba2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160450" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-2) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03928662-6606-5129-a72d-197d49633a11" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186275512} {:answer/id #uuid "36b3cff8-5ecc-5468-a7fa-6f23440457ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186275964} {:answer/id #uuid "6887ebca-c248-5881-954d-54e181b800dd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186276413} {:answer/id #uuid "8f343f00-0285-57ae-8578-7519e66e5776" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276775} {:answer/id #uuid "956e5fa9-6a49-5a82-89c8-6447bfbf9640" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f343f00-0285-57ae-8578-7519e66e5776" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276775} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc04777-b31e-5fbe-bdc5-27b020016731" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9f1f757-0f29-5257-92da-0c605e690da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-160455" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{1-2x},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=2{e}^{1-2x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }2{e}^{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-2x}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0635a288-3424-5623-8e57-1b3b518f0693" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186275533} {:answer/id #uuid "272eee21-fff3-5adc-b9d2-90a549a7bf4f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186275835} {:answer/id #uuid "61d29390-173c-5987-b3dd-5c50a916cff3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276360} {:answer/id #uuid "697f8c55-e019-50f8-ba40-ef76fef54d1f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276425} {:answer/id #uuid "6b906749-6c4e-5150-bdfb-38a2a704df05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "272eee21-fff3-5adc-b9d2-90a549a7bf4f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186275835} :answer-field/id #uuid "46321fd4-fcba-5032-bf57-4b6cf9004b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61d3fab2-038b-5d24-9a0c-708feb5787d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160617" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,1),(-1,2),(1,2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(-1,3)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(0,-1),(1,-2),(-1,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36022495-72a2-5e93-bf1a-8b5f2f33cc5a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186275959} {:answer/id #uuid "863deeca-aa1d-5bce-9cce-0a5af1b302b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276681} {:answer/id #uuid "8d06f1dc-af3e-528c-aff2-5338c4fa84e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276752} {:answer/id #uuid "b78753dc-9d91-59ea-bfa3-225c0eb2f9d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277107} {:answer/id #uuid "e4950627-9582-50ba-a626-0fc32658696d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4950627-9582-50ba-a626-0fc32658696d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277474} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7fbf2c-73b0-5fd3-90e5-9b2385027897" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db002c05-0ad9-5e9a-a3e6-2b51920ba6fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-160618" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-1),(2,-3),(1,5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(2,1)$\n- $(-2,3)$\n- $(-1,5)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(2,1),(-2,3),(-1,-5).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075cc8fc-050c-5ce2-9474-038725717320" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186275547} {:answer/id #uuid "099a3512-ff25-50ea-a53a-64d03f0c801f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186275559} {:answer/id #uuid "4867885c-5bc5-5b3c-92ce-4ba812211ff6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276119} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0bec23-e660-58cb-9e9e-bf867e808af1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276897} {:answer/id #uuid "c3746cdc-6312-57c7-8428-6f443a718163" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3746cdc-6312-57c7-8428-6f443a718163" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277215} :answer-field/id #uuid "2155e4dc-891d-5ccc-b422-ce54d6030371" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11e8f9e6-6315-5288-8908-c9cd51236438" :question/math-academy-id "q-160643" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,1),(-1,0),(-1,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(0,-1)$\n- $(-2,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,0),(0,1),(-2,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e809067-fccf-511c-9fa9-c259ab9e7737" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186275894} {:answer/id #uuid "40ecaffa-3314-5656-b789-5511c190e435" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276045} {:answer/id #uuid "483e2208-febd-5425-9f85-aeb182abec07" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276118} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6406ee-ca13-59f3-9ded-4abe2538501a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276589} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c8ca19-6194-528b-b32e-041a39580876" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186277375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e809067-fccf-511c-9fa9-c259ab9e7737" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186275894} :answer-field/id #uuid "59df823e-787f-5891-af52-12920962dd0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "932ec8a6-f54a-5a6e-82db-611776aadb8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-160644" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-3),(3,1),(-4,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(3,2)$\n- $(1,-3)$\n- $(-1,4)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-3,-2),(1,-3),(-1,4).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f0666e-f729-5d5d-985b-9b3b5b14541c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186275682} {:answer/id #uuid "630a936b-d542-585d-b79f-690989f4f5b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276374} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ff9584-6905-5910-ae2e-3dc01139b3bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277210} {:answer/id #uuid "d039d837-96b9-5df7-a439-a5936cc2ba06" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277321} {:answer/id #uuid "edb63265-b8c1-54df-bd63-f9bfae318c1d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2ff9584-6905-5910-ae2e-3dc01139b3bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277210} :answer-field/id #uuid "c21e65b9-a67a-5851-95df-a8435a9f7571" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b1a15ea-ca58-59ce-bcaf-a775bc97fe15" :question/math-academy-id "q-160645" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-3,1),(1,-4),(1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-1,3)$\n- $(4,1)$\n- $(0,-1)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-1,-3),(4,1),(0,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e457a68-368e-5519-ba70-e4e8377c24b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186275596} {:answer/id #uuid "6c00bf17-6e38-54fd-bfcd-0a33163b9db6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276447} {:answer/id #uuid "e5176e48-7ad7-5833-b3df-a314bb7db91e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186277476} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b30346-bad7-5377-822b-4463b42748de" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277491} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6f6235-bc2c-5db5-a32f-903fec60bd64" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186277580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c00bf17-6e38-54fd-bfcd-0a33163b9db6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276447} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcfe0732-cd93-5d75-b448-61744b58aa22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d53ec269-7021-530a-b0ec-0053da9223ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-160647" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(5,-1),(2,0),(-2,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-1,-5)$\n- $(0,2)$\n- $(-1,-2)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-1,-5),(0,-2),(-1,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ef0160-9c08-5420-ac3e-f0c9a2630c3f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186275543} {:answer/id #uuid "1049947d-3dc7-5dd0-a908-a940464ec261" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186275621} {:answer/id #uuid "27315988-2ebf-509d-a0df-f20a951101f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186275836} {:answer/id #uuid "85eb82e3-3096-5b14-9e3e-c4284501032e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276679} {:answer/id #uuid "efbaf79c-9396-519c-9312-50554e46451d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efbaf79c-9396-519c-9312-50554e46451d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277584} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f6ea799-ea45-5231-86a4-c7e103378e65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97308e70-f67e-5dc9-9884-0142720ba3a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-160648" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(1,-1),(-2,3),(-1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(-3,-2)$\n- $(0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,1),(-3,-2),(0,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40181228-d1a4-5f6a-ae31-3d18e7b02423" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276035} {:answer/id #uuid "65d0ca9d-0d4a-5988-b9af-cee83ee8965f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276390} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9e5fe0-5935-52d6-b88c-b47ff9e397ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276891} {:answer/id #uuid "a510b60d-9c71-5128-b4f1-b4d0e5b901e8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276966} {:answer/id #uuid "e28c29f0-60b2-5889-8d91-84e597eb513b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186277460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40181228-d1a4-5f6a-ae31-3d18e7b02423" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276035} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a33e723-4167-587e-b7ca-38e0c3b47429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "507c7b5b-1f55-5451-94d5-1770ddfeea4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161031" :question/problem "![](images/09/0963fc3c56b5baac68e2d587896bf4655950640ecce5b924edc2d8e26a3e64e2.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- One endpoint of the image is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, and the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(u,\\,0),\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/92/92bd315a07a5d76e77c249c12ea4544ee1555b0c214c68cf1f92fc3f07a71ddf.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $0$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\n- When $u=0,$ we obtain the point \n$(0,\\,0),$\nwhich is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane.\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{3},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0),$\nwhose distance to $O$ is $\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo, one endpoint of the segment is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, while the other endpoint lies on the circle with radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "430e8d34-03b9-591d-9c8b-3e31f116c812" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276072} {:answer/id #uuid "486f4a48-a6ae-542e-b3a0-6d01f2273f98" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276120} {:answer/id #uuid "b61b5d70-d1e6-5e4a-82bd-2355dd672e84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277098} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5de8b1-d4b1-54f7-94a6-cad99f16910b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277417} {:answer/id #uuid "f9abb118-15c8-5f39-ac81-9db0da50fa75" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186277661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd5de8b1-d4b1-54f7-94a6-cad99f16910b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277417} :answer-field/id #uuid "9db7279b-07f0-58ff-81d0-471d2a4854aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b7f9af3-cd62-5b44-9828-72b23454ff87" :question/math-academy-id "q-161032" :question/problem "![](images/16/16d74107dbbf12749766cd9cea7e38d16e28a59efb997bd224aed73c7830324e.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{10\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{10\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,2\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfe75ef6b4ea9ddd72be6501f95fd89dd1e80fb2aed691c3c22b158514141cc1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=2,$ we obtain the point \n$(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $2.$\n\n- When $u=4,$ we obtain the point \n$(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $4.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54e96dcb-b37b-59f3-a5d9-cf6b169e032f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186276232} {:answer/id #uuid "587aa0bc-7ea2-57e4-8ff4-3cc53fb9b78e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276270} {:answer/id #uuid "58ea32ea-2728-58c6-8af0-73ed46e3e2ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276277} {:answer/id #uuid "625a38d4-f891-54f4-af8a-15ab4d1ab5a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276365} {:answer/id #uuid "f5fef837-46d6-5e93-a262-d3a679ae1e31" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186277628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "587aa0bc-7ea2-57e4-8ff4-3cc53fb9b78e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276270} :answer-field/id #uuid "991a93a1-cd71-529f-b98e-a418fb4cd4fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35901d5d-b737-59bd-bd22-20c686ac3fe1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161035" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8afe0884ccf1e62ffdfef6cab4626bc59b3138aa5cd26f855980c88971753447.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- One endpoint of the image is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, and the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $5$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b836799bc512db70d2ce5fcfe70db2af3b2572d584d317fa5ed515b363a11ddc.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=0,$ we obtain the point $(0,\\,0),$ which is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane.\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{5},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),$\nwhose distance to $O$ is $\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nSo, one endpoint of the segment is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, while the other endpoint lies in the circle with radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at $O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c05d4f9-eb1b-59a1-ae0b-5d6869bad7c5" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f986685-2b9c-5ad8-afd4-5c1b7e29303d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186275767} {:answer/id #uuid "332a3130-e43a-557e-8e1b-e97db157e079" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186275934} {:answer/id #uuid "8718f4c3-3e01-525e-9503-03e608444155" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276692} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1b7dec-e205-571c-a49b-c55c5660c047" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276753} {:answer/id #uuid "efb3b210-9a39-5bd8-aa26-b746f808602e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f986685-2b9c-5ad8-afd4-5c1b7e29303d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186275767} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0e96430-d1f7-5606-bae1-fae2771fbd41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b05250a-2126-54a5-8110-d92f7f2aa122" :question/math-academy-id "q-161117" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bb6d2b9f9e09de44e078657ce3517bfd78261d3206743b7882abf0112f38a93.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=1$, $z=0,$ and $z=3-x+y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Indeed, we have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y.$\n\n![](images/65/65e179e16b66009b9168c4380032b01c3a79b3c4a2afc3b8e5084defb77b85fc.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c05d4f9-eb1b-59a1-ae0b-5d6869bad7c5" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a25012c-a27b-5018-a243-b72ad59f9ef2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186275722} {:answer/id #uuid "56fe8ae8-ef09-5baf-b6d9-cf3a045b9e98" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276254} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec1613e-aa94-5f61-ac6a-16e3c6b423e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276628} {:answer/id #uuid "939aacfd-4fe9-5c6e-a527-af6a74544fd5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276814} {:answer/id #uuid "f93026a8-8e6c-5ee7-b0fb-3f8e3fa2067e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f93026a8-8e6c-5ee7-b0fb-3f8e3fa2067e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277658} :answer-field/id #uuid "39edfbf2-3c27-5738-a40c-4f4be0593c2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99aeb9ca-3424-5f18-af86-89763375eaeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161125" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5369264a9ab9328449bafd6fd3dcda1a70b7d90cbaf4f9815211e0cc3873fc0.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=3$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=0,$ and $4x+5z=15.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II and statement III are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}.$\n\n![](images/ed/edea8af334c3362f583fcb86709150690eeec79a54185b67d242bfa14a8e4c75.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcc1bf5-5eea-5615-8b16-b8ced5de7984" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095c811e-7f7e-55f1-9a53-3a726b6a583d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186275557} {:answer/id #uuid "16dedc69-1d68-531a-b752-ea24759199f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186275689} {:answer/id #uuid "200d7982-f074-5588-a29b-ac678935f61b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186275773} {:answer/id #uuid "4682dce0-636b-5a89-b9af-41c9b4bfd09c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276101} {:answer/id #uuid "c8224cb2-c877-5bbf-9356-20367915e882" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "095c811e-7f7e-55f1-9a53-3a726b6a583d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186275557} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fdf8ee1-1c5b-54d1-ab38-980e9777e5f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83e332b2-7a45-565f-946e-09fe8f179dcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161247" :question/problem "![](images/60/60f622b161dbfb4da1d18e85d3eb3ab4e02c2af418f9845e05a3bf9704a28006.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $y=0$, $y=3$, $z=0$, $z=2$, $x=0,$ and $x=2+y-z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=2-y-z$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=2+y-z$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=5$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=2+y-z.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2af4029bd979d250ed15f351ef7f14d3ae688806db4ffec199a24b3591dadac.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dea8612-414c-5394-869d-1e404b048156" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type III Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037775b6-1f4b-5fc7-ad27-3b9ed81d566b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186275508} {:answer/id #uuid "57cf12bf-b36b-5b35-b186-38013f9ab479" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276264} {:answer/id #uuid "77a6fad8-1bdc-5445-8029-fd6f8eab32c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276560} {:answer/id #uuid "9609356f-5284-5c5c-9629-638c0cfb6067" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276826} {:answer/id #uuid "c40bff88-ef73-5e21-b5af-f52702082670" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57cf12bf-b36b-5b35-b186-38013f9ab479" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276264} :answer-field/id #uuid "155bc155-bfbc-5d0d-80ef-742677d10e35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d0077c3-5809-5281-a04f-def492b7900c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161248" :question/problem "![](images/40/40091bf7a66577a4a3f3b8cdf3b5da34cbe0e0198fdc5731c6d3dae8cb727418.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $z=0$, $z=3$, $y=0,$ and $y=4-z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=4-z$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=4-z$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=4$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. We have\n\n- the *left* surface $y={f}_{1}(x,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *right* surface $y={f}_{2}(x,z)=4-z.$\n\n![](images/b1/b1046e312a3783dfc3253646f8a71ae226a173ac882a6305850bdd81aed9dd7e.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dea8612-414c-5394-869d-1e404b048156" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type III Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b5fc6c4-6a34-5c6d-9659-e6f6b741bbce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186275731} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb27137-b383-524b-854c-5b360412a95f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276141} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8169d8-458c-5b8f-bcce-d1900c707665" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186276482} {:answer/id #uuid "90b58434-11b5-529f-831b-dda8ce34d98b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276789} {:answer/id #uuid "abf8205d-26ef-5b96-8adb-5e49c300676a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b5fc6c4-6a34-5c6d-9659-e6f6b741bbce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186275731} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6404de6-851e-554a-b5db-76be3d0e774e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bbc7b8c-0af0-5315-ba58-db2c9cd43206" :question/math-academy-id "q-161250" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d447e5a00aa324d83a3f513db11ff677fd2db2c7b1b33d8bc2bcfa73b4a08f3f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=3$, $z=0$, $z=2$, $y=0,$ and $y=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=z-\\frac{x}{3}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=3$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. We have\n\n- the *left* surface $y={f}_{1}(x,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *right* surface $y={f}_{2}(x,z)=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z.$\n\n![](images/88/88b5fde383f094436dd94adefa7912f1a194319739fdb0b8a6a9a74693362219.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcc1bf5-5eea-5615-8b16-b8ced5de7984" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8653b6c4-8467-597d-be8d-ba8d3a520d3a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276682} {:answer/id #uuid "9dac6dbf-e687-5240-8a96-e42d47819580" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276892} {:answer/id #uuid "b8bf702c-d63d-5794-9819-34f3987f3ae2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277115} {:answer/id #uuid "c9935fad-acb8-5afc-adc8-1171a3d5ddca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186277266} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fd63aa-c97a-5d76-9a54-d44d8fc1faab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186277384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8bf702c-d63d-5794-9819-34f3987f3ae2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277115} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc1d0c1c-3d5e-5115-bd79-aad5247359fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59f1a985-1f99-5d8b-a66e-0643cac8da20" :question/math-academy-id "q-161361" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7799ca5801bf019fc5b884590575346a8f298f8e9c91056c13a60c4dd23742d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $y=0$, $y=4$, $z=0$, $z=3$, $x=0,$ and $x=4-y+2z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=4-y+2z$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=4+y-2z$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=4$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true and statement III is false. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=4-y+2z.$\n\n![](images/3b/3bf8e141067fb6f675933f52e465114d8909d951f6ac85fb09c8dda7efbc2f38.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51545767-3cc2-5437-9f48-638348ea4cb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276205} {:answer/id #uuid "65e476b0-8d46-5284-adff-7ab3dfc01d42" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276391} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5f5c40-cedb-5337-a6d5-fdb5d40c8667" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186276728} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbc21fd-75c6-57b1-b5df-f0a1cd6cf1c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277540} {:answer/id #uuid "fbea97be-23e3-55aa-9854-424f79b2abcb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65e476b0-8d46-5284-adff-7ab3dfc01d42" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276391} :answer-field/id #uuid "79c6778a-20db-511f-af8b-42a6afc7a710" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be0484aa-ac87-5330-9ede-cd54e1386a15" :question/math-academy-id "q-161625" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge 2-x\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\nII. $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\nIII. $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cdca164d343d8bff4c78a1e366e52c588ca6c54dfdea61acea1900bcbdc79f31.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements I and II are false. As we have seen, $A$ contains all its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ec5642-511f-525b-ac0a-770318939699" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186275681} {:answer/id #uuid "7623462b-d6fb-53e4-a6d0-244ab142865f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276543} {:answer/id #uuid "81e99452-886a-5c7a-8df1-ffa01b9aedd0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186276649} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ea606-4938-581a-b235-1d5a03d8ffb2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186276771} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b651c8-5a34-50dd-b948-339f9951003b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b651c8-5a34-50dd-b948-339f9951003b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186277642} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2fcbcb7-ae99-53f0-9014-81699073744c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fb05970-59dc-5904-9b90-0c1d735b4269" :question/math-academy-id "q-161638" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\le 3}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<2-{x}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88fe12a7-69f1-5681-a35b-e338f545c46e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276713} {:answer/id #uuid "c2180e8f-4b9a-5aa2-9740-6862795df816" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277202} {:answer/id #uuid "ce490ba2-fe22-5d7a-91b3-06c3ed53dbda" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277303} {:answer/id #uuid "f2aa9c7c-2f3a-5bb8-ad00-d6a3b212e70b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186277605} {:answer/id #uuid "f3cb1adb-4be0-5edc-b104-f304ae26284e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3cb1adb-4be0-5edc-b104-f304ae26284e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186277613} :answer-field/id #uuid "0499070a-047d-5992-bdca-50f8c7b2a165" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20984d3c-4813-50f9-a16b-ba04fcaf3833" :question/math-academy-id "q-161640" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-3<x<-1,\\,1<y<0.5x+3\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<2\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186278578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6099c711-d3a9-564f-bb4a-99eabc4151f6" :answer/type {:db/id 1759218604