lse. Rather, it will give an instruction.\n\n- Option II is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the given statements.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186284748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a57218-72df-52b7-a7c9-b44507555a2a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186281924} {:answer/id #uuid "236f86aa-fa00-5096-a10a-e7651f6a6f6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186282002} {:answer/id #uuid "44f8d700-4e7f-5f15-911f-34b6d469a632" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186282274} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2190ba-be25-51f1-8832-021adf3dcc68" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186282623} {:answer/id #uuid "8f930388-ab9f-54e0-8499-291a03111580" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186282934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f930388-ab9f-54e0-8499-291a03111580" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186282934} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d6f2115-fb0f-5808-a58b-b7b09276ab3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186284062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e67e934-1049-5a47-aaca-94c1d2a3bfc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-177118" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186284756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9017b4e-ddb3-56f6-ba30-b494a7e4e09f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "461a316f-8095-5736-a73f-14c89cacc5a0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186282280} {:answer/id #uuid "60dad401-055a-535d-9243-9223840fe1a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186282513} {:answer/id #uuid "625e4385-042e-59ce-9595-8b37d9481c18" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements" :db/id 17592186282530} {:answer/id #uuid "df573c7c-da56-514d-981d-9b61682ec296" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186283638} {:answer/id #uuid "e47fc87c-53d2-5dec-afc7-2330ca8585f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186283680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e47fc87c-53d2-5dec-afc7-2330ca8585f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186283680} :answer-field/id #uuid "85eebd06-4496-587a-9e01-4fe697a9b331" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186284163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71c46d34-a9b9-54b7-8de2-8c7deffeb5f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-177667" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a square matrix\n- ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I$\n- $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The number of rows in $A$ is not the same as the number of columns. So, it's not a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Note that\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\cdot A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not a square matrix, our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186284761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112ffdc2-a1bc-5e4f-a55e-3a3c199d4142" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186281850} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbcca28-caa9-5452-900e-afa2fcabbb83" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186282085} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c77468-c43e-5f90-958c-881c1124c048" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186283228} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3faa70-e43f-5d34-b985-b24b5369ef6e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186283332} {:answer/id #uuid "f769c33e-f251-51ba-853e-fdc6f85daede" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186283854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f769c33e-f251-51ba-853e-fdc6f85daede" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186283854} :answer-field/id #uuid "09eb1e7f-4a4b-57de-a6db-ec536584eab6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186283951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9ed15ab-e314-5efe-ab81-8f51e9de96ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-178026" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{12}{25}+\\frac{12}{25}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $Q({u}_{1})$ and $Q({u}_{2})$ are unit vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q({u}_{1})‖ & =‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}+(\\frac{3}{5})^{2}}=1 \\\\ ‖Q({u}_{2})‖ & =‖{u}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{(-\\frac{3}{5})^{2}+(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186284764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109b4732-b4ed-5dc2-93e5-fc6df0a79524" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186281840} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf7ac07-9b77-5d6c-81f0-2fec01a30a87" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186282615} {:answer/id #uuid "763f9ce9-b22a-5b37-9a0d-3ba1d6d6b05a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186282700} {:answer/id #uuid "8b60eee0-9316-537c-a9cb-8496a8579979" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186282892} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcfed0c-7946-5790-89c2-4a9efeb3a303" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186283894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbcfed0c-7946-5790-89c2-4a9efeb3a303" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186283894} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c273430-736d-52ef-99e7-8d6ba618f911" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186284014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e16f6fa7-ae80-51bb-bdf3-1add916dcb13" :question/math-academy-id "q-178269" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=6+3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b304527c-5cdc-57df-b118-e555f6f8fa5c" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Schematic for a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c32748d-53f6-5219-89bf-1987877f591c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186284889} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb4fab8-bfa7-5791-960b-b5311170930d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186285221} {:answer/id #uuid "33b8f7d4-18ee-584b-92d5-7808bb34733b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186285264} {:answer/id #uuid "a45d8439-8e2a-50c8-9941-f89c6232644e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186286294} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2217f2-fb07-5826-ae20-a1d1452f2af0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186286378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a45d8439-8e2a-50c8-9941-f89c6232644e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186286294} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f04a716-8f4c-5681-944d-09ddbe53e434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b608e6a0-7a3f-5af5-a662-35948e07de5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-181279" :question/problem "![](images/08/08754e159a2b204fce6795292c56fb35dd497dc08611ffcbe9d379e1355ab4af.png)\n\nBy finding the missing entries in the schematic shown above for $A\\times B,$ where $A={x,y}$ and $B={0,1,2},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$\n- $(x,2)\\in A\\times B$\n- $(1,y)\\in A\\times B$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times B,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B$ at each intersection.\n\n![](images/c3/c33000e8aa6c217b5607e43e961aaea10586b615d633dc1b59cc598e2f7af56c.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- $(x,y)\\notin A\\times B\\,\\times $\n\n- $(x,2)\\in A\\times B\\,✓$\n\n- $(1,y)\\notin A\\times B\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3de9e532-559c-5fc1-a6ad-5fcd1ed77824" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Special Sets Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2229f041-75b6-5428-b04d-fd84c93cb5e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186285093} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa079d8-f342-59fb-874e-748917b910c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id 17592186285160} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbd27c8-d452-5178-8397-fa2634b6a18a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$N,Z,Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id 17592186285180} {:answer/id #uuid "644bb48c-c27b-5ce2-a851-38f1b9ca47e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id 17592186285700} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e53b22-5207-5841-bbbc-6c0acb34d78c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186286763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2229f041-75b6-5428-b04d-fd84c93cb5e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186285093} :answer-field/id #uuid "134c758b-4957-51be-95f8-4fc378eea4fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fcd97869-92b9-51fc-a566-7b09c1bf865d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181522" :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $\\sqrt{-4}$ belong?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- First, we note that $\\sqrt{-4}=2\\text{i}$ is a complex number. Therefore, we can write\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{-4}\\in C.\n$$\n\n- Since the imaginary part of $\\sqrt{-4}$ is nonzero, it does *not* belong to $N,Z,Q,$ or $R.$ We can write\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{-4}\\notin N,\\,\\sqrt{-4}\\notin Z,\\,\\sqrt{-4}\\notin Q,\\,\\sqrt{-4}\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d132801-b9cc-5c0c-a8fa-0c8b840c05bd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "663ed259-6683-5942-9113-3212b3b9965c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186285713} {:answer/id #uuid "937971bd-e796-5103-bbcf-2a5981185613" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186286130} {:answer/id #uuid "967f2de7-fc18-579d-8e31-31e89c1e8a76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186286155} {:answer/id #uuid "e0509c1f-9a2e-5ec7-bc1e-a4e57005dbc4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186286830} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fb0ac1-b46b-5f85-a67a-12bbdc222ef7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186287050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6fb0ac1-b46b-5f85-a67a-12bbdc222ef7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186287050} :answer-field/id #uuid "309eca38-c8d0-544b-8ab8-dabc935a86c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e51e86f2-eaf4-5f67-aebb-f55a23118243" :question/math-academy-id "q-181544" :question/problem "To which of the following sets does the number $-\\pi $ belong?\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$\n- $R$\n- $Q$\n- $C$" :question/worked-solution "The number $-\\pi $ is not a rational number. Consequently, $-\\pi $ does not belong to any of the sets  ${Q}^{-}$ nor $Q.$\n\nHowever, $-\\pi $ is a real number, so it belongs to $R.$\n\nFinally, since every real number is also a complex number, $-\\pi $ also belongs to $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d132801-b9cc-5c0c-a8fa-0c8b840c05bd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d305da1-10d6-5355-9cb5-30cb01bd3454" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186284896} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9b260b-9d22-5a49-84aa-81da5ff954e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186285480} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf800de-d960-55be-8e26-b8ab08e882bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186285641} {:answer/id #uuid "c53a58bf-a01a-5b0a-966d-3fa8b70d62cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186286595} {:answer/id #uuid "c5553184-f1f9-5273-ac8e-210c2376e7f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186286597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c53a58bf-a01a-5b0a-966d-3fa8b70d62cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186286595} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaca06d1-7c53-5668-966d-e6c98a240158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e2347db-9785-54d2-a2a4-893dc1f56ec9" :question/math-academy-id "q-181545" :question/problem "To which of the following sets does the number $\\frac{1}{\\pi }$ belong?\n\n- $Z$\n- ${Q}^{+}$\n- $Q$\n- $R$" :question/worked-solution "The number $\\frac{1}{\\pi }$ is not an integer nor a rational number. Consequently, $\\frac{1}{\\pi }$ does not belong to any of the sets $Z,$ ${Q}^{+},$ nor $Q.$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{1}{\\pi }$ is a real number, so it belongs to $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15eae42a-84d3-5af4-9723-ca2e6821bd94" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186284984} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc5b7e6-5ceb-5502-967e-b2e4311ce926" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186285072} {:answer/id #uuid "51d3a5cb-ab59-516d-82c8-bb3f3b8fa2b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186285541} {:answer/id #uuid "c67d60a5-4f36-5417-b8c0-6c359323b2d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186286610} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a52de5-ebd4-528c-a391-a6e41b264435" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186286695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51d3a5cb-ab59-516d-82c8-bb3f3b8fa2b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186285541} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4557020-2974-5580-8d39-25a92c52ddd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b09e4d7d-54df-5b2a-b13d-108c786b3198" :question/math-academy-id "q-181643" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x-1})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{-2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x/2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{x-1}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-2x})=-2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{-2x})=4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-2x})=-8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={{e}^{-2x},-2{e}^{-2x},4{e}^{-2x},-8{e}^{-2x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{x/2},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36a60e37-2471-5aa2-b57a-1481a0f6284d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186285298} {:answer/id #uuid "94ce2f0a-5b1b-5d86-8907-3894fa6c197b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186286141} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a848df-7548-5571-afb1-68281340115c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186286309} {:answer/id #uuid "bafe69e6-c6fa-50d5-8f8e-35701bfd258b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186286489} {:answer/id #uuid "e369fe1e-2605-5bb4-9f07-207e66002d01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186286866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6a848df-7548-5571-afb1-68281340115c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186286309} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c53cb0-8edb-512b-93f2-6deb1470cc08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "614d2565-08b2-5cdc-9df9-02ccb7032ebd" :question/math-academy-id "q-181650" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A=\\{{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,…\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains four elements:\n\n$$\nB=\\{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=2{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC=\\{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,2{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,…\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45239c87-b18f-5242-9853-f41f8f4874b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186285418} {:answer/id #uuid "573454a9-84bc-5580-a25d-f506a568c8ac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186285589} {:answer/id #uuid "780e8763-99bf-5416-800d-163ae64ab60f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186285907} {:answer/id #uuid "afaf5c4e-80d0-56aa-acae-ea3fd789d969" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186286388} {:answer/id #uuid "f372104c-f20b-5b83-84aa-93dd31a6cd3a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186287017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45239c87-b18f-5242-9853-f41f8f4874b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186285418} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6a71da9-8273-58bf-8716-79867df875a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "135e3cdb-fb83-5539-93de-65d28076eb80" :question/math-academy-id "q-181652" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({sec}^{2}⁡x)=2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,{sec}^{2}⁡x,2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is also infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)=-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x,-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains four elements:\n\n$$\nC={3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C|=4,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $B$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55b08ff5-bfcc-5989-8c71-5e69e9422989" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186285575} {:answer/id #uuid "67de69fa-1e6f-5a32-b489-7c59b5e3dfce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186285738} {:answer/id #uuid "a903f83b-b5c3-56a7-a880-f26f71d8db64" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id 17592186286328} {:answer/id #uuid "b88702cb-8ca5-51bc-b377-a6219664838b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186286471} {:answer/id #uuid "eb784300-190f-5a99-8906-929fd5a165b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186286944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a903f83b-b5c3-56a7-a880-f26f71d8db64" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id 17592186286328} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a75d6dc-29d8-58ed-bb9f-8a06bf5ab301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f218f2c-c350-5f75-a74b-659b486077d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-181674" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=0}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=0}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x\\pi }{2} & =m\\pi  \\\\ x & =2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,0,2,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Since $A$ is infinite, and $A⊆B,$ it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d553d05-8487-5735-9096-0b6509c082fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186285191} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1f19f1-6d18-5d75-8a3c-2c19eccd34ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186285789} {:answer/id #uuid "6efc152b-1c43-5917-9679-2a6ab86d48bf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186285818} {:answer/id #uuid "81df8ddf-8888-573b-a9c5-60409500a208" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186285979} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3cf821-82da-57d3-899f-c54449ad805e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186286209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b3cf821-82da-57d3-899f-c54449ad805e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186286209} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f0db27d-f520-566e-a6c7-2819cea98411" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c54f5b27-abdd-5f0e-891e-7fb28f946241" :question/math-academy-id "q-181702" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\frac{1}{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({x}^{3})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(4x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})=-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{1}{x},-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & ={x}^{3} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})=3{x}^{2} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})=6x \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x)=6 \\\\ {g}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6)=0 \\\\ {g}^{(5)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(0)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $n>4,$ we have ${g}^{(n)}(x)=0.$ Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\nB={{x}^{3},3{x}^{2},6x,6,0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=5,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also finite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =4x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)=4 \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4)=0 \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(0)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $n>2,$ we have ${h}^{(n)}(x)=0.$ Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\nC={4x,4,0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C|=3,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41163f20-8d3b-5907-919d-8c3fec49e7dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186285395} {:answer/id #uuid "42130499-89c8-5104-8223-7de68cee5145" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186285399} {:answer/id #uuid "42ebf907-7a90-531d-a713-8f5c139388fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186285406} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b1f4bc-f277-5418-8dcf-40610dec054b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186286572} {:answer/id #uuid "e4111ad3-8b6a-5b31-9da2-f9f49e4804c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,B,$ and $C$" :db/id 17592186286874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42130499-89c8-5104-8223-7de68cee5145" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186285399} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bfe782d-f2ff-59de-85c4-a6c163678612" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4302dd15-6000-5073-b484-7b81fc84a4c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-181719" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|3n|<10}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|3q|<10}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|3x|<10}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x|<10 \\\\ 3|x|<10 \\\\ |x|<\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{10}{3}<x<\\frac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=(-\\frac{10}{3},\\frac{10}{3}).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|3n|<10$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=7,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|3q|<10$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26ec0925-d738-54c9-871f-b146cc459e2b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186285129} {:answer/id #uuid "6045a956-a4ad-53c8-9ac0-2a66591b3a5a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,B,$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186285669} {:answer/id #uuid "8930c028-8bb5-5316-83c0-68ead489285e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186286044} {:answer/id #uuid "b398f3e2-ff4b-5483-9fb4-646de4e8068d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186286424} {:answer/id #uuid "c5db0d3a-6d24-5293-a1b9-40ed028d7434" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186286605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5db0d3a-6d24-5293-a1b9-40ed028d7434" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186286605} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8c5764a-7759-5100-a215-d81300911fce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4290422-629b-5741-89bd-a435f41aa909" :question/math-academy-id "q-181721" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|5n|\\le 4}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|5q|\\le 4}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|5x|\\le 4}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x|\\le 4 \\\\ 5|x|\\le 4 \\\\ |x|\\le \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{5}\\le x\\le \\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=[-\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{4}{5}].$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|5n|\\le 4$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|5q|\\le 4$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075d19a0-1bb0-50f9-977b-9376861a5179" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186284844} {:answer/id #uuid "67922a4d-f26a-5b62-a3b3-d988ff760bfc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186285733} {:answer/id #uuid "b512c37e-87bd-5777-abc9-9995106197e8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,B,$ and $C$" :db/id 17592186286434} {:answer/id #uuid "c0562573-1117-511e-b0ed-e25b14777bc2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186286546} {:answer/id #uuid "ea7acf61-c9a6-545a-966f-300ce8561bc0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186286935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea7acf61-c9a6-545a-966f-300ce8561bc0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186286935} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad43de07-8e87-5a5a-ab30-1f5eff9c3777" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6d13915-d6fa-5b7a-8103-f1358e92b8fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-181740" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,-{n}^{2}+1>0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,-{m}^{2}+1<0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,-{q}^{2}+1<0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)(x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-1$ and $x=1,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/06/0699524ff20f871c9eeca93faa9934832b8e5816fd23b46862e8b01d48e7d86b.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n-1<x<1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-1,1).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<-1\\,\\text{or}\\,x>1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{n}^{2}+1>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{m}^{2}+1<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={…,-3,-2}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $-{q}^{2}+1<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0df8f70d-37d2-51d1-8769-6dc393d0b548" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186284909} {:answer/id #uuid "57b16d2e-7ea7-5301-831d-37dfced8af24" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186285590} {:answer/id #uuid "74353ef9-c5ec-53a1-a641-ddfe8410543d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186285862} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac65b06-4d00-5534-b585-245486127bdb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186286062} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bae2f9-40d0-512b-b04b-46878dc3ed51" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186286996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57b16d2e-7ea7-5301-831d-37dfced8af24" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186285590} :answer-field/id #uuid "3782d53b-9ab4-51be-b256-47d16c8f4594" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29b1f7f7-72db-5315-ae82-b2adaab04321" :question/math-academy-id "q-181768" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,{n}^{2}-2n\\le 0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,{m}^{2}-2m\\ge 0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,{q}^{2}-2q\\le 0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/49/496c6d53993ca6cae499f617bca42dbc42e4c57bffa278da6312a5b1723bdcbc.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-2x\\le 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=[0,2].$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-2x\\ge 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx\\le 0\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-2n\\le 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=3,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-2m\\ge 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={…,-1,0}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-2q\\le 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22a28924-3c6d-54ea-9a3f-6a79a2680c20" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186285100} {:answer/id #uuid "24e268d9-4aab-5d16-a062-359f1a99535e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186285110} {:answer/id #uuid "2e244dec-e2f1-530c-87ad-44b58106f2cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186285197} {:answer/id #uuid "c117a24a-f0e7-5a98-9f93-b5be28219407" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186286554} {:answer/id #uuid "f891c455-3db3-51a1-bece-aab74e45482e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186287059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c117a24a-f0e7-5a98-9f93-b5be28219407" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186286554} :answer-field/id #uuid "63a8454a-367b-5fe5-b2d9-ca149e1e0897" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5ba42f9-2582-56fd-9cc1-974b79efc1dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-181941" :question/problem "If $A={a,b}$ and $B={1,2},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times A\\times B?$\n\n- $(a,a,1)$\n- $(b,a,2)$\n- $((a,b),2)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and the third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${a,b}$ with ${a,b}$ and ${1,2},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times A\\times B={ & (a,a,1),(a,a,2),(a,b,1),(a,b,2), \\\\  & (b,a,1),(b,a,2),(b,b,1),(b,b,2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|A\\times A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $(a,a,1)$ and $(b,a,2)$ are in $A\\times A\\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67e32f28-ba6c-58f8-9cde-0935c48a2104" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186285740} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7b550d-e11b-5ef6-84ad-ddc1cf3a3ff0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186285762} {:answer/id #uuid "a154c139-7cdd-5836-911f-28e304514e94" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186286261} {:answer/id #uuid "d541c411-a51e-53f1-88e9-bf18012d7b3c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186286728} {:answer/id #uuid "e83fbed6-e2ea-590b-a18d-ce8440dc2c42" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186286913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e83fbed6-e2ea-590b-a18d-ce8440dc2c42" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186286913} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0803712-7ce7-585b-8ac2-64340064603f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d67a16e-2bb4-53de-83ef-8253a6e9b7e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-181942" :question/problem "If $A={8,9}$ and $B={x,y},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times B\\times A?$\n\n- $(9,9,y)$\n- $(8,x,y)$\n- $(9,y,8)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and the third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${8,9}$ with ${x,y}$ and ${8,9},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times B\\times A={ & (8,x,8),(8,x,9),(8,y,8),(8,y,9), \\\\  & (9,x,8),(9,x,9),(9,y,8),(9,y,9)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|A\\times B\\times A|=|A|\\cdot |B|\\cdot |A|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $(9,y,8)\\in A\\times B\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "128cb112-83b8-53c9-b147-b9b4cf21ad0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186284953} {:answer/id #uuid "55a3c3fa-ef53-5a7b-8def-08dc24aa27e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186285572} {:answer/id #uuid "b35fefde-5785-511a-bb0d-c2e8e287b076" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186286422} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c89a29-29e4-5f63-a3e5-c7b69ae260d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186286732} {:answer/id #uuid "ed15f3df-877d-541a-9541-9e0febc1fa9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186286961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55a3c3fa-ef53-5a7b-8def-08dc24aa27e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186285572} :answer-field/id #uuid "9749546f-abc1-5769-b983-8820f39b6d3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3dced42a-f072-5a03-9e98-5ecc099c95d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-181945" :question/problem "If $A={v,w}$ and $B={3,4,5},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times B\\times A?$\n\n- $(v,4,5)$\n- $(v,3,w)$\n- $(w,5,w)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and the third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${v,w}$ with ${3,4,5}$ and ${v,w},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times B\\times A={ & (v,3,v),(v,3,w),(v,4,v),(v,4,w),(v,5,v),(v,5,w), \\\\  & (w,3,v),(w,3,w),(w,4,v),(w,4,w),(w,5,v),(w,5,w)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|A\\times B\\times A|=|A|\\cdot |B|\\cdot |A|=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 2=12.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $(v,3,w)$ and $(w,5,w)$ are in $A\\times B\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023726aa-bdb5-58b6-823e-174eeff1834e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186284807} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc0f0f7-8a28-509f-8f87-0882f5aea05e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186284883} {:answer/id #uuid "39e6c400-e101-5954-86c4-f61b53957456" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186285332} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a53757-c931-58a7-accb-93552c487d59" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186286561} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d1fab5-2ca9-5e50-8d02-d40e1e6f8e74" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186286844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "023726aa-bdb5-58b6-823e-174eeff1834e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186284807} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1532e6c-7c4e-56f2-a88f-452ccd1b60c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68270c3d-bf20-5dbc-8e60-99dca2522513" :question/math-academy-id "q-181947" :question/problem "If $A={p,q}$ and $B={2,4,6},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times A\\times B?$\n\n- $(p,q,2)$\n- $((p,p),4)$\n- $(q,q,6)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and the third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${p,q}$ with ${p,q}$ and ${2,4,6},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times A\\times B={ & (p,p,2),(p,p,4),(p,p,6),(p,q,2),(p,q,4),(p,q,6), \\\\  & (q,p,2),(q,p,4),(q,p,6),(q,q,2),(q,q,4),(q,q,6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|A\\times A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3=12.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $(p,q,2)$ and $(q,q,6)$ are in $A\\times A\\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b304527c-5cdc-57df-b118-e555f6f8fa5c" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Schematic for a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0293f2e6-924e-58e9-9d13-9d0d315f9002" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186284809} {:answer/id #uuid "52800430-7da9-5750-957a-fe138372d0bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186285546} {:answer/id #uuid "75d8e253-748f-5343-9c97-cbab141df228" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186285884} {:answer/id #uuid "91d4bc78-d9ae-5a68-921b-13c1a93531d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186286114} {:answer/id #uuid "a29a73b4-ea9e-577c-b53b-8adc8ec0d729" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186286280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0293f2e6-924e-58e9-9d13-9d0d315f9002" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186284809} :answer-field/id #uuid "894309b4-f9cd-553e-9900-2f55b6828938" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "acf6f0a0-0064-5b71-8b07-5e0f0eb96bda" :question/math-academy-id "q-181963" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb3e6ef6a832f2adbbc5e096bddf2c4f6a0445fbd77a71df5617298f8dad2e51.png)\n\nBy finding the missing entries in the schematic shown above for $A\\times B,$ where $A=\\{a,b,c\\}$ and $B=\\{2,4,6\\},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\nI. $(4,b)\\in A\\times B$\n\nII. $(c,6)\\in A\\times B$\n\nIII. $(a,2)\\in A\\times B$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times B,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B$ at each intersection.\n\n![](images/57/57d053cca53046f02201540e655030a37ba8f7a2811e7dc724cf30ca9fd3c072.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- $(4,b)\\notin A\\times B\\,\\times $\n\n- $(c,6)\\in A\\times B\\,✓$\n\n- $(a,2)\\in A\\times B\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09004638-b23b-5d7c-bf05-024a92db458c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186284860} {:answer/id #uuid "0ea9a8a6-d04d-5edb-bb85-9aaf504db77a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186284914} {:answer/id #uuid "30140e94-31a6-5e00-8d4d-cdfa8e87a563" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186285225} {:answer/id #uuid "8becfc26-2b75-59e1-b3ad-e02c5059b304" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186286069} {:answer/id #uuid "a01533fc-1265-5dae-9827-96be14f562b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186286246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a01533fc-1265-5dae-9827-96be14f562b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186286246} :answer-field/id #uuid "b99f6978-6f4a-52c1-a0cb-1ece2ed27467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ec96a4e-9e05-5335-919b-b747e9d81df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-181969" :question/problem "If $A=\\{p,q,r\\}$ and $B=\\{3,5\\},$ which of the following elements belong to $B\\times (A\\times A)?$\n\nI. $(5,(r,p))$\n\nII. $(q,(5,3))$\n\nIII. $(3,q,p)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${p,q,r}$ with ${p,q,r},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/aa/aad91ef7655fa8a5bf0c9852ee4e5e2166289087a56ac18e932ee3cce17164a0.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times A={(p,p),(p,q),(p,r),(q,p),(q,q),(q,r),(r,p),(r,q),(r,r)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times A|=|A|\\cdot |A|=3\\cdot 3=9.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nB\\times (A\\times A)\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $B$ and $A\\times A,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/e5/e573cecaa9333e071d980b1b03d65dafadd027506d2c2b9b6e8acfbe15d3be52.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|B\\times (A\\times A)|=|B|\\cdot |A\\times A|=2\\cdot 9=18.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $(5,(r,p))\\in B\\times (A\\times A).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b304527c-5cdc-57df-b118-e555f6f8fa5c" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Schematic for a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1232328f-69e8-55d3-887e-e4138da6c521" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186284950} {:answer/id #uuid "496e7024-3947-543e-8e38-682d25598921" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186285464} {:answer/id #uuid "562ec188-58f4-50a5-ad1e-066ac3b9f5bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186285580} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5945f9-f26c-5140-b84c-e6bf46e3d5b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186285774} {:answer/id #uuid "f0cc8d74-1e90-5911-8697-bf71e6de36f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186286997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0cc8d74-1e90-5911-8697-bf71e6de36f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186286997} :answer-field/id #uuid "510cd5a8-cfaa-5222-a99d-94d5d42ccfe4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffb21c72-0872-5ee1-9c14-274c6c7f743e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181975" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf1c37a92b184cb3e6c81bbd2d653008f5eff0e3b2131f2f297a336881661c22.png)\n\nUsing the visual schematic above for the Cartesian product ${A}^{2}$ for $A={p,q,r},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(q,q)\\in {A}^{2}$\n- $(p,r)\\in {A}^{2}$\n- ${q}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The set ${A}^{2}$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A\\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times A,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set $(A)$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set $(A)$ at each intersection.\n\n![](images/91/919ad6e16dcd1d8f02e8c15c7bd1dfa15f67232c99706dfbc1239e1f9a0f99e5.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- $(q,q)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\n- $(p,r)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\n- ${q}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f7aafa-48da-5a9a-8c4e-2db357b4d84d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186285018} {:answer/id #uuid "80d7f3e3-c855-5354-90bd-482a3a29e8fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186285972} {:answer/id #uuid "ac89eae1-7a02-5a20-8e6e-78af9e33af11" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186286365} {:answer/id #uuid "dd658f81-0ef3-5121-9761-f8e1ea69f717" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186286802} {:answer/id #uuid "eb312ff4-8754-5481-8f71-6c35c1ce24a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186286941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19f7aafa-48da-5a9a-8c4e-2db357b4d84d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186285018} :answer-field/id #uuid "a96dd43b-afeb-5167-94cd-5072d0236b88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e14f595-c123-5de8-8c3d-4afeb7095ae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-181977" :question/problem "If $A={4,8}$ and $B={x,y,z},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times (A\\times B)?$\n\n- $(4,(8,z))$\n- $(8,(8,x))$\n- $(8,4,y)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${4,8}$ with ${x,y,z},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/72/72feee192b949dcce7b0f10f4e1d772c809bcf73de6c66f15c7de75a4a1ff672.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(4,x),(4,y),(4,z),(8,x),(8,y),(8,z)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nA\\times (A\\times B)\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A$ and $A\\times B,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/1a/1ae41857d4aee82a2a4ad838c20bbe72b05a1505a5f4df3aa4a86fda0fff0612.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times (A\\times B)|=|A|\\cdot |A\\times B|=2\\cdot 6=12.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $(4,(8,z))$ and $(8,(8,x))$ are in $A\\times \\text{\\(}A\\times B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b304527c-5cdc-57df-b118-e555f6f8fa5c" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Schematic for a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e49d978-c74c-5053-916f-da9e91cd465a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186285652} {:answer/id #uuid "66e4ba73-987f-567f-b03a-1a4788dc8fb6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186285726} {:answer/id #uuid "755e41be-466c-54bc-814e-78ded8856969" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186285872} {:answer/id #uuid "85fff24e-7ebb-59f7-a8eb-f0c5e73cb140" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186286020} {:answer/id #uuid "df7f61d1-2b33-51b8-ac5f-36611eacb313" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186286827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66e4ba73-987f-567f-b03a-1a4788dc8fb6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186285726} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7cd1dc3-fc29-53e9-9ccf-cd951624e0eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16f7d99c-b5b7-5532-9552-dac57833067d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181978" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d6ee87bd861491394c6643679f62cb4b94436bf2777bd2436b3169e779736e2c.png)\n\nUsing the visual schematic above for the Cartesian product ${A}^{2}$ for $A={h,k},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- ${h}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}$\n- $(h,k)\\in {A}^{2}$\n- $(k,k)\\in {A}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The set ${A}^{2}$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A\\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times A,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set $(A)$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set $(A)$ at each intersection.\n\n![](images/50/507f8f6306c309d5e894cfb1dabfc2019f371911f7878b196e1f329f1b37dfa6.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- ${h}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}\\,\\times $\n\n- $(h,k)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\n- $(k,k)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6128eca-7f76-5386-9d67-d81db2943eeb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b32442c-08ba-5d50-8370-b4f7bb2844ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186285346} {:answer/id #uuid "45feb55c-e7bb-5ec4-bd19-6ccbce9dd40d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186285428} {:answer/id #uuid "5938940a-29bf-5630-bd36-aefb018067d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186285604} {:answer/id #uuid "701d78ff-0afb-5095-8913-1e2f45fedc33" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186285824} {:answer/id #uuid "f258fef0-e676-5a91-90bd-704c2797ac3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186287010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45feb55c-e7bb-5ec4-bd19-6ccbce9dd40d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186285428} :answer-field/id #uuid "26fdac77-4c39-59a1-bd83-eb6b67c2475f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b374349-dba8-556c-833d-28419c5d38a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-182011" :question/problem "For the sets $X=\\{x,z\\},$ $Y=\\{a,b\\},$ and $Z=X\\times Y,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\nI. $(a,x)\\in Z$\n\nII. $(z,a)\\in Z$\n\nIII. $(z,z)\\in Z$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $X\\times Y$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Y.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $X\\times Y$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b73e7aeb017bd9f7c50f7d110c7933763834eb391ac4a3e97d25f4e587a8a3d4.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(x,a),(x,b),(z,a),(z,b)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186287794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6128eca-7f76-5386-9d67-d81db2943eeb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185bd08d-601b-5996-aa46-119809fc0e9b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186285004} {:answer/id #uuid "6b25454a-379d-514d-9495-201f4fb2f25e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186285772} {:answer/id #uuid "d8592ff8-f46b-59c6-8155-1f6eed956f9d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186286757} {:answer/id #uuid "e113c0d2-d5af-5904-9bb3-8262221f8793" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186286835} {:answer/id #uuid "efc94a9f-df3c-552c-910e-e0e725d8b58f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186286986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b25454a-379d-514d-9495-201f4fb2f25e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186285772} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5236379-f233-5a90-9c31-1e6ca30f35bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "700eeff1-f170-54bf-ba40-d6fc6885d99f" :question/math-academy-id "q-182020" :question/problem "For the sets $Z={t,u}$ and $Y=Z\\times Z,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(u,t)\\in Y$\n- $(u,t,u)\\in Y$\n- $({t}^{2},{u}^{2})\\in Y$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $Z\\times Z$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $Z$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Z.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${Z}^{2}=Z\\times Z$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa14e54c0518f8f5e64d3ccb4f725bd0b3804fb197d7b90320398ebbf09d68d4.png)\n\nHence, the set $Y=Z\\times Z$ is given by\n\n$$\nY={(t,t),(t,u)(u,t),(u,u)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186290259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7362ff-a436-5cb6-9204-3010f624b437" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186288118} {:answer/id #uuid "453e0607-d5d2-538c-a3c2-e1a9e1e7a2f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186288503} {:answer/id #uuid "68d8bff0-cd45-560e-83b3-2bf7502b8689" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186288766} {:answer/id #uuid "7984ae98-d07e-5a40-926d-f57b4c7945eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186288869} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd2e152-3e72-5590-a016-28add032a5e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186289383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7984ae98-d07e-5a40-926d-f57b4c7945eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186288869} :answer-field/id #uuid "78352eff-37c5-5d6a-bb54-329102c8e509" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186289992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "206675d2-b06e-5deb-b30d-ae541d334107" :question/math-academy-id "q-20983" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+24x.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(2)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(2)=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=2$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+24x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-24x+24.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12x-24.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =12\\cdot 2-24=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-24x+24 & =0 \\\\ 6(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=2.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=1,3$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =6(1)^{2}-24(1)+24=6>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =6(3)^{2}-24(3)+24=6>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=2$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186290271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263a9ab0-0b2b-5f98-b08a-75e35a2b5402" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186288283} {:answer/id #uuid "2da74dc2-9cc7-5196-b1ef-75839c2e3d62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186288338} {:answer/id #uuid "8107db4e-6fee-5bbf-b090-80b86be25791" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186288902} {:answer/id #uuid "f3795410-4f80-5f8e-952a-15e81fad9a52" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186289640} {:answer/id #uuid "fb629756-ffa8-5582-a501-069e5ac41c24" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186289690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "263a9ab0-0b2b-5f98-b08a-75e35a2b5402" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186288283} :answer-field/id #uuid "270e9673-2204-5d44-bf5e-040805890aed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186289821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1e5b57c-3102-59cd-a153-f7966b8c71f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-21066" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,2)$, $(-1,1)$ and $(3,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(12,6)$\n- $(4,2)$\n- $(4,6)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(12,6)$, $(4,2)$ and $(12,6).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186290273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fd03eed-5160-504d-92b3-9fc4a9929faa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186288701} {:answer/id #uuid "b5444ca4-73fe-5b10-9da7-36fef390b188" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186289230} {:answer/id #uuid "c3cd9c3c-09f6-5fe6-b2f9-0e4a932e7829" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186289325} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0e1181-c323-5aff-a724-e6ea077e013b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186289407} {:answer/id #uuid "fab1568a-9d80-55a3-b772-c365ab8fdb93" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186289682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce0e1181-c323-5aff-a724-e6ea077e013b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186289407} :answer-field/id #uuid "db73644e-7f77-54f1-91db-8d3791e00546" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186290199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9622b4aa-6606-5dbb-91d5-63f920f6e03c" :question/math-academy-id "q-21068" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,2)$, $(-1,2)$ and $(-1,0)$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(16,8)$\n- $(8,2)$\n- $(-4,4)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$ \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(16,8)$, $(8,4)$ and $(-4,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186290309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43c12eb3-55f1-5542-9408-dd989e93c601" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288494} {:answer/id #uuid "496b50b2-2381-5c0a-a5ec-57b315ce03f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186288541} {:answer/id #uuid "a6aed7f1-0992-5dbd-9e5b-68b34683d114" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186289136} {:answer/id #uuid "e671bd82-67fb-5076-a0c6-d9a760f7d607" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186289562} {:answer/id #uuid "fae129e1-58dc-5648-9f67-66fc8378cba1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186289684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6aed7f1-0992-5dbd-9e5b-68b34683d114" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186289136} :answer-field/id #uuid "c77a77dc-758e-557f-a490-659cf8cc382d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186290162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48699d28-6189-5c8c-ac56-4e78a856e54a" :question/math-academy-id "q-21500" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-3{x}^{5/2}+5{x}^{3/2}+15$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=1$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{5/2}+5{x}^{3/2}+15,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3/2}+5\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}=-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2}(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}=-\\frac{45}{4}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{-1/2},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(1)=-\\frac{45}{4}(1)^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{4}(1)^{-1/2}=-\\frac{15}{2}<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=1$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186290310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a643fc-b865-5596-8f40-9701cd340073" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186288174} {:answer/id #uuid "710f998a-91e7-56d8-bc7d-70a603ad78c7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186288819} {:answer/id #uuid "cc280798-2e6b-5ad9-add1-5725b15e8da0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186289389} {:answer/id #uuid "d3960910-1995-56c5-b003-a5bddf4a4554" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186289433} {:answer/id #uuid "feca9e5b-047c-5d2d-861d-f5037ef6a710" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186289717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feca9e5b-047c-5d2d-861d-f5037ef6a710" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186289717} :answer-field/id #uuid "e49dfb32-835f-597e-bf8e-067b148c9379" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186290214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b9fc172-9b76-5d55-bed8-430ab7579f16" :question/math-academy-id "q-21504" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+36x-48,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=3$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=3$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+36x-48,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-12x+36,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x+36 & =0 \\\\ -12(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=3.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-12,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(3)=-12<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=3,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=3$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=3\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186290363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172441b4-d434-5708-a4a6-ef5a3be8199c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186288186} {:answer/id #uuid "23e10618-8683-5f0d-95cc-80d118d3ff12" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186288259} {:answer/id #uuid "57f0af3a-fe31-5542-a4b2-9b4f3ee54df4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288644} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4873fc-0aa3-5e53-a988-44237de9e1e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186288783} {:answer/id #uuid "d33d546d-1cbe-56d3-a508-703423641292" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186289431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57f0af3a-fe31-5542-a4b2-9b4f3ee54df4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288644} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e068c60-158a-57de-beed-8c11e09a8d10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186289973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2b37341-4b83-58cd-8078-617f2c8f3e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-22011" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-6$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(-6)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-6$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=-6$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=36{x}^{2}-{x}^{4},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2}-{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}(x+6)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =-6,0,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-6,0,6.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=-6.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=72x-4{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-6$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-6)=432>0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is positive at $x=-6,$ so this critical point is a relative *minimum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=-6$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-6)<0\\,\\times $\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *minimum* at $x=-6\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186290364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "060f212a-f478-5952-bd6f-7bf7f70e10d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186288077} {:answer/id #uuid "062633c1-4565-58df-8006-f852b8d73ae6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186288080} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2a9169-4d25-5c53-85fa-0c1c8b93a42b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186288696} {:answer/id #uuid "72388a42-fb86-50b2-96bf-d70a348cf382" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288827} {:answer/id #uuid "ff535e30-6ddf-5160-bc91-b1ca4d83420f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186289722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72388a42-fb86-50b2-96bf-d70a348cf382" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288827} :answer-field/id #uuid "183b3786-4390-561d-92eb-b9e0cde29dee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186289789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "247bae04-64ae-5dcd-80c8-d60760babf45" :question/math-academy-id "q-22015" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-10{x}^{3}+9,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(\\sqrt{2})<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=\\sqrt{2}$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-10{x}^{3}+9,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=15{x}^{4}-30{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{x}^{4}-30{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 15{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 15{x}^{2}(x+\\sqrt{2})(x-\\sqrt{2}) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{2},0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2},0.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=60{x}^{3}-60x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\sqrt{2})=60(\\sqrt{2})^{3}-60\\sqrt{2}=60\\sqrt{2}>0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is positive at $x=\\sqrt{2},$ so this critical point is a relative *minimum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(\\sqrt{2})>0\\,\\times $\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *minimum* at $x=\\sqrt{2}\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186290365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2a2818-f16a-5ca3-a33e-8cb2e00a9471" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186288120} {:answer/id #uuid "2d7c83d5-55ec-506f-87b9-7ecf2d7cbda9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186288336} {:answer/id #uuid "58f5aa1b-cd48-548e-9168-e16448b4505c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186288654} {:answer/id #uuid "62842dd6-40b1-5bf6-aa93-c01869c60a63" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186288720} {:answer/id #uuid "6342d3c9-7eaa-52a1-930c-3b1ad9bb7e70" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c2a2818-f16a-5ca3-a33e-8cb2e00a9471" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186288120} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7f6d5e2-3ce5-5110-a9e4-a19c7f7c0f86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186290163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfe05852-ce59-5bf2-a32a-e36a882c576e" :question/math-academy-id "q-22016" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+36x+12.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+36x+12,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-60x+36.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=36{x}^{2}+24x-60.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =36(1)^{2}+24(1)-60=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-60x+36 & =0 \\\\ 12({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-5x+3) & =0 \\\\ 12(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-3) & =0 \\\\ 12(x-1)(x-1)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-4) & =12(-4-1)^{2}(-4+3)=-300<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =12(0-1)^{2}(0+3)=36>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =12(2-1)^{2}(2+3)=60>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nAt $x=1,$  the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=1$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186290373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64787f14-ad0d-5964-aa88-af6f60cccc76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288742} {:answer/id #uuid "900d7d85-82c9-56dd-839d-06e06ab0b9a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186288996} {:answer/id #uuid "9beb6b1c-b3ec-5d27-9d13-316ccc11a3eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186289081} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c1970d-b060-543e-b891-38f272029a3a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186289429} {:answer/id #uuid "feef4d35-b938-5844-b8d1-240a2a629fb0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186289720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64787f14-ad0d-5964-aa88-af6f60cccc76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288742} :answer-field/id #uuid "c40ca960-9de3-5f92-ba75-0c68235b7752" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186290151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e347cb6-d962-5b7a-b93a-2a8ccafaafbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-22080" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)={x}^{5}-5{x}^{3}+15,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\sqrt{3}$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(\\sqrt{3})<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=\\sqrt{3}$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)={x}^{5}-5{x}^{3}+15,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{4}-15{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{4}-15{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{3},0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{3},0.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=20{x}^{3}-30x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\sqrt{3})=20(\\sqrt{3})^{3}-30\\sqrt{3}=30\\sqrt{3}>0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is positive at $x=\\sqrt{3},$ so this critical point is a relative *minimum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=\\sqrt{3}$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(\\sqrt{3})<0\\,\\times $\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *minimum* at $x=\\sqrt{3}\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186290410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21deba8a-f027-5afa-9698-5e0e3100a74a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186288249} {:answer/id #uuid "54ee7c20-cc6b-5dac-87a7-e444cd5b34f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186288619} {:answer/id #uuid "85e837ed-b68a-5505-9006-09bcfdbccf93" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186288937} {:answer/id #uuid "9e132901-959d-5961-87e5-0d1fc02806a0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186289092} {:answer/id #uuid "c1420df9-f8f0-581b-a863-ab43fc0dec99" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186289310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21deba8a-f027-5afa-9698-5e0e3100a74a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186288249} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3bdc091-4311-530a-86dc-ff8aa9fc7d93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186290097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "527ec064-c6b0-5d4a-ac97-7dea39646f9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-22495" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi , \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186290440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04adb896-af69-53f9-996f-8c7be69fb4c2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186288066} {:answer/id #uuid "3a968a72-2876-5175-83e3-a654fd44f16b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186288437} {:answer/id #uuid "636c3ca1-d99d-5757-a094-7af9bbcd44fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288727} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b58fac-4a93-5253-ac67-bbc75bccb331" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186289480} {:answer/id #uuid "f13f1b31-b868-5297-b55a-d2aac128a689" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186289626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a968a72-2876-5175-83e3-a654fd44f16b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186288437} :answer-field/id #uuid "40f54739-84a3-5784-b147-34f4eb92278d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186289875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c687bf7b-334e-54f3-b20c-cd4bb1895323" :question/math-academy-id "q-22826" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & >0 \\\\ 2 & >\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that $x>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186290445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba0ad98-6940-502a-873c-8a589a03a499" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "686d4b22-ca2e-59f6-8e25-257d6ecd36f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186288763} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba0df17-086d-58ed-9b7d-f24d67addc85" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186288793} {:answer/id #uuid "8c35509a-2cbc-54fd-8c0c-9fea55af851d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288970} {:answer/id #uuid "a424a5d4-9cbf-5918-9c70-6a17e021eef2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186289127} {:answer/id #uuid "de93cdc8-1f7d-5919-9d5a-9e6075b24bbc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186289513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c35509a-2cbc-54fd-8c0c-9fea55af851d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288970} :answer-field/id #uuid "2db6b84c-1bcd-5e77-b158-172c24231e44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186289835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca248134-22cf-5031-924c-1fcc047804dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-22979" :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =-{95}^{∘}?$\n\n- ${625}^{∘}$\n- ${160}^{∘}$\n- $-{455}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}:$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ to $-{95}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{95}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={265}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{95}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={625}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than ${625}^{∘}.$ So we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ from $-{95}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{95}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{455}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, only ${625}^{∘}$ and $-{455}^{∘}$ are coterminal with $-{95}^{∘}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I and III only."}] [{:db/id 17592186290566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e9ad609-8d7b-5e63-94bf-7cfba3aec5ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186288460} {:answer/id #uuid "abf31e5b-9e7a-5b32-8ddf-d15f59773d12" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186289170} {:answer/id #uuid "b1aa96ca-5fb9-50d0-8d56-f3836db4b900" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186289207} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a024b9-8648-5ee6-8075-33884e637425" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186289213} {:answer/id #uuid "e6200ebf-b364-55da-8734-0d50031c5a08" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186289558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2a024b9-8648-5ee6-8075-33884e637425" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186289213} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ce7f3d-ede3-5d56-84d2-9fd025fc8f92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186289984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54523433-2b3d-5350-9453-344ec229fc6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-2469" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n\nII. $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n\nIII. ${f}^{″}(1)>0$\n\nIV. $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false while statement VI is true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186290567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2992c7-e972-5221-9225-486c82bb8e2c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186288320} {:answer/id #uuid "960ec2d8-c0c3-5467-ab3a-5e4c2284c0c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186289036} {:answer/id #uuid "c45268a8-6ed8-5a58-8765-0d9ba9986146" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186289329} {:answer/id #uuid "d80cd24f-1447-5b7c-8949-769b33ee4c97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186289469} {:answer/id #uuid "f615375e-48a5-5056-9f13-2c61679f1457" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186289656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c45268a8-6ed8-5a58-8765-0d9ba9986146" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186289329} :answer-field/id #uuid "90484cdc-753b-56f4-ad23-95386946959f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186290052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "19694b62-f531-5e43-9d81-ba1b9a788be0" :question/math-academy-id "q-2471" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-10,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(-2)<0$\n- $x=-2$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-10,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=15{x}^{2}+30x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{x}^{2}+30x & =0 \\\\ 15x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,-2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=30x+30,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =30>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =-30<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I is true while statement II is false. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is true while statement VI is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=-2,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186292543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37ac181e-f3e3-57ed-a551-0fcd69b656a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186290861} {:answer/id #uuid "7379f244-251b-516e-bb6c-6df3e9750189" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186291127} {:answer/id #uuid "85773a88-ab71-5f2f-8b24-e2364651ad65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186291200} {:answer/id #uuid "943f4957-8672-539c-a148-c12d80f86f21" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186291275} {:answer/id #uuid "db61e708-0dd2-55c4-8d75-a8f5d1daa241" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186291550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7379f244-251b-516e-bb6c-6df3e9750189" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186291127} :answer-field/id #uuid "312e558c-5174-5996-94d0-b061d662915f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186291901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c70458e-a9c0-5f53-8bd6-90f09521b402" :question/math-academy-id "q-253144" :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}+5{a}_{n-2}$\n- ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}+3{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=4{c}_{n-1}+7n$" :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $7n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186292557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dbb7da2-ea66-5b52-9302-ca4c39e2dbf0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186290695} {:answer/id #uuid "52bb7bbd-6c3d-523a-be74-00838fa298d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186290985} {:answer/id #uuid "80c32bd0-4cf5-53f8-8450-1957372f36f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186291177} {:answer/id #uuid "9f75d5fc-5716-5e77-bda7-413b14e2eeeb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186291323} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc3b14b-039b-55a1-b69c-a57544be0b6e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186291552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dbb7da2-ea66-5b52-9302-ca4c39e2dbf0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186290695} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f38d93c-f65b-5a2b-8351-7ceddf8321fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186292075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e47f465-4e25-5a70-adfe-d172f722b8f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-253467" :question/problem "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{2}+3$\n- ${b}_{n}=2{b}_{n-1}-{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}={c}_{n-1}+\\frac{1}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "The order of a recurrence relation is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${b}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${c}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${c}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186292558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00559cb2-bd4f-513b-ab1f-903cc3d78a0f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186290637} {:answer/id #uuid "0770898f-538c-58c2-a7c4-9285df1937de" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186290669} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a6a26b-c115-5b9f-9483-8468b6cc30ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186291358} {:answer/id #uuid "af09b7b4-5e81-533b-bd4e-4f8a438df128" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186291392} {:answer/id #uuid "d66f9e03-8e50-579d-ba70-ec9750819aa0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186291531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0770898f-538c-58c2-a7c4-9285df1937de" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186290669} :answer-field/id #uuid "72961321-d712-5e5e-a0d9-8d90041f3b8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186292087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1f62b55-725b-50fe-8589-865ff5ea3249" :question/math-academy-id "q-253468" :question/problem "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n\n- ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}$\n- ${b}_{n}=3{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{c}_{n-1}}{3}-2{c}_{n-3}$" :question/worked-solution "The order of a recurrence relation is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${b}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${c}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${c}_{n-1}$ and ${c}_{n-3}$ in the relation is $3.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186292560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d9f27e-7800-56b9-9226-098496e041e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186290643} {:answer/id #uuid "26d10e33-bc36-5085-b0ed-e645cd08d368" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186290792} {:answer/id #uuid "59244b09-f996-5219-a0a1-da045f107124" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186291010} {:answer/id #uuid "7c1b5d38-5442-5da3-a331-b00127071a4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186291158} {:answer/id #uuid "ded3bfdf-b944-54bb-8e12-915290f48b19" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186291566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c1b5d38-5442-5da3-a331-b00127071a4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186291158} :answer-field/id #uuid "72169963-fa38-5472-934c-e80e3348c250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186292085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6aaec8bd-79a0-54bb-bcfb-1f08b1113c88" :question/math-academy-id "q-253485" :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=3{a}_{n-1}+2{a}_{n-2}$\n- ${b}_{n}=4{b}_{n-1}+{n}^{2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=5{c}_{n-1}+6{c}_{n-2}$" :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term ${n}^{2}$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${c}_{n-1}$ and ${c}_{n-2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186299696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147498 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df3a798c-f226-5b4f-8d65-4a305de2b087" :knowledge-point/title "Checking ODEs for Exactness"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bef0bb8-217e-5b8a-a9cc-536ee2789431" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186297611} {:answer/id #uuid "0de244d0-0abc-5db8-b1f6-33c12e7e4d3c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186297619} {:answer/id #uuid "4610e6ed-8488-5ffc-ac46-2ae33e6cf7e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186297840} {:answer/id #uuid "96337b72-0ea9-503f-87e2-d8f2d39190e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186298201} {:answer/id #uuid "a14f5f06-92b3-58c2-b787-460bec0bf2f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186298263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bef0bb8-217e-5b8a-a9cc-536ee2789431" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186297611} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6bb327e-5b1c-548b-8cdb-ae1ff55bd5a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186299305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70dbdf12-3bc6-5349-9efa-62768bd9b3d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-318629" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\n- $({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+2)\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $(3xy)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+2)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}+y,\\,Q(x,y)=xy+2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+2) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n \nThus, equation I is not exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n(3xy)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3xy,\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy) \\\\  & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(y-4x)=4x-y,\\,Q(x,y)=6y-x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6y-x) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, equation III is exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186299697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147498 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df3a798c-f226-5b4f-8d65-4a305de2b087" :knowledge-point/title "Checking ODEs for Exactness"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21782e87-59b6-5912-b8b4-9fa6cf1b6342" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186297696} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc718c6-f925-5144-a9e5-17a853705577" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186297745} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0d2ffe-15cb-550f-9751-5852c9fb4b82" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186298172} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9ca69b-44e0-51bf-82e4-b60846979e58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186298456} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a11aa3-7602-58a6-84d9-d87aaf9f82e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186298527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf9ca69b-44e0-51bf-82e4-b60846979e58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186298456} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbcc345d-f8ed-56e0-8640-dbcb0a23f4e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186299381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "861223b8-6640-50cd-ba1a-46920dc4bc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-318630" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\n- $(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2xy+5,\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+7.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+5) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+7) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n \nThus, equation I is exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{2}+x,\\,Q(x,y)=xy+1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+1) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(y-2x)=2x-y,\\,Q(x,y)=5y-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5y-3x) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation III is not exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186299786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02bab4d3-6b3c-53df-9fc7-544789b896ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Y$ and $Z$ only" :db/id 17592186297570} {:answer/id #uuid "489f69c0-1a1b-59e7-8b51-291265d6c731" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id 17592186297849} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a73b98-3971-595d-8705-c31d2d15129f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ and $Y$ only" :db/id 17592186298265} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b24876-2947-57fe-8ccd-af0c39bfc822" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ and $Z$ only" :db/id 17592186298343} {:answer/id #uuid "be22a610-f1a2-5929-9a8b-4e3c27a93183" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z$ only" :db/id 17592186298383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5b24876-2947-57fe-8ccd-af0c39bfc822" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ and $Z$ only" :db/id 17592186298343} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b416bba-d53a-5be8-8e38-203a53a4ab36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186298888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cbe50951-f70c-5a66-becb-e9afd71bebc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-31968" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $W$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $Y$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $Z$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $W$ and $Z$ are elementary matrices."}] [{:db/id 17592186302077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "117151e4-84f1-51a0-ad37-2790a67d8c5f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186300033} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef8e7de-cda4-5bc2-9a75-09cd3fe231de" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186300106} {:answer/id #uuid "559acb8c-35c5-53e0-8999-681587b19763" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186300407} {:answer/id #uuid "bb256cbb-a3a8-5890-9ba0-be61ca97e9fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186300900} {:answer/id #uuid "c17033bf-5f8f-5dc5-91b3-24397815b685" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186300934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ef8e7de-cda4-5bc2-9a75-09cd3fe231de" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186300106} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a072265-5db1-5ee1-9458-23dd71204ad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186301256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "775e3d7f-2959-5adc-b3eb-ee2a98bbde96" :question/math-academy-id "q-321646" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-t\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186302081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "335a12ad-0dac-5924-8ffe-a33133e004e8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186300218} {:answer/id #uuid "43d9f0e8-cdc2-56a1-8bab-c00724f75170" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186300296} {:answer/id #uuid "4f638168-55fc-55a0-b8d7-14935a1f7909" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186300376} {:answer/id #uuid "7eaf1a59-f06c-5167-8a94-ef48da242119" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186300589} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7f8157-ed75-5b01-8f83-5eb9006ffeed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186301209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eaf1a59-f06c-5167-8a94-ef48da242119" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186300589} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8e0352e-73bc-52cb-8b75-a00696bec104" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186302022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "587dcbe8-780f-5ae0-a952-bc8f4458a90a" :question/math-academy-id "q-32176" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186302082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30eb8020-4362-5dca-9aef-7187ca844777" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186300198} {:answer/id #uuid "53cd87ad-3a34-59b5-a960-6f170b23d525" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186300394} {:answer/id #uuid "704302a3-7420-5ad6-8aa0-cb9bb2d442dd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186300526} {:answer/id #uuid "725d1bc1-bca2-529a-9f96-435247a5b785" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186300531} {:answer/id #uuid "7c66242d-7e64-59c7-9be0-c1c3b331ab58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186300582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c66242d-7e64-59c7-9be0-c1c3b331ab58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186300582} :answer-field/id #uuid "8960c85e-f54e-543c-bb0f-5279313d6604" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186301659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1208e79a-a661-5205-bd4d-ce2da940348a" :question/math-academy-id "q-32177" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block lower triangular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A block lower triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks above the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block lower triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186302336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166970 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9740440-bdfb-588e-8f23-2eec9427e449" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Runge-Kutta Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6c2612-857d-542a-9a6b-ad1f47af45f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186300172} {:answer/id #uuid "93d80384-ebe5-581f-ba4a-d8a5e20516f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186300704} {:answer/id #uuid "9571f723-2f95-54ed-bbee-880c66f493ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186300712} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c57575-0e5f-5557-ba3b-da777fe1bb9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186300776} {:answer/id #uuid "e2673918-0fa6-5a74-8010-7dbdc705928b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186301085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9571f723-2f95-54ed-bbee-880c66f493ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186300712} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaed8cd6-2dad-51ca-9f3f-aa8b6a8316c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186301974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c780dd-c7ca-58af-8e2e-0a4e9d41e44d" :question/math-academy-id "q-327582" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y).$ The table below shows the intermediate quantities computed in the first iteration of the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nGiven that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ at which of the following points is the function $f$ evaluated during this iteration?\n\n- $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{2})$\n- $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4})$\n- $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{8})$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow, we have the following table of values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nWith this in mind, let's find each point at which the slope is evaluated in turn using the values given in the table.\n\n- The slope ${y}^{′}$ is evaluated at the initial point $(x,y)=({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(0,1).$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{1}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{2}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{3}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{4},\\,1+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the slope is evaluated at the points \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d411a23-cef5-5417-ac73-cff86304f884" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186302644} {:answer/id #uuid "3640132a-8f13-5c3e-904b-39778c6e3de5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186302792} {:answer/id #uuid "a49d14e5-b780-5b98-be31-7d115025fd80" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186303468} {:answer/id #uuid "a514caf8-f8b6-5221-937c-adcab645e60a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186303472} {:answer/id #uuid "e382c4b4-e686-51d2-beb7-5cafb3e78d27" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186303872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a514caf8-f8b6-5221-937c-adcab645e60a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186303472} :answer-field/id #uuid "100c3e25-c5ac-57fe-8297-0c550fe9a4ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f297359-1573-550c-acfb-39ada6af0bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-330297" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35ffec46-af25-548c-b530-f028c09d9793" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186302789} {:answer/id #uuid "815db271-adae-55b8-bace-6da5e3ef1cec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186303263} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc0aecd-50d8-55d9-b9a3-af9953232c14" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186303844} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f93721-52c4-554c-a99d-200f6b43b1ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186303863} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b33593-404e-56a3-a687-32d764b0ffe4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186304020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9b33593-404e-56a3-a687-32d764b0ffe4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186304020} :answer-field/id #uuid "c30f7306-2335-5f7a-8979-654d70343cd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78db71f4-3fc9-53f6-9a4c-5f66663f6749" :question/math-academy-id "q-330298" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $-2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $4$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106675f3-db71-5027-b3b7-f8833f64f184" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186302563} {:answer/id #uuid "229b952a-fc8e-5586-bfda-1680d8d306aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186302675} {:answer/id #uuid "5f46ff7b-dc2c-5706-b853-9ed0f8d1eaba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186303037} {:answer/id #uuid "badcbf32-c7d2-53de-a4f3-ed8437e856f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186303610} {:answer/id #uuid "fd41e97c-ed05-5e99-9314-1d4e73072845" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186304046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd41e97c-ed05-5e99-9314-1d4e73072845" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186304046} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8cb72c0-abe7-58fb-8b53-26c8e68b1182" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d50adfd6-221a-580f-9d34-b5717546927f" :question/math-academy-id "q-330300" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $5\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $5\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $5$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32fce60c-20f6-5b64-96ff-f3f273ae9463" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186302769} {:answer/id #uuid "4987ef9f-dd17-58f4-8313-a94b824ccb02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186302919} {:answer/id #uuid "59618d1c-67e5-5572-aba8-d8372571bf7f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186303001} {:answer/id #uuid "8535ddb6-6773-5f99-9bec-2b7887f28ec8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186303285} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ba1f6f-70ad-5a51-8747-82113bdb911e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186304000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59618d1c-67e5-5572-aba8-d8372571bf7f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186303001} :answer-field/id #uuid "541d9549-9edc-56d2-946a-2fb52062a7cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6d8d0be-fe4e-5e1a-948d-b8eb21d680a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-330302" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)={x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{{x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ce0f124-614f-5018-bf24-274425b0384f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186302939} {:answer/id #uuid "7760ac55-6d9d-5f25-a75a-d8143b980502" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186303193} {:answer/id #uuid "92bc0931-0ed9-5514-8f29-5214a0b79efa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186303368} {:answer/id #uuid "99d5125d-679c-51df-b3e6-3667846f3cc3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186303410} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc7ca9-c17f-59de-a296-35d2471cf4d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186303694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ce0f124-614f-5018-bf24-274425b0384f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186302939} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfc37eb7-c9b5-5399-8d2e-3637e84ad0f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63b3f264-c6f0-527c-811d-3ed89e69129d" :question/math-academy-id "q-330305" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e27b405-c7c3-5f62-a8f6-6a64cf338f01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186302742} {:answer/id #uuid "63f3178a-b9c6-5d7e-b736-cc9281b3ce05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186303061} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3afe43-bab1-5b9d-9b78-d4bcade00070" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186303336} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcca6c4-65bc-5bc2-8b5d-99e7b4b6dea5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186303825} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ca7eaa-ce56-541a-ab75-04508ebe8595" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186303993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63f3178a-b9c6-5d7e-b736-cc9281b3ce05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186303061} :answer-field/id #uuid "51d2ea82-7e8d-541f-94a3-049caf6d6179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed465271-2eba-5278-8938-505251fa9eb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-330308" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nI. $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n\nIII. $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff09b51-a998-59f2-b80f-ec8dd5327c5c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186302560} {:answer/id #uuid "3f887f82-672d-59da-ad03-484f8f660797" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186302856} {:answer/id #uuid "7c36c13b-c34e-5b12-b9a9-ec4a81dec8ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186303226} {:answer/id #uuid "b0816ecf-db08-5968-b24e-af05713a4856" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186303544} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e636f4-c99d-5f7d-be29-44c525a2ab46" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186304022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f887f82-672d-59da-ad03-484f8f660797" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186302856} :answer-field/id #uuid "0225f4e6-2a7d-585e-9f5c-7e7304a3d60f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7119481-1ac5-5669-b8e8-7afd953dbdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-330309" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)={x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{{x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536b00ce-ce2e-5ea4-a4fb-c820ac14e96b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186302974} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1732fe-1723-5267-989d-f733369e76d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186303214} {:answer/id #uuid "e94231f7-23b2-525d-8155-f6679b777102" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186303909} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2f0c7e-3d6a-54da-94f6-7b7dd1e20601" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186303943} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1fb0dc-be08-5240-8989-5dc6857c1f9d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186304024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b1732fe-1723-5267-989d-f733369e76d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186303214} :answer-field/id #uuid "df82608b-0ae1-5687-8ecd-46a02fadb56d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "891dbe2f-f88c-5e79-ae9d-64f7413a702a" :question/math-academy-id "q-330312" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={t}^{2}\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=5,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17e31168-b925-55a0-918c-6a38af46bc18" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186302613} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5c738e-7eec-521a-97f4-0924a180ef65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186302947} {:answer/id #uuid "52fb4f41-baa4-56a4-878f-9f64b18ce17c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186302970} {:answer/id #uuid "8390a730-eb52-524a-8a4d-b3c8c27dae1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186303277} {:answer/id #uuid "c334134a-7cb5-54bf-b6ad-e3150bba6e94" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186303654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52fb4f41-baa4-56a4-878f-9f64b18ce17c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186302970} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d90b965-cdf8-5201-a449-7a708227a8f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fac4a66c-708b-59cc-844a-c54884651cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-330332" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$\n- $x(t)=-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={e}^{2t}\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option II is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=0$ and ${c}_{2}=-4,$ respectively.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f3ed5c2-1c9e-5734-aad6-8c9c21beef7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186302656} {:answer/id #uuid "367e29de-e862-5fd8-9bd3-61eaa8ee74a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186302793} {:answer/id #uuid "84f575b3-b359-5d38-86a0-403e93a5b541" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186303284} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7df275-3573-595f-b0e9-fa90bbdcf224" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186303526} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8f2e4e-26b6-5ea9-80c3-3285fc30d58b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186303928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84f575b3-b359-5d38-86a0-403e93a5b541" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186303284} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e5c2929-a9b4-560f-a877-a5e2f588c6b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89894e08-2da2-5128-a91b-b1054d28b3f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-330353" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166970 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9740440-bdfb-588e-8f23-2eec9427e449" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Runge-Kutta Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d8ac2a0-fa87-5056-972b-131bc1967d12" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186303125} {:answer/id #uuid "b20ccc0e-1380-50a3-a249-a2c7d937e704" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186303558} {:answer/id #uuid "d98f18ae-3b52-5e3d-ba39-5b298d5c454f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186303809} {:answer/id #uuid "f4eaa315-3d04-541e-9b40-dbca306a0d43" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186303995} {:answer/id #uuid "ff282c72-ce1b-548f-aa07-a8228769755c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186304065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff282c72-ce1b-548f-aa07-a8228769755c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186304065} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3f5b2b5-c0fa-51a4-9270-eb12dfa9c461" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9b7930e-1624-5550-b77a-3086492ab273" :question/math-academy-id "q-331060" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y).$ The table below shows the intermediate quantities computed in the first iteration of the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $3$ |  |\n\nGiven that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ at which of the following points is the function $f$ evaluated during this iteration?\n\n- $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2})$\n- $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{3}{2})$\n- $(\\frac{3}{2},2)$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow, we have the following table of values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $3$ |  |\n\nWith this in mind, let's find each point at which the slope is evaluated in turn using the values given in the table.\n\n- The slope ${y}^{′}$ is evaluated at the initial point $(x,y)=({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(1,2).$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{1}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{2}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{3}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2},\\,2+0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2},2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the slope is evaluated at the points \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cfc4cf2-a8e8-59f5-a6f8-ccb2a8fca6ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186302739} {:answer/id #uuid "41c868f4-3633-5cd6-abce-f793c2d3089c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186302870} {:answer/id #uuid "9568a1c8-b5cc-544c-8e61-bd7731544f05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186303384} {:answer/id #uuid "cb37e7ac-a885-5c55-b66d-8c173d1e8048" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186303704} {:answer/id #uuid "fa36311d-8e86-5e73-8d31-3fce04a49a81" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186304026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb37e7ac-a885-5c55-b66d-8c173d1e8048" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186303704} :answer-field/id #uuid "155ccb92-44b7-540b-afa7-3a3cb56fda2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "363ad515-bb3d-517b-aff5-449014ea0698" :question/math-academy-id "q-331114" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-8{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-8{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d5c6cb-1c97-55b7-9cac-22add3c7e377" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186302567} {:answer/id #uuid "192e55d9-a1be-5ba6-9cea-29f23e40a4bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186302625} {:answer/id #uuid "74223080-19b0-548c-8a50-72e471779141" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186303164} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d9cab2-ec9a-50f9-ba0f-14cab6570e05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186303649} {:answer/id #uuid "df283476-20f4-569d-aa03-d80d3e148f0f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186303856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "192e55d9-a1be-5ba6-9cea-29f23e40a4bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186302625} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b760af-2c42-586d-8f5f-8c6c4f28c362" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "164f3277-5bbd-5470-9288-18ef536cb19b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331974" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186304969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f35cdf-6adb-5ac5-9eb7-3ffabd05a2e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186302465} {:answer/id #uuid "0b3f4ec4-4119-547d-93c8-de1ae8af98d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186302534} {:answer/id #uuid "3deb4ddc-7f6f-500d-a593-973f1dce61d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186302844} {:answer/id #uuid "8149fa1b-3362-5f6f-8369-dc6999a85895" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186303262} {:answer/id #uuid "db6c31dc-cbb3-59af-b486-ecc070980367" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186303823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3deb4ddc-7f6f-500d-a593-973f1dce61d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186302844} :answer-field/id #uuid "48e06dea-4d34-535e-bf2d-4bc51876df02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfb33e06-625f-5894-a78d-d1f418d2b822" :question/math-academy-id "q-331976" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acbc1af-8a14-5ab7-8415-61b9dda79cb2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186305398} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc3cbbe-2e5b-59b6-8a5a-412a02b45ffe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186305404} {:answer/id #uuid "34d3f7e6-b13c-5811-b3e5-a2b5caef1eb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186305656} {:answer/id #uuid "4d39e25c-6724-5165-bcc1-467f2cb114e8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305900} {:answer/id #uuid "674772bb-eb1c-50ea-a9f1-dd10214cf197" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186306136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34d3f7e6-b13c-5811-b3e5-a2b5caef1eb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186305656} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6fa20bb-793d-539d-95f3-08b715ccfaa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30787f50-63d0-5577-9588-239251d45d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-333092" :question/problem "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.0$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.4$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.0$ falls *outside* of the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.4$ falls *outside* the given region, and\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ falls *within* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is stable for $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ and $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b2af33-606a-518a-a8cf-a8bb610a7b1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186305201} {:answer/id #uuid "060a171e-7f26-5def-8d3d-13034bb01098" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305206} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f4877f-1839-5815-9828-0deb66ddcb34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306733} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fa6777-e518-5edf-947f-6d302dcde10f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186306876} {:answer/id #uuid "d474b0ee-27ca-5e8b-8a77-8ad1c7317178" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186307124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "060a171e-7f26-5def-8d3d-13034bb01098" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305206} :answer-field/id #uuid "911fa9a0-6565-5e5f-a627-64ff80af1793" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ad56467-9a9c-5515-8f2a-3d6fb0d92b9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333311" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3y+x$\n- ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+5$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with \n\n$$\na(x)={x}^{2}+1,\\,b(x)=-3,\\,c(x)=x.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{5}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{5}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186308437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2358fabc-35da-5c45-baf8-07b2f30d9c06" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186305488} {:answer/id #uuid "333ea3b2-7f3f-5203-a4b5-6d85b16a2847" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305635} {:answer/id #uuid "44c5883c-1e43-5155-b381-97eec704a494" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305827} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca81b20-4839-5e14-9ec1-e7a5dc7b8a58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306042} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d2decd-0ac2-5f83-bdee-3460714a0bf1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186307242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ca81b20-4839-5e14-9ec1-e7a5dc7b8a58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306042} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf0e7541-daa3-5d8a-a67a-87297cf4bf96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2271166c-28ab-5bb4-b5b8-f402b78156b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333316" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+5$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4$\n- ${y}^{′}-4y=(x-2){y}^{2}+{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+5.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{4}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{4}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\n- Equation III is a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-2){y}^{2}+{e}^{x}+4y.\n$$\n\nThis is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=x-2,\\,b(x)=4,\\,c(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186308438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddf69d1-8a04-5d1b-99ce-2b182d9bafcc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186305435} {:answer/id #uuid "811ada5b-7928-5774-b79e-5a9c2ff8afa4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186306383} {:answer/id #uuid "c41ca1ee-70aa-5c23-886d-93429094afb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186306979} {:answer/id #uuid "caebde4d-d2c9-5134-b2e1-db038881d554" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186307034} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e290cb-1568-58ec-aaa1-2250b6faa322" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186307112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3e290cb-1568-58ec-aaa1-2250b6faa322" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186307112} :answer-field/id #uuid "26b8b869-368d-506b-b867-9bccee293cb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2cc3f6c-b7da-5ffb-9443-884962200fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-333321" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-xy+1$\n- ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+3){y}^{2}-4y+{e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-xy+1.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation II is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)={x}^{2}+3,\\,b(x)=-4,\\,c(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186308439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51985448-bc1b-59e0-93ed-7a018ee8b30b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305946} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc6209a-8a97-5420-b24d-d559c70b2336" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186306340} {:answer/id #uuid "93ffac01-5204-5239-b9b6-78a8edc3b208" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186306550} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e4bb01-56d1-500b-b2f0-395619506f78" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186307125} {:answer/id #uuid "fa424b7a-26c5-5220-adbd-eb4c07342c10" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186307471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51985448-bc1b-59e0-93ed-7a018ee8b30b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305946} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3e1200-b5e7-5f4c-a02b-68c1496541d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f788c2b2-09c8-57a5-9839-705d54ca27d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333322" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}+2xy+{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}-y+{e}^{x}$\n- $y{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=x-1,\\,b(x)=2x,\\,c(x)={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}-y+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation.Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1)y-\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{3}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186308493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "028e70db-8be0-51ec-b480-454108d04c10" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305174} {:answer/id #uuid "68618160-c25b-5518-b475-d2ba9a8809c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186306146} {:answer/id #uuid "6a85475c-f421-5bf0-b5ae-f9c3d2584ad1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306169} {:answer/id #uuid "aed08e77-2de1-5f13-865a-20a5b87bd3ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186306801} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cb97d6-10d6-5a47-bd89-526dee76274a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186307299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a85475c-f421-5bf0-b5ae-f9c3d2584ad1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306169} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c169aca-18d3-5845-823c-2826f9825770" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81f632f0-dedf-5479-9362-313bdb74c812" :question/math-academy-id "q-333693" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2}$\n- $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $-3(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $(Δx)^{4}=(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186217238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Clairaut Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dbf98f0-ea49-52b1-a600-6b15f91137ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186305432} {:answer/id #uuid "209f8e19-2c68-5e9d-8ab2-2795ff0b948e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186305468} {:answer/id #uuid "286a604e-d41c-53a9-bb48-5ed41562363d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186305529} {:answer/id #uuid "8da9593f-4660-5d97-8986-b78ebbfb1fdd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306495} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9d6905-ff5e-5f00-b14b-96f9d7d4c547" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186307474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8da9593f-4660-5d97-8986-b78ebbfb1fdd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306495} :answer-field/id #uuid "678be234-cb88-599d-97c8-859a104b3ce5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "077e1927-c6a2-59ff-bf00-a9d36509bb47" :question/math-academy-id "q-334000" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y={x}^{3}{y}^{′}+x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$\n- $x{y}^{4}-xy{y}^{′}-{y}^{2}=0$\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx{y}^{3}-x{y}^{′}-y=0.\n$$\n\nAgain, this equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186308541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268daf83-684a-57ba-bc1d-3e92d8b533d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186305513} {:answer/id #uuid "4c6d40d3-3b27-5616-874a-42fdafbef333" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186305889} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2a1ca8-e5b6-5ae4-b548-9a837e3fe4bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186306506} {:answer/id #uuid "bc693768-9b7c-56b8-8ebb-07f0c5feb4f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186306907} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f22fef-d93c-501e-8296-59ce6a0eb5d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186307244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc693768-9b7c-56b8-8ebb-07f0c5feb4f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186306907} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6c78ac-09b5-5634-9073-dcdd32de958a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3f7955cf-0d1a-5a59-88f5-cf30308c87f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334008" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **stable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- $Δx=\\frac{0.25}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{3.5}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{1.5}{\\lambda }$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.25}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.25.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<0.25 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{3.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=3.5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.5.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.5 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "618fd53f-7214-5fe3-ba95-c929c1533c60" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186306095} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0fe8fd-8747-5c54-9cc6-d98eade730a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186306175} {:answer/id #uuid "721d5b56-c606-51a7-ad65-e2822e48a253" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186306239} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1f1921-ba77-51d5-af00-11a250e49417" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id 17592186307338} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6edcb2-3ec7-5f34-9cc3-8632b5f6e79b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186307473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee1f1921-ba77-51d5-af00-11a250e49417" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id 17592186307338} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b7bcc1b-f63c-51ef-8877-c463fcde4161" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f21f7e32-ddad-5b51-8839-e9c4ba026875" :question/math-academy-id "q-334009" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **stable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nI. $Δx=\\frac{1.4}{\\lambda }$\n\nII. $Δx=\\frac{1.25}{\\lambda }$\n\nIII. $Δx=\\frac{2.5}{\\lambda }$\n\nIV. $Δx=\\frac{0.75}{\\lambda }$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$  is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.4}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.4.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.4 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.25}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.25.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.25 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.75}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.75.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<0.75 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b11f9f4-4960-552c-b663-cb5220a87649" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186305401} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb2759e-5b0d-5bba-b9d7-ccf3840e9d8d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186305442} {:answer/id #uuid "4f70c57f-70da-5bc5-b4cb-2895141a8312" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305924} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6d122f-4417-5923-802f-cfcb7ffb789f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186306029} {:answer/id #uuid "7d034cb5-6946-5137-b387-7741e60de6d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186306342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eb2759e-5b0d-5bba-b9d7-ccf3840e9d8d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186305442} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b5ddf82-da88-560e-9bc8-8376662f9872" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c9db1fa-3df6-5592-9112-986f10641d50" :question/math-academy-id "q-334014" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{0.4}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{5}{\\lambda }$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.4}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.4.$ Since this value falls within the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <\\lambda Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <0.4<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method must be stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186217238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Clairaut Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2990c86f-a20b-50ce-9f4c-c5fd624ca975" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186305545} {:answer/id #uuid "5249f8b3-9dc6-5aa1-a58a-1eec13e6cfa4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305949} {:answer/id #uuid "75dc5375-ed8a-5721-8783-5619b0298c18" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306274} {:answer/id #uuid "db9558f0-7080-5607-bc97-8d2d978dab83" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186307186} {:answer/id #uuid "e50baa6f-4955-55f8-8e7f-4cfc358c5642" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186307274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2990c86f-a20b-50ce-9f4c-c5fd624ca975" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186305545} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2b6dac0-ea57-55ff-aa37-78cfb06f2dd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf963632-b648-5646-b884-a63de120bfff" :question/math-academy-id "q-334133" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y+2=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}$\n- ${y}^{′}-y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′})$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}-2.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={y}^{′}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186308567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186217238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Clairaut Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb9777b-cef8-54f7-8778-b72a31d8b1b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186305288} {:answer/id #uuid "179ea438-0b6c-53b7-ad38-f33ab8437bbd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305370} {:answer/id #uuid "a92bde58-962f-5f65-91d1-ab49b72d6dc4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186306744} {:answer/id #uuid "f2db7926-cc67-52b8-8fff-0e499b25e3ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186307391} {:answer/id #uuid "f95c54cb-b6c4-53ed-8cd5-0089266e9b34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186307462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "179ea438-0b6c-53b7-ad38-f33ab8437bbd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305370} :answer-field/id #uuid "07ed33ed-e094-5a5f-b5ec-49ad3d42c1ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "feda7358-5560-5f92-8b57-984ba0bdd378" :question/math-academy-id "q-334135" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})$\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+{2}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- ${y}^{2}=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{2}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={2}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. It cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186308570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186217238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Clairaut Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0610b96c-c939-5fdd-9b23-75c1e2f7e536" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305207} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff32b56-f252-5f32-87b1-acd0ce784491" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305296} {:answer/id #uuid "1b17c01e-1493-55d8-8d9e-9f5c70ad2ccb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186305402} {:answer/id #uuid "2098a81b-253b-5564-9abf-8668e2191ef5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186305467} {:answer/id #uuid "309f7ed6-0da6-533a-a5e8-6f8657743110" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186305610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "309f7ed6-0da6-533a-a5e8-6f8657743110" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186305610} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed6aaed7-7ccb-5583-a9a1-5c5d7d64e28c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d6213f1-60d6-5a67-a158-34abc9070a3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334148" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′}-1)^{4}$\n- $y{y}^{′}-x{y}^{5}-4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{2}$\n- $x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2)-y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=({y}^{′}-1)^{4}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-x{y}^{4}-4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y.\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2),\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186308575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "554994b7-1fe1-55bd-bdc9-36b5428dad8c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305977} {:answer/id #uuid "74ddd464-3292-5782-9157-db366879f36d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186306263} {:answer/id #uuid "b7bbdcb1-5516-504f-9577-c37d59f5d673" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186306866} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c82f10-6d46-59ad-bdf7-170c947065c7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186306973} {:answer/id #uuid "f0164736-76c6-50c2-8781-888e98d0dd1d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186307356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0164736-76c6-50c2-8781-888e98d0dd1d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186307356} :answer-field/id #uuid "40910ee2-bc9c-533e-9c39-c0b7c4815fae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d23e4c59-133c-5f47-8ffc-1426beb350c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334199" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-3(3{e}^{3x})+2({e}^{3x}) & =0 \\\\ 8 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x}-3(2{e}^{2x})+2({e}^{2x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35922e3f-153f-53c7-b8e3-b3dba47bfab5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186305663} {:answer/id #uuid "7879b8eb-1ddb-5111-9405-978e0495241a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186306296} {:answer/id #uuid "96b9e231-25c7-5d16-9091-bd91f5cf2702" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186306570} {:answer/id #uuid "b1210ca9-b8a7-5b20-9b80-54b6efe0c34e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186306814} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf83594-f0d6-5d4f-811d-336b0ffa479c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186307210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddf83594-f0d6-5d4f-811d-336b0ffa479c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186307210} :answer-field/id #uuid "44a67f1b-ab27-5410-8ba8-d0b8071f2993" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9207ec17-19e6-5c10-b901-32c1b663c7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334255" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.95$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=2.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.95,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.95)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.95}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.95}| \\\\  & =0.5128…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.2}| \\\\  & =0.4545…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.4}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.7142…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.3)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+2.3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3.3}| \\\\  & =0.3030…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186308586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "365de797-aa57-52fc-a723-789e61c5220f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305674} {:answer/id #uuid "4170404b-8229-5651-95f0-a492adc9ffd8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186305795} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd7b01f-bd9f-5477-bb52-92eb740adf8b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186305906} {:answer/id #uuid "6871f385-b333-5489-98b6-f1035a48fe1b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186306148} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0c73cd-533e-5ea3-81d7-3915741aade1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186306893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "365de797-aa57-52fc-a723-789e61c5220f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305674} :answer-field/id #uuid "34e42d9d-e69b-5207-acb8-a31812e69cd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4674804-88cb-5499-be8a-d4a228b08c3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334258" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){e}^{2x} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+5{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01bb70bc-cb5c-5861-a385-48a7bde5a3e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305166} {:answer/id #uuid "342d50f1-5d60-5bac-816e-2d53093b9d6a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186305647} {:answer/id #uuid "57c45ccc-1f14-5148-91c1-7e0e52abd774" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305997} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a0466d-4e02-5cd9-b1ba-b66d882e87d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186306720} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d3af81-8e29-547c-9c41-96d75684e3fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186306731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6a0466d-4e02-5cd9-b1ba-b66d882e87d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186306720} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe003948-886a-5b7a-bab5-5fd3f2992bb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce7557df-4bdb-5791-b8df-2efda5a01a24" :question/math-academy-id "q-334260" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x}+9{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x-1} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x-1}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}+9{e}^{3x-1} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x} & {e}^{3x-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3x} & 3{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}-3{e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x-1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{6x-1}-3{e}^{6x-1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4752d93b-093d-5d84-af2d-c98cbb61fac3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305844} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9038ba-5b77-5857-bb8d-09f6e43e1a50" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186305916} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf35e59-6974-59ba-95f4-5d52b9a555a0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186306194} {:answer/id #uuid "73999a29-e475-5aed-adc2-529dba98b76a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186306250} {:answer/id #uuid "8864854d-83a6-5879-861b-aada034aa0c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186306456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cf35e59-6974-59ba-95f4-5d52b9a555a0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186306194} :answer-field/id #uuid "1815ddf4-bcea-528a-ab59-c7e89bec51af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbddaa01-5158-5d3b-b619-76268088aef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334266" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2-3x\\cdot 2x+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)-3x(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ 2x & 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f2a1b2-0fff-5c3e-b982-e7d57c3b60a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186305324} {:answer/id #uuid "490c271b-9e2e-51db-a620-2f1605eefe48" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305860} {:answer/id #uuid "86a02ffc-3022-5a2c-ba39-4a3aab938d5b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186306435} {:answer/id #uuid "8d9f3082-0fa6-527a-b75f-81251bbbee57" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186306493} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f96877-d442-5c94-acec-552c52c92c49" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186307442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13f2a1b2-0fff-5c3e-b982-e7d57c3b60a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186305324} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c02124-a667-54aa-af69-f5fffebd4c39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a08136e-69ff-52a7-8d77-c9b9c2b3571c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334278" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28c61591-d1d6-54f1-9c55-a8ea7751b145" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186305532} {:answer/id #uuid "418009f0-b11d-554c-8742-521af288a4e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186305798} {:answer/id #uuid "6324ae58-d806-5022-be3f-fb1a4ff525ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186306112} {:answer/id #uuid "8578a33a-b351-5893-a06b-1981f4362e98" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186306423} {:answer/id #uuid "c51edf8d-51dc-52f1-98ce-ac92d3ffb42e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186306988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "418009f0-b11d-554c-8742-521af288a4e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186305798} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0187509-6fa0-58aa-949e-58c32bb01912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f92808c-572e-59a4-9f7a-86aaaf0f9d81" :question/math-academy-id "q-334431" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=3.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.5)| & =|1-0.5+\\frac{(0.5)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.5)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.5)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{233}{384}| \\\\  & =0.606…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.5)| & =|1-1.5+\\frac{(1.5)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1.5)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1.5)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{35}{128}| \\\\  & =0.273…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.5)| & =|1-2.5+\\frac{(2.5)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.5)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.5)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{83}{128}| \\\\  & =0.648…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3)| & =|1-3.2+\\frac{(3.2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3.2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3.2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3427}{1875}| \\\\  & =1.827…<1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable."}] [{:db/id 17592186308616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5c1882-5b6e-5b9e-ad5a-4d856ce5c49f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186305455} {:answer/id #uuid "24e5101f-1ffd-597e-96bb-357157524f8e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305500} {:answer/id #uuid "35737fce-ffe9-5f4b-953a-2afb499e00fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305661} {:answer/id #uuid "a444e984-302d-54c0-a248-b713026de199" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186306698} {:answer/id #uuid "bf012756-9f2c-580f-9a04-6ac8d66cbcb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f5c1882-5b6e-5b9e-ad5a-4d856ce5c49f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186305455} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c31909-9258-5d8b-b171-a534e133f3a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e489da60-4fc4-5e3e-84ad-c60bd1e817ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-334435" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.2$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.8$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.7$\n- $\\lambda Δx=6.6$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.2)| & =|1-0.2+\\frac{(0.2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{12\\,281}{15\\,000}| \\\\  & =0.818…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.8)| & =|1-1.8+\\frac{(1.8)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1.8)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1.8)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1427}{5000}| \\\\  & =0.2854<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.7)| & =|1-2.7+\\frac{(2.7)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.7)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.7)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{70\\,307}{80\\,000}| \\\\  & =0.878…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=6.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.2)| & =|1-6.6+\\frac{(6.6)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(6.6)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(6.6)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{236\\,627}{5000}| \\\\  & =47.3254>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable."}] [{:db/id 17592186308617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb6f30c-e120-5450-a7a9-eb8caf0b1034" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186305261} {:answer/id #uuid "208a0586-b76b-5730-bed1-7521241cbe76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186305466} {:answer/id #uuid "624f4250-f13d-5efc-87f9-81fb37bb6ef4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186306104} {:answer/id #uuid "a21e25d6-fa54-5072-85b5-74f7e9ca8724" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186306671} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ca3d21-d3a6-5545-b410-9d870bad1bc8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186307029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9ca3d21-d3a6-5545-b410-9d870bad1bc8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186307029} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ece80dc-5d35-5e03-b19b-7c5d23c9547f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "803f3927-d5f3-5c35-a7cc-27c03125aa08" :question/math-academy-id "q-334436" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.1$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.9$\n- $\\lambda Δx=3.4$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.1)| & =|1-0.1+\\frac{(0.1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.1)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.1)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{72\\,387}{80\\,000}| \\\\  & =0.9048…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|1-1.2+\\frac{(1.2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1.2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1.2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{199}{625}| \\\\  & =0.3184<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.9)| & =|1-2.9+\\frac{(2.9)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.9)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.9)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{284\\,921}{240\\,000}| \\\\  & =1.1871…>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.4)| & =|1-3.4+\\frac{(3.4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3.4)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3.4)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{11\\,987}{5000}| \\\\  & =2.3974>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable."}] [{:db/id 17592186308624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a6aa657-bf96-509c-9974-00ceb93ec6f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186305394} {:answer/id #uuid "70713e09-06bb-537a-9ee7-5bd95e947e0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186306222} {:answer/id #uuid "89df3693-a6ef-5ada-b92c-856b628fa893" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306467} {:answer/id #uuid "cb528311-962c-55d8-8831-33af0f561e34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186307037} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5b5512-9643-5db7-9b03-7219233573a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186307332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb528311-962c-55d8-8831-33af0f561e34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186307037} :answer-field/id #uuid "182cf3f9-1629-5745-b9da-36ec8c63b9e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ca57581-1de5-5ee9-ad10-ddff45b3ad82" :question/math-academy-id "q-334466" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the trapezoidal method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.15$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=0.8$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=2.9$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=6.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the trapezoidal method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1-\\lambda Δx/2}{1+\\lambda Δx/2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\text{ }\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx)\\,{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the trapezoidal method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1-\\lambda Δx/2}{1+\\lambda Δx/2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the trapezoidal method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.15)| & =|\\frac{1-0.15/2}{1+0.15/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{0.925}{1.075}| \\\\  & =0.8604…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.8)| & =|\\frac{1-0.8/2}{1+0.8/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{0.6}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.4285…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.9)| & =|\\frac{1-2.9/2}{1+2.9/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-0.45}{2.45}| \\\\  & =0.1836…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=6.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(6.2)| & =|\\frac{1-6.2/2}{1+6.2/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-2.1}{4.1}| \\\\  & =0.5121…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “I, II, III, and IV”.\n\nNote that, since the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186308653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b5f0533-b025-5bc3-b245-894b7317d42e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186306478} {:answer/id #uuid "8da5c750-bf0b-58a4-988a-1abf7e3abaf4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186306494} {:answer/id #uuid "926ad0c6-66b8-57f0-b105-2c0a78b48493" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186306536} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e613f4-2763-56b1-b4cb-0e8e823ea42e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186307140} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d4c3d1-89e7-5c68-ac99-97d54948d4e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186307390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8da5c750-bf0b-58a4-988a-1abf7e3abaf4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186306494} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b20210-f5bf-5ad0-86e0-5ce6cdd096e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "faa06cb1-bef4-588f-8333-1536faf16397" :question/math-academy-id "q-334627" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda =4$ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- $Δx=0.2$\n- $Δx=0.45$\n- $Δx=0.9$\n- $Δx=2.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda =4,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <4Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.45$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.9$ doesn't satisfy the inequality, and\n\n- $Δx=2.2$ doesn't satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler’s method is stable for step sizes $Δx=0.2$ and $Δx=0.45.$\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b41d669-db2b-5ca2-aa13-793bd0a51960" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186305716} {:answer/id #uuid "3e232e85-39d8-5a41-98d8-7ccfd0094456" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id 17592186305753} {:answer/id #uuid "5a3c2be8-b76b-5d02-adb8-de3297afbf24" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306014} {:answer/id #uuid "a9547929-e04a-59e1-8ee3-68c3aa5262a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186306747} {:answer/id #uuid "e2861842-6097-5876-a367-6196902aafad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186307249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9547929-e04a-59e1-8ee3-68c3aa5262a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186306747} :answer-field/id #uuid "bba348cd-cfbb-520a-9eed-e9275bae68f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "331acb28-49e8-50e4-b8d3-d67c401cc67e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334715" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the modified Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.6$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.2$\n- $\\lambda Δx=3.5$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the modified Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.5)| & =|1-0.5+\\frac{(0.5)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{5}{8}| \\\\  & =0.625<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.6)| & =|1-1.6+\\frac{(1.6)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{17}{25}| \\\\  & =0.68<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.2)| & =|1-2.2+\\frac{(2.2)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{61}{50}| \\\\  & =1.22>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.5)| & =|1-3.5+\\frac{(3.5)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{29}{8}| \\\\  & =3.625>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the Modified Euler Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable."}] [{:db/id 17592186308676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b8cbf73-76e1-54a5-84d4-d80333a4ef75" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305721} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdf10d7-6e1c-5467-b614-8a3266c84156" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306607} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e8e5bc-4fba-5680-bb8e-d9821151b2d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186307083} {:answer/id #uuid "f2940528-333a-58e7-b7e9-e35fa963ea78" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186307386} {:answer/id #uuid "ff68c2e6-89a9-5261-a203-cad23dd679a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186307507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff68c2e6-89a9-5261-a203-cad23dd679a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186307507} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7482acd-6813-50d9-954d-0ffe427a7d23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "512c526c-f958-5c94-ad6a-b60bd207799d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334745" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $11(Δx)^{5}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.2Δx.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "207ae958-d6a8-5c30-97cb-b014496b44e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186305465} {:answer/id #uuid "27f2f331-b583-5a87-aa44-7f36b0bc7255" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305527} {:answer/id #uuid "543878ac-58f1-5af4-aafe-91a3e074823b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186305967} {:answer/id #uuid "7fda176c-b009-5d58-8ca4-753c2690f12a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186306373} {:answer/id #uuid "c4605bac-4a79-50fc-8231-401531ac26d7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186306981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "207ae958-d6a8-5c30-97cb-b014496b44e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186305465} :answer-field/id #uuid "86305808-46ce-55fa-86fc-882f5bbb2b17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5074782-bb53-5a41-89b0-5eb1637aa0c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334752" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{6}+5(Δx)^{7}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{9}$\n- $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}+3(Δx)^{8}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{6}+5(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{6}=2(Δx)^{5+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{9},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{4}=0.4(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}+3(Δx)^{8},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{5}=6(Δx)^{4+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b36245-18fd-594b-a1d6-36644d9479a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186305165} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1ab820-afa9-55d9-8d1a-fe1018f2fb2a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186305266} {:answer/id #uuid "420987ca-9ad2-527d-8178-ad5448d4a95a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186305801} {:answer/id #uuid "7da00cff-ba81-57b6-8632-628369dca500" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186306351} {:answer/id #uuid "a40f1596-325b-598f-9111-faa35a7f6406" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186306696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a40f1596-325b-598f-9111-faa35a7f6406" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186306696} :answer-field/id #uuid "9001c35c-d333-5345-9589-8c2c1c4aebbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdca2386-e70b-5926-863b-7ba03141d57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334753" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6}$\n- $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{3}=0.3(Δx)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{5}=9(Δx)^{4+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46fe8e72-698f-5596-8137-70b4b92ab30f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305840} {:answer/id #uuid "4f4ee2bb-60b4-55fc-8f50-b0e0237dab3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186305923} {:answer/id #uuid "73517bf7-ce1a-5be5-8940-b481351d0997" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186306246} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d9db9e-c905-5833-96fa-ed8d39cff198" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186307272} {:answer/id #uuid "efb99644-2adf-55c9-91f3-804c91a3e395" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186307351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f4ee2bb-60b4-55fc-8f50-b0e0237dab3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186305923} :answer-field/id #uuid "66746ea0-f448-55c6-ad00-975f27e3dcd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3ccb3bf-e44e-5f18-88ca-d013183f6901" :question/math-academy-id "q-334755" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+6(Δx)^{6}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.8(Δx)^{2}+5(Δx)^{3}$\n- $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+6(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{3}=4(Δx)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.8(Δx)^{2}+5(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.8(Δx)^{2}=0.8(Δx)^{1+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{4}=9(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31288407-bbbf-5f89-9116-835338a2d24d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305614} {:answer/id #uuid "498851e6-7f9c-5c1c-9a25-12ccdc47f138" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305865} {:answer/id #uuid "4e837d9d-4fdd-54ca-bf7e-b90afbb103fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186305914} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3879a3-bd80-5414-ae30-63937aa46873" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306357} {:answer/id #uuid "efcdba3a-9867-52c1-9b41-79650168578b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186307352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e837d9d-4fdd-54ca-bf7e-b90afbb103fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186305914} :answer-field/id #uuid "b61b1923-af08-5104-8adf-bce92ffaea17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1edbdd20-a6fa-5411-be9e-ad5ea2edf0c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334756" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6}$\n- $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{3}=4(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "258769c1-bf65-597f-8e52-3e552d53ceac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186305504} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa1aee3-6818-5be2-aeaf-4e83d8d3bbec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305925} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd305b9-c364-5db9-9cd3-8d702d6cdf01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186305926} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9f1ba5-d800-553a-9ca9-e8bd8bcc728f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306605} {:answer/id #uuid "9e91947c-1f53-5a58-859d-b1bf1211a2fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186306637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "258769c1-bf65-597f-8e52-3e552d53ceac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186305504} :answer-field/id #uuid "14a0aacd-a164-531b-8f30-7df34b77c607" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b0cbcd0-f13f-5586-9c22-f5d860877a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-334757" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6}$\n- $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7}$" :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{3}=2(Δx)^{3}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{5}=0.4(Δx)^{4+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0b38d1-cc53-5003-ab21-59d598a85883" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305247} {:answer/id #uuid "1dac2a51-67dc-5978-84aa-28c750346ae8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186305430} {:answer/id #uuid "4e36bd0e-06a3-5286-ba8a-22dd04516132" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186305911} {:answer/id #uuid "ce450c3a-fdfa-5f33-bb90-09a7b865d6d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186307058} {:answer/id #uuid "f1332e31-2bdd-518f-8f59-2434948780a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186307366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1332e31-2bdd-518f-8f59-2434948780a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186307366} :answer-field/id #uuid "69565827-8477-59f8-a80c-746ae9d9fb9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a147217d-4ada-5afd-bf77-eb76079d5271" :question/math-academy-id "q-334761" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7}$\n- $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.5(Δx)^{4}=0.5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186308693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0244e6f0-96d3-553f-8442-163e492cca45" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305171} {:answer/id #uuid "1201ffb3-0a9f-55fb-87db-daab500df03a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186305310} {:answer/id #uuid "4287694c-3530-5b5e-9ac3-841ec8d8815b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186305808} {:answer/id #uuid "9a33068a-b97a-5fbd-90f5-9362a2325c14" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186306591} {:answer/id #uuid "f907339d-5375-5fea-9066-54654d55abb0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186307457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0244e6f0-96d3-553f-8442-163e492cca45" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186305171} :answer-field/id #uuid "54008c0f-87bf-5340-81e6-12289c30c1de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186307776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05b85b1b-94cc-5955-8bdf-0e035f03c881" :question/math-academy-id "q-334851" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=3{x}^{5}-2x+1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{5}-2x}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(3{x}^{5}-2x) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{5}-2x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(3{x}^{3}-\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{5}-2x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186308716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b276fe-3cf7-57cf-b9f4-d196045a2203" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186305239} {:answer/id #uuid "15292b7f-56e6-5bd3-95b8-c9e0c4571d4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186305334} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7a331b-5455-5917-b9da-a066f46db285" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186306646} {:answer/id #uuid "e954cb4c-cec6-5790-b690-df3555baaa85" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186307302} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d2ba17-dd4d-5fce-8f72-70ec76aaaf26" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186307466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9d2ba17-dd4d-5fce-8f72-70ec76aaaf26" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186307466} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf558613-5788-5902-8832-fe0df25b00a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e125563-3523-5218-87f9-08cecc055fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335020" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{2}+5+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "489cf97e-6c0f-5597-800e-5df4c84dc7c6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186309302} {:answer/id #uuid "52f463cd-7b7b-5861-a0e6-e446661d097c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186309377} {:answer/id #uuid "5e89d5e4-0385-57c3-bb82-acd44dba8514" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186309455} {:answer/id #uuid "a477c7ea-2183-5af4-afc2-1c9de12376cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186310073} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7cc0b7-396a-5b5c-b805-605879748b71" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186310152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a477c7ea-2183-5af4-afc2-1c9de12376cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186310073} :answer-field/id #uuid "27253de7-dbc7-5838-a4cb-f5a95a31f6a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186310892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a8a1841-dc47-5a84-bd2d-e5c201943d9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335246" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{1}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2tx}{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{1+x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊂(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{1}{(1+x+t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1674bcea-39fc-5aad-aca7-372a177fe050" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186308917} {:answer/id #uuid "25e64bcc-0147-5181-9ef9-b6cb514ee5a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186309029} {:answer/id #uuid "511cfe36-d25a-5e4f-af0d-d2735ed975a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186309361} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5d3830-b200-5801-9be5-e52c9b90e922" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186309864} {:answer/id #uuid "955b3dd8-2188-5b71-8408-64ec11de032b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186309945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d5d3830-b200-5801-9be5-e52c9b90e922" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186309864} :answer-field/id #uuid "c85287b0-57f7-5ef2-b815-ce3761ebab78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9de6d57f-11dc-5bb2-bebc-d38ef2773685" :question/math-academy-id "q-335247" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>1$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\sqrt{x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+t}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$ (since $x+t\\ge c>0$ on $R$). Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2x{t}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t>1$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ never vanishes on $R,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c23f2cc-82f0-5867-b00d-89c6883bf817" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186308834} {:answer/id #uuid "22fe8dc0-6cee-58c2-8654-f942a7436068" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186309011} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb0f1cb-d3fe-542e-9b7a-c1e573068d6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186310029} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b7bfc2-5415-538d-a056-230b0f1b053d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186310176} {:answer/id #uuid "dfedda34-e72c-5a4e-b2ae-71901b0f875c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186310544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22fe8dc0-6cee-58c2-8654-f942a7436068" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186309011} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d724d1-f317-5650-bac8-04e43dc1b9b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186310861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57d1f543-d099-580f-9280-d60da64177c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-335248" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{|x-t|}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $0<c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{-xt}-{x}^{3}t{e}^{-xt}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{|x-t|}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f1d1d5-26f2-5996-9b45-e843073eebd6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186308784} {:answer/id #uuid "105ce355-6bc0-5eac-ab1d-30bf73c3e3ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186308865} {:answer/id #uuid "1489c33b-6f54-52d8-aae5-e12710732f04" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186308900} {:answer/id #uuid "8dad0b51-3e0e-5303-9848-e926f7285ee8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186309871} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8570d1-eab0-562a-97e3-ada215b2c56d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186310757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04f1d1d5-26f2-5996-9b45-e843073eebd6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186308784} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4cc132b-f46c-51a6-9635-8bd3d3a39e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7963456-3a78-5141-94e0-577dece1c8b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-335249" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{3}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ So, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2}).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nSince $x\\in [0,1]$ and $t>0,$ we have $x+{t}^{2}>0,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t}{x+{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11f46424-29be-5d3d-b031-687406eee03c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186308879} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ed01f6-8a08-5ec8-861d-b3e1e088b9d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186310063} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c13585-d0f3-59b9-8c0d-4e5fcb99b25c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186310243} {:answer/id #uuid "c5889f99-7390-5c8b-ace3-4be3fb47757b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186310334} {:answer/id #uuid "d640ac00-bf89-544e-bb7c-ad79cfdd36ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186310474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8c13585-d0f3-59b9-8c0d-4e5fcb99b25c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186310243} :answer-field/id #uuid "07e9c6d7-afe3-5aee-99f7-a8d468714a00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186310814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8de3c48f-01de-56bf-9ee6-1a3bd0e95cc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335251" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (-1,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (1,2)$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (-1,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since $1+tx>0$). Computing the  partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(1+tx)(1+{x}^{2})}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (1,2)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,2)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (1,2).$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{3}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f80e846-ac27-534a-a725-f39ca5ca5e32" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186309352} {:answer/id #uuid "9dea671c-d517-542c-9844-cfaeaa711a60" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186310022} {:answer/id #uuid "a506c6b6-2284-564c-80c1-f230b001d945" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186310079} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a648f9-d339-5127-b434-7fbefbc0e4f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186310226} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7776d4-1560-53ec-9fcc-60878feed89f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186310636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6a648f9-d339-5127-b434-7fbefbc0e4f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186310226} :answer-field/id #uuid "b61165cc-0943-5bff-9268-8b0e0e9c0962" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b175da7-6b72-569e-916d-da964786e33a" :question/math-academy-id "q-335252" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{-{t}^{2}x}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-2tx{e}^{-{t}^{2}x},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f99b46f-1bfb-592a-8590-5899bae0952b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186308858} {:answer/id #uuid "12a31e39-e59e-5a7c-af39-50efb6880801" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186308883} {:answer/id #uuid "77c96f06-3b49-50a4-9066-9d569fe5b993" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186309687} {:answer/id #uuid "7c55a2a5-0062-5bfa-8d99-7f341c26802d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186309719} {:answer/id #uuid "c55be731-7911-59e5-9425-6aa78a0dc81a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186310332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c55be731-7911-59e5-9425-6aa78a0dc81a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186310332} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2593ec9-6ec0-52ae-8ade-9433bf52750e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0283e52-8fee-5c3a-a13d-c279ee722034" :question/math-academy-id "q-335253" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>-1$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since ${x}^{2}+{t}^{2}>0$ on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-\\frac{t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ so that $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xt)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>-1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{(1+t{x}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2437fa4d-29a9-5143-ad35-cab5794108ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186309020} {:answer/id #uuid "2f79000b-1a1d-53e2-8c95-11ca44047cb5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186309109} {:answer/id #uuid "6f88e01b-9a1f-5545-9075-0ba03094539e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186309610} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9b6164-6b6f-59c4-8f76-6a4bb925415d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186310399} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f1448e-d841-5762-bd59-43fbc9c7dd6e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186310597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd9b6164-6b6f-59c4-8f76-6a4bb925415d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186310399} :answer-field/id #uuid "814cdb8b-e518-56eb-baee-ee5982de94e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "757f1645-7b5c-5866-81f6-7ae1eefa124d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335254" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{1+{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{3}{(x+t+1)^{4}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "689a4721-d905-5dd0-ab19-a3b17d5aad7b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186309553} {:answer/id #uuid "6f758fac-f361-5da4-8ffd-b008a2a5efe4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186309609} {:answer/id #uuid "76301b92-8e49-5056-8988-b54e793563f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186309672} {:answer/id #uuid "e575ecf1-1749-5d8d-8a11-da063e9249a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186310577} {:answer/id #uuid "f87b66e2-96d0-5b1c-a973-1e763283b85e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186310730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "689a4721-d905-5dd0-ab19-a3b17d5aad7b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186309553} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf334cf0-1f36-5980-8df6-c5f2211d44fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7fa63bd-d15a-5bf7-b7e2-ddd1185810da" :question/math-academy-id "q-335255" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)={e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3ca35c-5986-512a-a939-03679884558b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186308844} {:answer/id #uuid "323851ee-65ca-58d4-aba6-3dbe85562ce1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186309127} {:answer/id #uuid "32588f4d-1732-5291-bdb9-37cdfe9b02e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id 17592186309130} {:answer/id #uuid "cc87a612-feb9-5caa-b1a0-d4d7393c6cac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186310388} {:answer/id #uuid "f1105306-5f44-5be5-ad98-9fbf2cd26c6d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186310670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1105306-5f44-5be5-ad98-9fbf2cd26c6d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186310670} :answer-field/id #uuid "35f1abb1-a448-528a-83a3-13ad2bb497f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186310935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e65967cd-c336-53ba-a105-1c4f34ff015c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335824" :question/problem "![](images/96/96df34b3f844ee5abace182769011a3b4b2e413100fbca4cf25e829ca67948cc.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6)$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=2,$ $y=4$ and $y=6.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (4,6).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y-4),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=2,4$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=2,4,6.$\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2).$ This does not correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4,6.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(y-2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (4,6),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a50a8b-6b4b-5499-bfaf-f73e029fb3a0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186308944} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab04844-b9a7-5018-8552-6bfcb710cbb6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186309066} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcbd94a-3f96-5fb9-a8c4-7d16a08c5729" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186309595} {:answer/id #uuid "979282b7-3740-556d-8772-3318b6c3422a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186309965} {:answer/id #uuid "d3931537-1e8d-5f73-8afd-1ba1b3628fba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186310451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3931537-1e8d-5f73-8afd-1ba1b3628fba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186310451} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e5b37a6-7401-5bf7-a3a8-466d6f9e917c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186310834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "870fa3ac-a172-57d1-b18b-7584f7771a44" :question/math-academy-id "q-337223" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+({y}^{′})^{2})$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+1$\n- $y-x({y}^{′})^{2}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}=0$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′}),\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+({y}^{′})^{2}).\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x({y}^{′})^{2}-{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197585d7-48ec-5bfa-bb26-ca9d9544d47b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186308951} {:answer/id #uuid "2af4a78e-e83e-588e-a29b-1ce8712f1067" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186309069} {:answer/id #uuid "5280920d-a88f-5d86-b456-83315d137ef4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186309373} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8e9d50-9bad-5af4-b248-3eae428838e8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186309602} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee70eae-18ae-5be0-af64-a0fc60ceabec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186309886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "197585d7-48ec-5bfa-bb26-ca9d9544d47b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186308951} :answer-field/id #uuid "2925ca55-ddf8-5e7e-a92f-f37ffb46b6a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186310900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76e64e6f-7a8a-5b8b-bac6-612af4c245fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-337226" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′})$\n- $y+x({y}^{′})^{2}={2}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+x$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}).\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=-x({y}^{′})^{2}+{2}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})={2}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03795dba-e4b3-563c-9505-932103c7f09e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186308771} {:answer/id #uuid "0ddef58e-faa9-5b35-abe1-0c78d4dcdfcf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186308841} {:answer/id #uuid "438c4fcc-3c33-5727-8c46-cc5eb658abe3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186309259} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cbf7a4-665b-5b8e-a13e-bb7aebcef7f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186310566} {:answer/id #uuid "f9975ab7-4dcb-58f1-ad62-e809069b4f9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186310739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9975ab7-4dcb-58f1-ad62-e809069b4f9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186310739} :answer-field/id #uuid "77d3e201-d9fd-5f9a-ab64-8446c71c235e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b52c57b-852c-58e2-ae93-cc78b2af7920" :question/math-academy-id "q-337229" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+x$\n- $y+x({y}^{′})^{2}={e}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′})$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={e}^{{y}^{′}}-x({y}^{′})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140fb66d-7dba-5266-9485-d536777408b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186308894} {:answer/id #uuid "46aca1bc-24cc-5256-bd7c-120ad086c2a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186309287} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1d08bf-5824-5597-94aa-977ff6f39c7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186309319} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1ff627-9c16-5002-88d9-5ccbe9ae798f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186310158} {:answer/id #uuid "dec41f0a-e626-59e6-8bc5-6c658f741ff0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186310538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b1d08bf-5824-5597-94aa-977ff6f39c7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186309319} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cc6f94b-06f0-5a01-a6a9-2c2acc2d8019" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af31d734-fa0c-53ba-89f5-33969f158e1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337233" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{y}$\n- $y+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{3}+\\sqrt{1+({y}^{′})^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={e}^{{y}^{′}}-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}),\\,g({y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\sqrt{1+({y}^{′})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "042ed97e-d5ea-5837-9be9-b68218769512" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186308775} {:answer/id #uuid "194a19e8-ed20-5efa-9322-495da5103502" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186308949} {:answer/id #uuid "3021bce8-c9fb-5fd5-b406-379213be3b04" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186309113} {:answer/id #uuid "9138feeb-64d5-5918-a962-3b7647e57e64" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186309910} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5477dc-2f71-5c29-8219-68406c7e07c8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186310626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "042ed97e-d5ea-5837-9be9-b68218769512" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186308775} :answer-field/id #uuid "b838dbea-d2c1-50d5-976c-d7243d3f2e56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a1da43a-e002-579e-9946-31a6ae805b48" :question/math-academy-id "q-337245" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{3}+({y}^{′})^{2}$\n- $y-x\\sqrt{1+{y}^{′}}+\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{′}}=0$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3},\\,g({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x\\sqrt{1+{y}^{′}}-\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21da9bb8-c049-5cb4-b242-7fc53d58be9c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186309002} {:answer/id #uuid "28135ff8-1d3c-5e20-8b2e-db510d594cb2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186309050} {:answer/id #uuid "8befa612-e0bf-5637-8864-2a59a933e33d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186309852} {:answer/id #uuid "c37f5f21-7e6b-5baf-b911-1b95958af413" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186310314} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8ad1a6-dc6e-51a7-adb5-61e0148c87e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186310657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28135ff8-1d3c-5e20-8b2e-db510d594cb2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186309050} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e64c49-7e98-5327-9e8e-29def6193e77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4fa353d-a0fb-5d32-a867-d6df2715a793" :question/math-academy-id "q-337765" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=(x-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=x-1$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=2x.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $y,$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=4,{f}_{2}(x)=0,{f}_{1}(x)=-{x}^{2},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4231943f-e21b-50ba-b62b-35503d9e1c14" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186309249} {:answer/id #uuid "762dbd80-e8cb-56f2-904c-d215284cfc36" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186309671} {:answer/id #uuid "a68ccd64-f0de-545e-8960-d6ea998f5120" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186310091} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8d38b2-de59-57b9-aa32-220c842de69c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186310523} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f32e63-9cbc-50c5-83b3-387841bd1945" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186310687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3f32e63-9cbc-50c5-83b3-387841bd1945" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186310687} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9f0052c-6498-5ac3-bb74-14bd0b20b5af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ea3d35e-7e2f-5e81-99fa-fd738c89805d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337773" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{x}y$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)={e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}},{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the last term is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23747e65-928b-5e4b-922e-e73b4e74a8b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186309017} {:answer/id #uuid "41869df2-afa5-5cf9-9e61-0be681edd492" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186309247} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cc32ae-f43a-591b-bcf0-7e39e3a79e1f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186310244} {:answer/id #uuid "d96b2d30-06cd-5824-8b85-a6c3aec875a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186310497} {:answer/id #uuid "f3fbed37-7e33-533f-9780-dff263a9ecd3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186310688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d96b2d30-06cd-5824-8b85-a6c3aec875a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186310497} :answer-field/id #uuid "e04e99c3-eacd-51df-af42-343b6b1f4b04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a7fcf4e-fdda-501c-b765-bd8e87f5aae0" :question/math-academy-id "q-337774" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x),{f}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dfad69d-c539-5aa8-a20b-cc48752c73d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186309340} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6d65b4-f9e8-5193-9ddf-a4b88db71dc9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186309454} {:answer/id #uuid "6e68bff1-c792-51cd-80a6-4308ab9f4ebc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186309599} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf55dfd-8ad3-5325-bea5-c4b38e781953" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186309853} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1d35bc-9eb2-5186-8a5b-702c7a52fc33" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186310750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb1d35bc-9eb2-5186-8a5b-702c7a52fc33" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186310750} :answer-field/id #uuid "af547b4f-a6df-56ac-aa0e-831ceeb8578c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47d8bb1c-4d46-5c7b-bec5-01c857be1d48" :question/math-academy-id "q-337777" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{x}y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $xy,$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),{f}_{1}(x)={x}^{2},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)={e}^{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the last term is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d87b94e-2a58-5a91-b413-e2ace8c0acef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186308839} {:answer/id #uuid "3977be65-6745-5b00-9e8b-2471e205c872" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186309184} {:answer/id #uuid "62e91591-c9db-593a-9041-dbbc0a50e178" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186309500} {:answer/id #uuid "7f143cd7-e11a-59b4-bd54-f611d460cc2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186309741} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ed839d-2200-51a0-8970-9289668f781e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186310471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62e91591-c9db-593a-9041-dbbc0a50e178" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186309500} :answer-field/id #uuid "0af460f7-9e3d-5968-8b8f-b6d594daad31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186310825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a6ed137-ea67-591f-bf1a-a0d1b0037ea7" :question/math-academy-id "q-337778" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nI. ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,y$\n\nII. ${D}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y$\n\nIII. ${D}_{3}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x},{f}_{1}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-2.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=2,{f}_{2}(x)={e}^{x},{f}_{1}(x)=0,$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a0694d-5ba0-59c1-a8e5-2c4573d2c912" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186308875} {:answer/id #uuid "24881b5f-7168-5dde-aa2f-79d5822c355c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186309021} {:answer/id #uuid "55ec1c8a-0464-50bf-880d-57363f13b364" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186309395} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc67f7e-ec91-51b5-99c7-c36fc0d9ae01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186309469} {:answer/id #uuid "ad64757b-2e99-59cc-8fd4-49759964480f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186310150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fc67f7e-ec91-51b5-99c7-c36fc0d9ae01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186309469} :answer-field/id #uuid "839362f3-a078-55ac-808a-c2140ece70d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5914278-adab-5478-b262-9e2341a90a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-338102" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}$\n- $y{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x$\n- $5{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+7{y}^{2}=x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=x,b(x)=0,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n5{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+7{y}^{2}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $7{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186311917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a7940b-0f3f-508b-a871-15800c6fb8b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186308818} {:answer/id #uuid "36582925-902c-57ac-a95b-7adf28f5b5dd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186309163} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fd2fea-f0c6-5fc6-b76e-5c25cb8f9b2b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186310213} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8fdc15-819c-5879-ae73-8350d36043df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186310651} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf1f99a-680a-5646-b2a0-1bc10f15965f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186310781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdf1f99a-680a-5646-b2a0-1bc10f15965f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186310781} :answer-field/id #uuid "698a23fe-8a46-5cb4-b72e-ffe832d89c98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1c5937c-0d4d-5848-8380-65f08dc10e61" :question/math-academy-id "q-338104" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=(1+{x}^{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=1+{x}^{2},{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186314699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09178b04-bd98-588e-bede-8ee7e96e2b49" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186312041} {:answer/id #uuid "37c76a34-ae9b-5e38-97da-1b66a8487677" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186312425} {:answer/id #uuid "74b0f70f-341f-56cb-ac17-2e8b98cdbe22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186312905} {:answer/id #uuid "cc62253a-567e-5b64-a9ab-f6d7d1c562d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186313573} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d27d08-7b11-540f-86bb-babed660a878" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186313956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7d27d08-7b11-540f-86bb-babed660a878" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186313956} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1db2b22-e47d-53ca-9e53-25c549e46a18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dabe6837-4031-5ea3-85c8-7a53d65944dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-338531" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x},\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(2)=0$\n- ${y}^{″}+4y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-1$\n- ${y}^{″}-y={x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified."}] [{:db/id 17592186314700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07efab25-6886-5e52-8d46-c2a559ac1019" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186312031} {:answer/id #uuid "2c7098cf-b116-5904-9694-dedeb9dc5128" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186312320} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8a6a02-d4d5-5c01-b6fd-2ff5cda3a0ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186313437} {:answer/id #uuid "ceee585a-3847-56cc-a47b-dca9a37e5e0f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186313593} {:answer/id #uuid "d756000d-75d3-53ac-a8fe-9bffae524109" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186313673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb8a6a02-d4d5-5c01-b6fd-2ff5cda3a0ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186313437} :answer-field/id #uuid "752d6c27-fdee-5fe5-8c2a-6e555a305157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce074393-6c5e-5e9b-ab9a-92fd971d7d06" :question/math-academy-id "q-338533" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=2,\\,y(5)=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+y={x}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(-3)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-3)=1$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-3$)."}] [{:db/id 17592186314701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be47449-5ee2-57a9-8729-139c804b67b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186312062} {:answer/id #uuid "20b7057f-8151-5aee-b7e3-21eb04501cb2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186312224} {:answer/id #uuid "35454e6e-1293-518d-83d7-8733d296c529" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186312404} {:answer/id #uuid "83a920aa-cfc7-5778-8108-3a459f40e158" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186313035} {:answer/id #uuid "e91f4b05-eb68-5bca-8eb6-de17a14f1767" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186313831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83a920aa-cfc7-5778-8108-3a459f40e158" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186313035} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b10264-2c46-5bec-8c28-bce288ba276a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fcfd17f-1887-5fc2-a463-d8fe70a76b3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-338539" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+y=x{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=3,\\,y(4)=3$\n- ${y}^{″}-y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0,\\,y(-2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(-2)=0$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=4$).\n\n- The second system is not a boundary value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the *same* location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-2$). This is an **initial value problem.**"}] [{:db/id 17592186314708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "130c81ac-e871-55cc-95b8-7a7a071fc1c8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186312110} {:answer/id #uuid "3a6425e3-1e59-5813-a34b-10a58f5f7d89" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186312449} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3efadc-48f9-5604-a7a8-8caf593805bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186312952} {:answer/id #uuid "7b7ded43-bef8-521b-8b55-f75b5437f787" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186312962} {:answer/id #uuid "bc693c5e-f884-5c43-b661-2e0cd8f961c2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186313443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a6425e3-1e59-5813-a34b-10a58f5f7d89" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186312449} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f41a75e-38f6-5ddd-9832-01919cd770a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4fd4473-8c46-5808-aedb-c06540dd956d" :question/math-academy-id "q-338595" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(2)=0$\n- $3{y}^{″}-y={e}^{x},\\,y(1)=4$\n- ${y}^{″}+y={x}^{2},\\,y(-1)=2,\\,y(1)=2$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$).\n\n- The second system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition.\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=1$)."}] [{:db/id 17592186314763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232e1687-2b34-5620-82b4-0d138f862182" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186312236} {:answer/id #uuid "4b38d8ec-d531-5cf9-b4c3-9bd0e7ce8dd1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186312585} {:answer/id #uuid "735e065c-1988-56ad-8759-0134dabdff16" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186312888} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b8076f-3089-5a15-89d0-1919cd69c1ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186313484} {:answer/id #uuid "e878fa27-db60-57da-aa04-d934c1901f38" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186313825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "232e1687-2b34-5620-82b4-0d138f862182" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186312236} :answer-field/id #uuid "02efa76e-cd06-50a0-bb62-a3fd2816554d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "baccee23-0e1a-561a-a3ca-cf008d4fd658" :question/math-academy-id "q-339253" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-y=4x$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-5y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(2)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=3$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6x{y}^{′}+9y={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(5)=-2$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=2$).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=5$). This is a **boundary value problem.**"}] [{:db/id 17592186314764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c114e9e-4a35-5945-be53-ea661282072d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186312821} {:answer/id #uuid "70e8738e-d541-52e5-8498-0cf69b712026" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186312862} {:answer/id #uuid "a838513d-8c93-5cae-a2db-0967ee3fec2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186313293} {:answer/id #uuid "efbf4849-338d-5e69-8c8c-a8000a2b3187" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186313894} {:answer/id #uuid "f2353756-54d9-5b2e-88d7-60288c65a536" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186313917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2353756-54d9-5b2e-88d7-60288c65a536" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186313917} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a9980f7-9c44-52eb-95f1-350e7a4948cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99d74bf8-bcac-5e65-b877-61e885b1b024" :question/math-academy-id "q-339254" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x,\\,y(-1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y=8$\n- $x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-3y={e}^{x},\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem.  It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$)."}] [{:db/id 17592186314791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5385c7ea-97b0-5cd3-b407-e0b5a65933bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186312653} {:answer/id #uuid "60093836-8629-59c4-80d3-2bc1f040c2be" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186312745} {:answer/id #uuid "7f10b445-8e28-57fa-9ef4-9ffb5015d937" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186312997} {:answer/id #uuid "817a6641-15a5-5723-b261-fdac7757ebb2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186313019} {:answer/id #uuid "bd83da3b-2e54-5b82-960c-036904d3cfa6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186313458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5385c7ea-97b0-5cd3-b407-e0b5a65933bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186312653} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad326b15-681f-596d-b066-c89a18877887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27876a51-c9f8-5f4d-9c9b-be85b540294f" :question/math-academy-id "q-339539" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}^{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\,{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+3t\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}, &  & b(t)=-2, &  & f(t)=1, \\\\ c(t)=\\sqrt{7}, &  & d(t)=5, &  & g(t)=3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186314792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b98914cb-c5a5-53a9-b026-3e2bf2976a77" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186313418} {:answer/id #uuid "c7813db9-4ef5-5b5f-8d21-f012fa56d240" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186313534} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdd177c-9b09-58cd-9378-0fef1fe8aa76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186313705} {:answer/id #uuid "eb0514a8-4aa2-54dd-8f61-2feccf10f107" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186313845} {:answer/id #uuid "f9390918-851c-5846-8e6d-631d52dd4ee7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186313969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb0514a8-4aa2-54dd-8f61-2feccf10f107" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186313845} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c14c918-1ed3-5833-b916-578a7a9df365" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "686f5a84-c1ca-551b-b5e9-fd8d6a7feafb" :question/math-academy-id "q-339540" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t)\\,{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+\\sqrt{3}\\,t\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}){x}_{1}(t)+{13}^{2}{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{1}(t)-\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}^{2}(t)+{x}_{2}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t), &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=2, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\,t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}), &  & b(t)={13}^{2}, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=9, &  & d(t)=-\\pi , &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the terms ${x}_{1}^{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186314793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053f3697-b8c6-5f4c-bddf-90e6089b5f5b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186312009} {:answer/id #uuid "14efacbd-4a68-5f00-b65f-c48221075050" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186312122} {:answer/id #uuid "8608580c-b3af-534d-9668-e2ae2c103214" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186313050} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6aa881-f44f-5466-9462-3fbc1987f153" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186313219} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8e6aaf-ceae-5f11-b643-0c2063cd8518" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186313588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d6aa881-f44f-5466-9462-3fbc1987f153" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186313219} :answer-field/id #uuid "a862c719-4ccd-5ed8-9143-c972a25dc1af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4edb4632-82c5-5949-9b3d-34461c2dad7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339543" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,{x}_{1}(t)+2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=t\\,{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)+t\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}^{2}(t)+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)=2, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=t, &  & b(t)=-1, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=0, &  & d(t)=1, &  & g(t)=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}^{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186314794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42eb79ee-12fc-5ebe-ae84-5140c7e8f9d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186312508} {:answer/id #uuid "5eed0038-5857-5699-b170-89ae78c852e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186312733} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ca7dfa-d660-570a-8248-4046d8e69f27" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186313255} {:answer/id #uuid "d9682625-a9ab-5c8a-a507-8e4b8411a9ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186313685} {:answer/id #uuid "ee323000-79c5-521c-98bd-93aa3131775f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186313875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2ca7dfa-d660-570a-8248-4046d8e69f27" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186313255} :answer-field/id #uuid "997d6138-d847-5aa5-a81c-5fbc072a03ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60e129ea-a029-5585-b3d8-45191fe84a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-339547" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}^{2}(t)-{x}_{2}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=7{x}_{1}(t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=0, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the terms ${x}_{1}^{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=7, &  & b(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186314795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "385ba27a-e049-525b-a5d8-dc375ac40522" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186312431} {:answer/id #uuid "5d706bb9-8917-5f9b-87f5-2f2eb19da810" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186312721} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff99d3e-e281-5989-a314-caaa6c8573c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186312743} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3f91dc-d67f-5295-be0d-c243d8997671" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186313085} {:answer/id #uuid "d57330ed-602f-5498-83d1-414994e3b76b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186313657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "385ba27a-e049-525b-a5d8-dc375ac40522" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186312431} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c2d3ab7-8136-5ffe-8c53-662bed8250f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91a61e63-0bab-516c-b096-3ec2dc905625" :question/math-academy-id "q-339548" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)-8{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))^{2}{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=3, &  & b(t)=-8, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186314812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "089fa75c-22da-589f-8db5-c808b4588e6b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186312038} {:answer/id #uuid "1fcf70e1-e4ec-5357-9cca-54bb5671779e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186312214} {:answer/id #uuid "35af6117-640d-5a96-ab6d-e3fc035b3687" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186312407} {:answer/id #uuid "d66f778c-0d98-537d-ac0d-5f4f9c886db5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186313665} {:answer/id #uuid "ee27ed23-4145-5110-aeb6-48b4a7020130" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186313874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d66f778c-0d98-537d-ac0d-5f4f9c886db5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186313665} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d7fbaa0-4bef-5461-b36b-f05ed1300373" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d50aebed-a4c6-5b64-b5c3-ebb00a836540" :question/math-academy-id "q-339658" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)$" :question/worked-solution "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\n- System II is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186314813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b82825d-196f-5a80-98c4-5f569521512e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186313089} {:answer/id #uuid "93976ac9-ba07-5d4f-a328-f88b71eda228" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186313159} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a93f52-c153-5f99-8635-f8bf4809a506" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186313490} {:answer/id #uuid "ded42c0e-852b-5b4f-afb3-14ecb23bc461" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186313726} {:answer/id #uuid "fde2e8ad-9f47-56b3-9724-004a17610772" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186314000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ded42c0e-852b-5b4f-afb3-14ecb23bc461" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186313726} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a6a539c-6ac6-595f-adcc-883beb90512e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d86591cd-cf9c-5b7c-ab46-ee83fbeda055" :question/math-academy-id "q-339661" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\n- System III is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186314814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302adfae-ee3c-5c66-8dd2-e7a4b320fbab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186312363} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef31b0a-b9d9-5492-aa9a-b36315ad404d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186312847} {:answer/id #uuid "741a1eaf-5e3b-5a67-adc0-f1af5f8ff2a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186312898} {:answer/id #uuid "d3481cdd-2ea0-5be0-9e9e-6bab9b38c0cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186313633} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a3e913-06e4-58ac-86a0-e605bf487ce3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186313953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7a3e913-06e4-58ac-86a0-e605bf487ce3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186313953} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd566448-f834-5b9c-9174-d334a70fb817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a032209-c882-5496-a788-3681657f5173" :question/math-academy-id "q-339663" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+t\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-2, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=4, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=1, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=0, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186314815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da9d30b-9af5-5213-8917-9446376514c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186312071} {:answer/id #uuid "589393df-7bc7-5a90-8263-53d67c64983c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186312685} {:answer/id #uuid "70f839cb-952f-531a-b542-262e1a7103b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186312863} {:answer/id #uuid "78cc97f9-33e7-59d4-9b18-c4c9a83c9ded" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186312937} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe8553b-5c76-5132-885a-602051f2f1e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186313858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78cc97f9-33e7-59d4-9b18-c4c9a83c9ded" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186312937} :answer-field/id #uuid "13b7a5d2-c30b-5f43-a916-f63fc3d12cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b869e45a-6b1f-53e1-aacc-10ffe2baaefd" :question/math-academy-id "q-339664" :question/problem "![](images/52/528bf1bc9d6177f031b8b5504641903a11dfc2c2576e4759bf4cb7c278d0585c.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=0$ is a sink\n\nII. $y=3$ is a source\n\nIII. $y=3$ is a node" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=0$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=0.$ This means that $y=0$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=3$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from $y=3.$ This means that $y=3$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186314816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34f5ba07-2d39-5284-88cd-8dafd1509296" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186312400} {:answer/id #uuid "7613e263-9952-5990-aed8-826a643f14f5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186312918} {:answer/id #uuid "77245c0e-5f7d-5be5-96cb-2e2adf948029" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186312928} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a7044f-69f2-5fc9-8c12-bae63f8c52ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186313298} {:answer/id #uuid "c1568721-486b-5389-baeb-a6e48e4aa8b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186313487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7613e263-9952-5990-aed8-826a643f14f5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186312918} :answer-field/id #uuid "7739b5fc-3cfd-54b2-89bc-2d7d4e08bd4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "834c59a7-4f2c-54c0-913b-f196a907cc0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339669" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)+4{e}^{t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)-4{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5){x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-3, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6t) \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-2, &  & g(t)=2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=1, &  & b(t)=3, &  & f(t)=4{e}^{t} \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-4, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5), &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186314817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2be005df-3982-55df-ac3e-cd80e88c2c16" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186312317} {:answer/id #uuid "2eac7b39-f4ed-5b3e-b6fc-b793afff61bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186312342} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae58956-3b44-5a41-9c31-4728d4238f23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186312699} {:answer/id #uuid "617292e0-5bad-5157-9d1b-55723ea846a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186312753} {:answer/id #uuid "8c25d28e-3369-5dca-8378-55d7cc09bbd2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186313095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c25d28e-3369-5dca-8378-55d7cc09bbd2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186313095} :answer-field/id #uuid "a38d082a-8435-5db0-b65d-c71130dc183e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6099dfb-5cd3-520d-98c0-7759f4ae7ca2" :question/math-academy-id "q-339672" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)+1\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+{e}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)+3t\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=3, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t) \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=4, &  & g(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-1, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=5, &  & d(t)=6, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=4, &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)={e}^{2} \\\\ c(t)=0, &  & d(t)=1, &  & g(t)=3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186314829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a0c2ee-c46e-5868-b641-d39caff08766" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186312378} {:answer/id #uuid "4bde6670-ef25-5ac4-a615-8158d1a9abb7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186312589} {:answer/id #uuid "6b63cdf5-5dd5-5050-90cf-c7e5ad3c88b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186312816} {:answer/id #uuid "6be54e27-a6cf-5aa7-9e45-f12a3672c434" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186312820} {:answer/id #uuid "def122b9-c29d-54fe-a5a0-59a579e457d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186313727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31a0c2ee-c46e-5868-b641-d39caff08766" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186312378} :answer-field/id #uuid "58b6b0b3-60ba-5893-9027-7f391a26bf55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb8d059f-7809-5041-a8a8-a0657d861dc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-339707" :question/problem "![](images/21/21970ba0ad8ae32ea95ad4f253d7be63aefe90316415f1aebad15c1fb3ccfd25.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=1$ is a node.\n- $y=1$ is a sink.\n- $y=-3$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/ec/eca3b223699f18fbb09c6657708b7680c4228f52323018168202351820360d9d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=1$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186314830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b86a59d-4cdc-59aa-941e-4c3c9d08e39d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186312312} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd1de06-ce78-5592-9c8f-c0e4d83feddb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186312356} {:answer/id #uuid "32ed33e3-2784-5014-ae1d-18024611d7f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186312388} {:answer/id #uuid "a17ad547-fd94-580a-ae51-377a500870a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186313244} {:answer/id #uuid "e237f762-cfea-5eff-8131-f91756c50b08" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186313757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e237f762-cfea-5eff-8131-f91756c50b08" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186313757} :answer-field/id #uuid "27f330e9-1904-5a76-b1e6-bccb74f47256" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186314115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e0251a4-2b0b-541f-aa9b-c1b63489b966" :question/math-academy-id "q-339708" :question/problem "![](images/08/08bb9e4aed81bc739fd63e167058ff4f98ed98d022071396fbfb225271cc25b1.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=4$ is a node.\n- $y=4$ is a source.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/7c/7cc5955c5268c6e933265632082a0d5be4fa87c96118b011351b71aa9ee89a7d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=4$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:d