{:db/id 17592186317172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c07460d-1eec-5672-90f4-7116bb6897fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186315377} {:answer/id #uuid "5e82cbb5-72e9-5b8b-95fb-01801ff4b6f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186315650} {:answer/id #uuid "877cb6e8-1876-5f80-a0b2-98a2462c4775" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186315878} {:answer/id #uuid "a54e12c5-211f-5ac8-b470-1cfd82428117" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186316049} {:answer/id #uuid "b1cd5717-22ff-5564-89cb-a84b93a092d7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186316124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c07460d-1eec-5672-90f4-7116bb6897fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186315377} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ac1933-5ac4-59df-9437-0db31d242812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186316637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "278579d2-6c97-5a07-b8af-1fbac0d66174" :question/math-academy-id "q-34174" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4bd3b326c89316bc916658421bcd4c65c32a72672af9fbd7aa5397cb53ab374.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$  lies inside the given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186317229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b7d38b-59e8-5c99-baf1-8ebca2bb3e9b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186315394} {:answer/id #uuid "33432922-1ca0-5ab4-81d5-8b2d3857e426" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186315417} {:answer/id #uuid "36e2aefa-28e8-537d-a0b0-a16e4b71dc0e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186315437} {:answer/id #uuid "697157b6-d0b7-514a-85b2-51f3d2ff6cad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186315714} {:answer/id #uuid "f5260582-e5a1-582d-8cf8-b708b93e1374" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186316487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33432922-1ca0-5ab4-81d5-8b2d3857e426" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186315417} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe195ec3-885a-500a-a80c-d7dfafea1cf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186317153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8ba2fd2-65b4-550d-8354-517b0631809b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342275" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{(x+4)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-4$\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{x+4}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-4,$ since both denominators at $x=-4$ equal zero. So, $x=-4$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186317230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d51af1-e503-5a7e-b5c4-3ae217bde11e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186315305} {:answer/id #uuid "24800d2a-5fc1-582a-9fd9-908f2235d74f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186315323} {:answer/id #uuid "317ac0b1-2810-51b7-8d45-3ccec2ac879c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186315401} {:answer/id #uuid "a902dbd9-63b6-5ef6-bbe8-ce3be56fbbc0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186316067} {:answer/id #uuid "ac091dc7-3951-5fcc-8f2c-d6262ff6a701" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186316087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "317ac0b1-2810-51b7-8d45-3ccec2ac879c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186315401} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c9a3fe6-a8ea-5181-ac25-7f8ac8fec6c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186316868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "905b994c-71d1-59e7-beab-bb9f96b34458" :question/math-academy-id "q-342279" :question/problem "$$\n(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+2xy=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-3}{x+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{2x}{x+1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-3}{x+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{2x}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-1,$ since both denominators at $x=-1$ equal zero. So, $x=-1$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=3.$ So, $x=3$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186317231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "325de50a-bd2e-5b08-8def-2a320b8858b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186315411} {:answer/id #uuid "43571603-dcf9-5d5f-b4d5-068e7b710ac5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186315507} {:answer/id #uuid "4960eaa9-2ef2-55f9-9e74-72b970724f7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186315540} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ca10b7-c88f-59b6-99d2-5015abb15acd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186316370} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fc1a8c-df7b-5734-81b5-5015a8867753" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186316485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4fc1a8c-df7b-5734-81b5-5015a8867753" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186316485} :answer-field/id #uuid "0998a080-e070-520b-b84d-c64f605ce95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186316574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aae5f1f1-0d61-5bac-bdd4-df8a71221a89" :question/math-academy-id "q-342290" :question/problem "$$\n(x-2)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is an **ordinary** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-2)(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-1}{(x-2)(x+3)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x+3)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x-2)(x+3)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=2,$ since both denominators at $x=2$ equal zero. So, $x=2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186317232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3778da-c7c9-52dd-a4f4-b677913ed30e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186315211} {:answer/id #uuid "4af8856d-b077-55be-a4c5-06637c7bf5a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186315546} {:answer/id #uuid "5897673e-eff1-5308-8269-e1dce86b32e7" :answer/type {:db/id 1759218