15422e7-0f13-5981-b899-ff599464c03e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186337045} {:answer/id #uuid "8648494b-e924-5c2c-9ea0-e87367ae71b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337247} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e52c73-e245-5532-aa9e-cc0fde9e9d97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186337498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8648494b-e924-5c2c-9ea0-e87367ae71b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337247} :answer-field/id #uuid "f311d111-581f-59e1-8837-7f929a884ad6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d44ff842-4620-550a-bab6-87cb0a1ac002" :question/math-academy-id "q-71179" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}3x, & x\\ne 4 \\\\ 13, & x=4,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=g(4)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(4)=13$ exists$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}3x=12, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}3x=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12,$ while the value of the function is $g(4)=13.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)\\ne g(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=4,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186338935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f126da-5f3b-5af8-9aee-d6f0a4c61c49" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Shape of a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03afa3f4-6ea9-5c81-a348-fa186894d2eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186336065} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4d62de-3a97-57b8-be6c-ba2fc6704e58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186336427} {:answer/id #uuid "acd31018-2732-5a9d-97a2-4de86a5828c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186337585} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f8155b-65ed-551d-a9b8-5cddb95b41cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186337783} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a2d819-9cd3-5847-8953-1d7a843d477a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186338057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acd31018-2732-5a9d-97a2-4de86a5828c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186337585} :answer-field/id #uuid "47214f3b-43c1-54f5-a0d8-4d5bdf8ec580" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e04ff7ee-ed94-5861-b7e8-a3f79254490c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71391" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4}{{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=3$ gives a hyperbola\n- $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n- $c=1$ gives a circle" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4}{{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4 & =c{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(3-c){y}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=3$ then the level curves are $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2},$ which consists of two vertical lines.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=4,$ which is an ellipse.\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2,$ which is a circle.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186338937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f126da-5f3b-5af8-9aee-d6f0a4c61c49" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Shape of a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a905dcc-7062-5ba9-9481-732d5b5264b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186336131} {:answer/id #uuid "92663878-d31f-5b9a-82dc-e0531f783d7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186337363} {:answer/id #uuid "9e18aeef-f61b-5c4d-a0f7-d57397008dc9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186337462} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ece99e-e9d1-5800-b5d5-2a569fbee7c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186337538} {:answer/id #uuid "c045c018-7da4-5278-a1f9-dc81aff7cf6b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92663878-d31f-5b9a-82dc-e0531f783d7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186337363} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f5ebcf-28da-59c5-ae3f-90358c258495" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b94a6f8a-60a9-5806-9e18-4814d39c5835" :question/math-academy-id "q-71395" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=1$ gives a line\n\nII. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola\n\nIII. $c=0$ gives two lines" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}-2{y}^{2} & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=1,$ which is not a line.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=4,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $y=\\pm \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}},$ which consists of two lines.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186338988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3149834-ef92-5016-a84a-40b207fee519" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b674f7a-25d9-5f6b-9857-8fdce265e72a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186336284} {:answer/id #uuid "225f0767-63be-518e-a5bf-baea693e346a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186336353} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba35cbb-90df-55e2-b446-b761afe65d3c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186336865} {:answer/id #uuid "742e3dd0-d8b6-5c11-9e94-7709534a2413" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337075} {:answer/id #uuid "edf09fa1-30d1-54e1-bbc3-a280e8820fb6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186338112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edf09fa1-30d1-54e1-bbc3-a280e8820fb6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186338112} :answer-field/id #uuid "817fc8b5-b8fe-5b39-802d-d08801384e9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1e22680-b6e5-5705-939d-f4a384a3db7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71791" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- $(3,5)\\cup [2,3)$\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $(0,2]\\cup (7,10]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $[-1,1]$ is closed. The boundary points are $-1$, and $1.$ All of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other sets are not closed because:\n\n- $(3,5)\\cup [2,3)$ has a boundary point $3$, which does not lie in the set.\n\n- $(0,2]\\cup (7,10]$ has a boundary point $0$, which does not lie in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186338989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3149834-ef92-5016-a84a-40b207fee519" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a950bae-a0dc-516e-a6da-d49abd3ddb51" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186336429} {:answer/id #uuid "a4da8b60-1761-5db2-bbc6-23876dc9e1d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186337529} {:answer/id #uuid "be6ccf1d-9a89-59b4-bcc1-ec4de6d5b210" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337724} {:answer/id #uuid "da0d2b1d-9554-503f-9437-f6bc4dee0644" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186337931} {:answer/id #uuid "f4708771-0601-5132-9251-09f500e6cb5f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186338167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4da8b60-1761-5db2-bbc6-23876dc9e1d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186337529} :answer-field/id #uuid "c988a9b1-2f1a-544f-aff6-2e0d71da90c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20e43566-ea3c-59ee-a80b-84f1522c9eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-71798" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are open?\n\nI. $(-\\infty ,-2)$\n\nII. $(0,2]\\cup [7,9]$\n\nIII. $[-5,-1]\\cup (2,4)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $(-\\infty ,-2)$ is open. The boundary point of the set is $-2$ and it does not lie in the set.\n\nThe other sets are not open because:\n\n- $(0,2]\\cup [7,9]$ has four boundary points $0$, $2$, $7$, and $9$, and the points $2$, $7$, and $9$ lies in the set.\n\n- $[-5,-1]\\cup (2,4)$ has four boundary points $-5$, $-1$, $2$, and $4$, and the points $-5$ and $-1$ lie in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186338991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3149834-ef92-5016-a84a-40b207fee519" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0012e9cc-af21-59ec-a97b-dcb82f9e5ddb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186336030} {:answer/id #uuid "1dab3ced-c7f9-566a-9417-2e0b27a6a4c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186336308} {:answer/id #uuid "4b26b5b0-3a27-5c4f-a158-69cddf0a1bd5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186336726} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc0d3b6-9b69-5865-97af-0b453225227a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337151} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe019e4-4f70-504a-a6c3-0a622e10ef27" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186337332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dab3ced-c7f9-566a-9417-2e0b27a6a4c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186336308} :answer-field/id #uuid "91b4065d-5e15-57b6-820e-26f308d7946b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be4bb9a2-432f-5bf7-aa6c-466035df5777" :question/math-academy-id "q-71802" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are open?\n\n- $[-7,1]$\n- $(-1,3]\\cup [4,5)$\n- $(-5,-1)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $(-5,-1)$ is open. The boundary points are $-5$, and $-1.$ None of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other sets are not open because:\n\n- $[-7,1]$ has two boundary points $-7$, and $1$, and both of them lie in the set.\n\n- $(-1,3]\\cup [4,5)$ has four boundary points $-1$, $3$, $4$, and $5$, and the points $3$ and $4$ lie in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186338993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39cf0de3-8586-5a80-9765-fc15ba42c46e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186336576} {:answer/id #uuid "58acf433-8e2d-5202-98f0-948f7ab6f841" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186336842} {:answer/id #uuid "744377f7-616f-53fe-8956-33395bca738f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186337078} {:answer/id #uuid "9faf3b1c-83dd-58e9-94dc-8563f19f9873" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337477} {:answer/id #uuid "a15f9904-0853-571b-95c3-d7479f11a3ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186337494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9faf3b1c-83dd-58e9-94dc-8563f19f9873" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337477} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f7b347c-d8ac-5574-a932-e414cda7b1ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1615097-eb1e-5984-b134-50e4277f6126" :question/math-academy-id "q-71814" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }x<y<{x}^{2},\\,1<x\\le 2\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }2<y<x\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }4\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,3).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(2,2).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186338994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034ee953-8e42-5d4f-9ffd-643223680ded" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186336061} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e659c-5d07-52c4-b526-23b3bfb65498" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186337010} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ee5674-04b9-5043-b281-83cb64765c0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186337542} {:answer/id #uuid "b5423d8f-866a-57fb-bf8c-161986bd620e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186337645} {:answer/id #uuid "ba69c603-d9dc-5d2b-97b6-5045ea9697f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e659c-5d07-52c4-b526-23b3bfb65498" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186337010} :answer-field/id #uuid "81c3396a-b700-5d8b-a2a8-a611018f6a4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8018b9d7-b35d-54ca-b97a-1ac935d3c057" :question/math-academy-id "q-71815" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }2x+y<6,\\,0<x<3}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }1\\le x<3,\\,2<y\\le 4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}<6}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,4).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third set is *open *since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186338997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72e2207b-6055-51e8-b7a7-061a705d6dd2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186337066} {:answer/id #uuid "c144e221-9cad-536d-88a0-6a790cfd45a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337748} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3e80f8-20a8-5657-8f6a-fe53342b0195" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186337837} {:answer/id #uuid "d1da01dd-af9e-59c1-b556-cce9316f423b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186337869} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1efdb1-2fef-576a-9d2e-70d7b90bcb04" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186338260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c144e221-9cad-536d-88a0-6a790cfd45a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337748} :answer-field/id #uuid "a372a90e-3a78-5b4d-91cf-9dfd092483e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef298213-bf03-538b-9273-2913d7465266" :question/math-academy-id "q-71819" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0<{y}^{2}\\le 2\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<{x}^{2}<9,\\,1<{y}^{2}<4\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x<1,\\,{y}^{2}<4\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,\\sqrt{2}).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186338998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4acd24fd-f5ea-50cf-9dad-9929014c3768" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186336720} {:answer/id #uuid "78c37609-0b21-50bd-824a-787ead460fb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186337119} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1050c0-4e22-52bb-bd91-b38c648eab7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337157} {:answer/id #uuid "b528becf-dbd1-5ac8-ba44-16cb5d5d19c5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186337641} {:answer/id #uuid "dd75987b-7a23-561a-b0f8-99cc85254680" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186337958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b528becf-dbd1-5ac8-ba44-16cb5d5d19c5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186337641} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad5cf70d-5c88-583b-b888-85260ecff5c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d61f9b2d-e5ea-58f1-be0f-837958c85bd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-71820" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<{x}^{2}<9,\\,1<y\\le 4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,4).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186339016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25bf70ff-b994-5535-8efe-5e571a827da4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $S$ only" :db/id 17592186336384} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2c2ef0-4441-556c-91d1-869591ce8b28" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id 17592186336609} {:answer/id #uuid "974473d9-6217-5b21-b771-e42c139b5bb7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id 17592186337409} {:answer/id #uuid "eccc2950-0821-56f5-a1c8-330d5b81af47" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id 17592186338097} {:answer/id #uuid "f7124a5c-f1e9-5c26-9aa6-b10cf5197f80" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id 17592186338198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25bf70ff-b994-5535-8efe-5e571a827da4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $S$ only" :db/id 17592186336384} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e04abee-98c1-555b-a5ad-820352237a1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6118feb2-c751-599a-b3b9-c8c760c49b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71934" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 10 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $R$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(R)=5\\cdot 4-10\\cdot 2=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(R)=0,$ we conclude that $R$ is not invertible.\n\n- The matrix $S$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $T$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(T)=2\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(T)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $T$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $R$ and $S$ do not have an inverse."}] [{:db/id 17592186339017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fe7a17e-699b-5815-9a3e-8c195290665d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ only" :db/id 17592186336634} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe808c6-8c78-5b74-b734-4332d2bbc55a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z$ only" :db/id 17592186336765} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c41241-68f5-537f-a2f4-36ef3a8fa0d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ and $Z$ only" :db/id 17592186337782} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3208fb-30da-50b9-a09e-289044fe0c17" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Y$ only" :db/id 17592186338075} {:answer/id #uuid "f80e89dd-c652-5265-923f-af822d38aa7f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ and $Y$ only" :db/id 17592186338206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fe7a17e-699b-5815-9a3e-8c195290665d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ only" :db/id 17592186336634} :answer-field/id #uuid "975bdda1-4123-5df1-96af-d7afaa2e70d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f09c20fa-fbe3-5a9f-8218-d2a4dd2af13c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71938" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6 \\\\ 10 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $X$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $Y$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(Y)=6\\cdot 6-7\\cdot 5=1.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(Y)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $Y$ is invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $Z$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(Z)=(-2)\\cdot 5-10\\cdot 1=-20.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(Z)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $Z$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $X$ does not have an inverse."}] [{:db/id 17592186339061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8f012d0-444a-5035-8a72-a053de312d63" :knowledge-point/title "Changing the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2010236d-47fb-566d-af0c-b027bf90808c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186336331} {:answer/id #uuid "3cad5ca4-81de-5679-b6a5-afb792473624" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186336604} {:answer/id #uuid "60293a1e-e9b9-5afd-9f24-67ec9dc41d8b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186336902} {:answer/id #uuid "b561e370-ef46-5cc5-bdd6-66e8842c1d9d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186337647} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b347c1-0e4e-5f8a-b185-d8391cccc11f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186337993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b561e370-ef46-5cc5-bdd6-66e8842c1d9d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186337647} :answer-field/id #uuid "166535c1-1bd7-5b6b-be78-91bf0ba1824c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2073d8ee-8ecd-594e-9fd2-e836c959a0b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72130" :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le 1}.$\n\n- ${∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$\n- ${∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}A={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that:\n\n- the limits for the variable $x$ are from $1$ to $2$, and\n\n- the limits for the variable $y$ are from $\\frac{1}{2}$ to $1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the region of integration is rectangular, we can simply swap the order of integration. This gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186339067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8f012d0-444a-5035-8a72-a053de312d63" :knowledge-point/title "Changing the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e17575-0ade-5823-9fa0-2fc5cba651ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186336564} {:answer/id #uuid "3c61013d-7f45-566e-9c1f-dc853c17ca02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186336602} {:answer/id #uuid "4c80e22b-9d6e-5774-90f6-d056de93d371" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186336744} {:answer/id #uuid "890f0155-82db-50f6-b377-11c22d644867" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186337268} {:answer/id #uuid "a966105f-7c64-5390-a6c1-44e721cc4739" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c80e22b-9d6e-5774-90f6-d056de93d371" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186336744} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2d07712-dafc-5310-b4ad-bbf99bc2af70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4009c26-3316-54ec-b0bf-1d56ecb8b0b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-72175" :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le y\\le 2}.$\n\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that:  \n\n- the limits for the variable $x$ are from $0$ to $\\sqrt{2}$, and\n\n- the limits for the variable $y$ are from $-\\sqrt{3}$ to $2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the region of integration is rectangular, we can simply swap the order of integration. This gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186339069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17be251d-7119-505a-822a-7b4f4a540bc7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186336254} {:answer/id #uuid "28248254-c521-555f-a8eb-3698fd411628" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186336409} {:answer/id #uuid "54358dc3-968d-51c2-b7a6-0a9e65676557" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186336809} {:answer/id #uuid "559f9ad0-2178-5631-a30c-df72edf7e3d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186336818} {:answer/id #uuid "55dfee5e-aee0-54ba-a2ef-8bb2f5c930f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186336821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17be251d-7119-505a-822a-7b4f4a540bc7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186336254} :answer-field/id #uuid "acf0a001-ed03-592c-9465-79a8c55ab0a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bcd8034-22f3-5298-b8d3-b69cdcbc84fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72185" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region inside the circumference ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\, & ⟹\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/09/0960063edcce8ccd55dc2f77147c4a4982645c180e8b998d7e520c65131ea045.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186339070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d5dfdf6-e50a-544c-a082-757d6a3cc45e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186336750} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3a5eba-d4b1-5a73-b36e-ccfbf5537a92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186337017} {:answer/id #uuid "71d9509f-880d-594a-ad0c-8f134a4a17bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186337050} {:answer/id #uuid "87c90cf0-1d0a-5ecb-9f7d-4e9022a09814" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186337256} {:answer/id #uuid "fc897d08-7b5c-5946-a0d6-80162b7a9c92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186338252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87c90cf0-1d0a-5ecb-9f7d-4e9022a09814" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186337256} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dc16394-01fd-51be-9be9-07cf0cfe3cbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f581ab20-43e5-59a8-ac58-914378a7361e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72186" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ and the line $y=2-|x|?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/6f/6f35161e7efbd53c9ef558ffe88d8e046771128965a9fdd02143ae37a9b130ea.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,2-|x|\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- The horizontal line $y=1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/76/769a521cd6b05ecf05052da382e40295b26be66fe56e4eff7c6940c9032f2f0b.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186339071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b81e3f-060b-5845-a303-b88224da1581" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186336078} {:answer/id #uuid "46f070d3-ed05-5034-af82-a9adf57c60b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186336690} {:answer/id #uuid "4deab650-a626-55f2-b9af-f7b4ccb3ee34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186336755} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1230df-1cda-5e95-8f8f-00f39e16c196" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186336882} {:answer/id #uuid "cf98decd-9264-56e3-850f-4cfdc80409db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186337849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46f070d3-ed05-5034-af82-a9adf57c60b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186336690} :answer-field/id #uuid "08cff232-9d15-5600-89b1-e7cd0fac0002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b03842bf-e94f-5526-903d-de5a2a3bccca" :question/math-academy-id "q-72187" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circles ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/28/28290c5530efd27a979f4284481ca7e0384342d7d426dde0ab36894a0d77a166.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.5$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/30/3007cffb725faee44d2a06ad077eec630791b1a94c373a323f62d0d721c87207.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.5$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/38/383f88668435556d3a856cf852caf548b1f1bf3a90d50f9d85d7968188984804.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186339126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129114 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d390841-351e-54f5-ad19-90b6ebae5799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45d99b9d-5704-58cf-919d-6915dcae3299" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186336680} {:answer/id #uuid "52468b8e-d113-5efc-a093-9eec57bc9b1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186336789} {:answer/id #uuid "77b4fe93-508f-553a-8b98-740710c54788" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186337110} {:answer/id #uuid "dfcedb6f-7df8-5c52-95d7-61e9b09424a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186337986} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe12b6b-39a0-5ff6-a668-5408621fc8b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186338243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77b4fe93-508f-553a-8b98-740710c54788" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186337110} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4379a0-d5ee-50fe-b962-654c0a9cb62f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ddf8ccb-f483-5bbe-8662-ef5250ea89a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-72489" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry ${c}_{32}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ and $C$ are **not** in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id 17592186339127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129114 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d390841-351e-54f5-ad19-90b6ebae5799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022a93a6-705d-525b-a6ed-eb10665cd446" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186336051} {:answer/id #uuid "252169cb-b78a-5e0c-a6be-164c180eae59" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186336381} {:answer/id #uuid "2806d007-25ff-5cf7-bbf1-c3e504570ea6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186336405} {:answer/id #uuid "380294b6-401a-5a3f-a8b6-b3b07de91291" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186336553} {:answer/id #uuid "7f80b45e-e58f-5638-a324-065ed3129df7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186337188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2806d007-25ff-5cf7-bbf1-c3e504570ea6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186336405} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecf9782f-4e02-54dd-b3fd-efc5b9df2009" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "937eaf0d-76e6-56ec-a651-23458856322a" :question/math-academy-id "q-72498" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$  is not in row echelon form since ${a}_{32}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id 17592186341835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129113 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f8be93a-652f-57e0-8d38-bcd230082b57" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186340274} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f562f0-2895-533d-b1a7-b975b9fe7bb5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186340787} {:answer/id #uuid "befdff1d-4580-52ec-be14-2fa18ba303c7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186340831} {:answer/id #uuid "cef2d373-08dc-51b2-a572-5824121784fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186340968} {:answer/id #uuid "f84f8da1-987d-5353-91bb-4a28605cc356" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186341325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8f562f0-2895-533d-b1a7-b975b9fe7bb5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186340787} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbc4fdb1-28ec-53bb-808c-40da78982aca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f7d9000-6b1e-59bb-84e6-86f972afc228" :question/math-academy-id "q-72570" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 9 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 11 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 6 & 6 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the bottom of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column to the right of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 9 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$  is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 11 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 6 & 6 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ and $C$ are in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id 17592186341837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129113 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ac7ba1b-b723-5280-92ad-4c9f642b3f93" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186339954} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6f0a64-f7aa-5222-8fb4-a8d2f3e74b11" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186340248} {:answer/id #uuid "a43eda66-5103-58ad-a759-656e383e2463" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186340618} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9daa56-0bd1-51f3-ba11-66f6510ea1da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186340814} {:answer/id #uuid "e3046a5c-b97e-5253-ad4c-9e218fcb631f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186341137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c6f0a64-f7aa-5222-8fb4-a8d2f3e74b11" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186340248} :answer-field/id #uuid "25f51ed4-31e6-5d99-abf8-f209f3fd72ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c409d434-4c7f-59fa-9d55-4c82bc621cc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-72574" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the bottom of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column to the right of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied). \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since ${c}_{21}\\ne 0$ (condition  II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id 17592186341838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129113 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05490973-f9e5-5e64-ba65-4e2fea411064" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186339167} {:answer/id #uuid "326890e9-4656-517c-8e21-551f43e4b4b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186339572} {:answer/id #uuid "3454ef84-206a-5201-9156-5622a49f30dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186339593} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa11633-8afd-5d03-ab16-bb473b613354" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186339980} {:answer/id #uuid "e6714207-3005-5ade-af54-df6eaa9a8613" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186341167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05490973-f9e5-5e64-ba65-4e2fea411064" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186339167} :answer-field/id #uuid "63681ebc-7c8e-5281-86d6-7870a0940d7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d3c090e-cc4f-51d5-a663-f597f7113489" :question/math-academy-id "q-72576" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the bottom of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column to the right of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since ${c}_{21}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id 17592186341908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33ab5c9a-741e-507f-8a99-07a9f7aa451c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186339588} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf55990-23dc-5693-8dde-0dd252a432b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186339663} {:answer/id #uuid "48fe273f-13eb-5f78-8272-5b9b3a2db061" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186339782} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2895ac-425c-5001-bb2a-284e8601acaf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186339796} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2ab1df-0cb6-5919-a2be-e9cca71bcc58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186341194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a2895ac-425c-5001-bb2a-284e8601acaf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186339796} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf84d3cb-0dfa-513a-bcdd-3183ad8dee6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79464211-37d1-5cac-b164-284bb9d9308a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73105" :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad3fb8a0cce2a6dac706d7876aca0a2339fe49c05cb7aa6ae54de86735ceafab.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nI. The function is left-continuous at $x=3$\n\nII. The function is right-continuous at $x=3$\n\nIII. The function is continuous at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=3,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 1=f(3)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=3,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=1=f(3)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=3,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186341909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44870b19-7de8-568b-9bca-2faf7aacb656" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186339734} {:answer/id #uuid "6d08a5ce-252a-5950-9158-72ef68730a92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186340088} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f89edf-e35a-52a7-8ca1-d55f110862dd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186340749} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ddd360-ea1a-5bee-8ba6-6e9b7129a9e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186340908} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c70b93-5d4c-58f2-989d-44cba9e4cf22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186341192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44870b19-7de8-568b-9bca-2faf7aacb656" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186339734} :answer-field/id #uuid "dce5165d-a261-5af6-ae8f-5e3cff741926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f47bb47-6168-5cb8-92db-b7a3fc493275" :question/math-academy-id "q-73106" :question/problem "![](images/89/89dca4b059ccbd1fe93d000acabcbd33b5fc10389de48ef3cbae65cb4455f29e.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 3=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=1\\ne 3=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186341927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115380 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195bda69-5d84-5c1f-93f1-061ff6e7af90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186339351} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3d3665-b6a6-5975-9783-9af879c2d659" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186339383} {:answer/id #uuid "582777b8-345a-5528-a5bf-6cb7f361b239" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186339931} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7998fd-d3d9-5720-bee0-6d1d6f6064f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186340701} {:answer/id #uuid "f1bb4be9-df0f-5398-8fd0-b36df3ca0d06" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186341257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac7998fd-d3d9-5720-bee0-6d1d6f6064f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186340701} :answer-field/id #uuid "a43e6b64-3905-50fe-84a0-c65639ebeb11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9eb34e90-018e-520a-8053-32b72e25bff0" :question/math-academy-id "q-73249" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec8101640f2bd84506d1c47b92f088675080d87eafff4000a4f3e0d14ccebb55.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=-2$\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of $f(x)$ shows a cusp at this point and so it is not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186341928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115380 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3115a701-908d-5194-89b5-3934f2aee943" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186339559} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef63771-16e0-5c66-87bb-9773a47f39ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186339696} {:answer/id #uuid "4084d34d-cdd6-5521-90d7-5d09fec010be" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186339705} {:answer/id #uuid "5f7cee9c-67f1-509a-a047-56e0f6f1ef9b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I o