"}] [{:db/id 17592186341975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9c9d7d-cafe-5ca3-aeda-6ab3a8844c04" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186339402} {:answer/id #uuid "466f898a-a17d-5ef2-9df7-de21d4467270" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186339753} {:answer/id #uuid "53dd7fea-deec-5659-abd2-913b366a2e31" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186339904} {:answer/id #uuid "71573f37-88c6-5316-841b-9e7cbf18b8f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186340135} {:answer/id #uuid "9acfcc52-3763-5eb4-9f6b-ab6d62822c30" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186340525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53dd7fea-deec-5659-abd2-913b366a2e31" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186339904} :answer-field/id #uuid "8811fa51-bfe6-5509-90af-f31705a35d36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbdd6305-e297-593d-89f4-fb7ada8ade14" :question/math-academy-id "q-73455" :question/problem "![](images/de/de39663db59e42565c7d69d8a44a4cb95f14af0f4946b77cfc42eac02f7d2786.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\nI. $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$\n\nII. $[-\\pi ,0)$\n\nIII. $[1,\\pi ]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval  $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is closed,but the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-\\pi ,0)$ is not closed, so, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,\\pi ]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186341981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2667e8a9-ad2e-5060-ac38-bc2c70de2517" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186339462} {:answer/id #uuid "625380bb-0130-5ea5-a938-fd4c6911619a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186340005} {:answer/id #uuid "6e925fcb-aeca-55f4-a2d0-bdc171bc4053" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186340107} {:answer/id #uuid "91fa11e7-ee32-5b9c-b3e7-3b327d3b922a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186340438} {:answer/id #uuid "ff71884c-52b4-575e-b1f2-db8af6ee5dd9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186341380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff71884c-52b4-575e-b1f2-db8af6ee5dd9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186341380} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e8ea3ae-c1eb-57e3-9b69-648b0d966d96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b5c08f3-5fbe-50f0-8681-6cf3ebc354c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73463" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4de4aa4956b0b70e6e08c9f0188944469270ab19501194ce897e2af9411a0d98.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[-\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n- $[0,\\pi ]$\n- $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval  $[-\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$  is closed, but the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\pi ]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$  is not closed, so, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186341984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c85ec74-7261-5a84-b268-2cb5156ec3e8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186339237} {:answer/id #uuid "324afc9c-5fc7-5f9