 is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186341988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a506d32-c549-5247-82e3-f237fa06872d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186339215} {:answer/id #uuid "22362652-f0bc-5e3c-afa7-25ce82d78661" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186339421} {:answer/id #uuid "327c0e31-b1ed-5548-81bc-18ff4bca8bd4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186339573} {:answer/id #uuid "87824f92-9edf-52ca-9f8c-b3eeb64b58cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186340341} {:answer/id #uuid "a19759de-fa06-5d86-925a-320a7299775b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186340594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a19759de-fa06-5d86-925a-320a7299775b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186340594} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8dae27-98ed-5b9c-b45d-d7007b99d819" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96d14ec6-ae06-5360-8ddd-d401dbb48508" :question/math-academy-id "q-73487" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{x}{{2}^{x}-1}?$    \n\nI. $[0,1]$\n\nII. $[0,1)$\n\nIII. $[-2,-1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=0$ (it makes zero the denominator).   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval  $[0,1)$   is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-2,-1]$    is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-2,-1].$   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186341990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cfa7a4-d6c1-5351-99b2-6635044d85f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186339474} {:answer/id #uuid "65459a76-323b-5a88-8a2f-2e108e43ea11" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186340024} {:answer/id #uuid "88971863-2310-5e3f-ac5d-6e4e1adfb7df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186340348} {:answer/id #uuid "91912873-03a1-524d-9ad0-7c617923af84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186340433} {:answer/id #uuid "be62f3bd-2eeb-5b57-889c-ca65dbe099b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186340825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91912873-03a1-524d-9ad0-7c617923af84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186340433} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcb8b636-417c-59ba-b9cc-599917ded547" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b9b884e-eb8a-59c7-aa31-49f0025fb47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73495" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ -1, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-1,1]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$\n\nIII. $[-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}({2}^{-x})={2}^{0}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$ In this interval, $f(x)={2}^{-x}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[-1,0)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186341991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ceb0ce8-da1c-5b99-9182-d772be7cec72" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186339520} {:answer/id #uuid "7896c996-e406-5136-a44c-25645e86bbc5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186340203} {:answer/id #uuid "9a19840a-be8c-5757-bad3-4daa8391bbb3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186340517} {:answer/id #uuid "a718fb5b-1dd8-5664-a8f8-13023ada0bfa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186340646} {:answer/id #uuid "f499afb4-f0c7-5505-912f-71dee8dd2498" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186341285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a19840a-be8c-5757-bad3-4daa8391bbb3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186340517} :answer-field/id #uuid "141d901b-70e2-5398-8913-6c50c5c4a073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d1eaad6e-884d-57bd-a4ae-a690d3d6abe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-73499" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1), & x>1 \\\\ x-1, & x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[0,2]$\n\nII. $[-1,0]$\n\nIII. $[-1,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,0].$ In this interval, $f(x)=x-1$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,0].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, in the interval $[-1,2]$, we also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,2]$ and therefore  the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186341993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "235e2186-c516-5a12-99e1-855472aad9f5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186339433} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1ad79a-9054-5adb-8e27-82c74fb0eeee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186340265} {:answer/id #uuid "d65d9629-6375-5d3b-9bf7-be4b8ce2e02b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186341027} {:answer/id #uuid "de97e50a-3ac0-5909-8788-42a53cdaa913" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186341105} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a1b048-a2c7-5d2d-99eb-e4c76eea7395" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186341157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e1ad79a-9054-5adb-8e27-82c74fb0eeee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186340265} :answer-field/id #uuid "3883c8f8-56cb-5ac2-9602-b5a9a360bba9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a761269-f6ea-50eb-8e45-58983c3d9692" :question/math-academy-id "q-73503" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $(0,3]$\n- $[-2,-1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186341994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f8ca629-0927-538d-ab6c-ffef86ed4c3f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id 17592186339401} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbeae54-1a8e-537b-bcc8-b644943612ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186339669} {:answer/id #uuid "4680f954-f4f8-5258-ae55-a7412a19432c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186339754} {:answer/id #uuid "bfda1598-8520-5bca-930a-7ba3397c4c03" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186340839} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb6ae8f-aab7-5bdc-8f7c-dc14885c5a5b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186341353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cbeae54-1a8e-537b-bcc8-b644943612ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186339669} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c36c391-b98e-5750-8019-3f694dde0b5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dcdd6cca-dc15-5e0c-a55e-4d73a59866ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-73504" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-1, & x<1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡(x-1), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,2]$\n- $(1,3]$\n- $[2,\\pi ]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-1,2].$  We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2{x}^{3}-1)=2(1)^{3}-1=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}{sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)={sin}^{2}⁡(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[2,\\pi ].$ In this interval, $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,\\pi ].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186342003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085a372e-3d44-5218-8ffc-7245e73f20e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186339197} {:answer/id #uuid "27bfa14d-cc0f-5a6b-9127-444c2c3cbe94" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186339472} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff1f004-8a24-53f6-8512-8daed863b9af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186339982} {:answer/id #uuid "807a16d0-c898-5c5a-aa54-af34b1d71126" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186340280} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef8375b-1f86-5526-a7e2-474095047082" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186340564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "807a16d0-c898-5c5a-aa54-af34b1d71126" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186340280} :answer-field/id #uuid "1167d122-71e9-5a13-8edb-8b2e825eb1e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8abc4b9f-caf8-5c94-91da-62ad0ae61b3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73531" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-2,2]$\n\nII. $[-3,1)$\n\nIII. $[1.5,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-2,2].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-3,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\n- Finally consider  $[1.5,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is not in the interval  $[1.5,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[1.5,2]$, and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186342012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f079a8-3eef-5e0c-b508-56b0a28e810a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186339187} {:answer/id #uuid "33e2246e-0cb8-5b3d-a663-6ded80537d31" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186339590} {:answer/id #uuid "36e9f6d3-ade4-54f2-83b8-98b440f7e2f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186339621} {:answer/id #uuid "9be8a799-d92f-523e-80f4-9f217caeda37" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186340530} {:answer/id #uuid "a94a7551-868d-5a9d-8d58-61cf61d367d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186340670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9be8a799-d92f-523e-80f4-9f217caeda37" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186340530} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c233766-89ba-51e9-91fa-b4ab683f8678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "451e02c3-10e2-5101-a83f-538a8be6b03d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73549" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the ellipses $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/62/627e7e102a0f16cb0c4946e42b48ad122299d729c5bc419293e258a3f2e1701e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.25$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/d7/d7aa48c887d6fb1c3d0420867b7e74010fa93e16d7e24317e2cf214764e56113.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.5$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/ec/ec632fa5f86d8d9b34b9ccf661a3219e965f289c28bf69d7d80eeeb46093e7ae.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186342187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db4180e-9e56-5615-a261-a06b057afbc7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186339254} {:answer/id #uuid "44382309-a210-5068-b421-83edd371f540" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186339728} {:answer/id #uuid "705bf110-f45f-5748-8977-a6e4eaab55d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186340124} {:answer/id #uuid "a661de8b-e986-5edb-ae2a-54d4fbff884d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186340638} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1d59d6-b1c0-5ab1-b672-2772922e372b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186341209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "705bf110-f45f-5748-8977-a6e4eaab55d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186340124} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4646991-5d1f-5339-9e5a-2aa1bae21d22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e93e600-4a01-5ada-842d-9b175b57ee56" :question/math-academy-id "q-74217" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:1-x-y=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:2x+3y-z=0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $1-x-y=0$ since $1-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is in $B$, but $2u=⟨2,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since ${2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}≰1.$ So, $B$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $2x+3y-z=0$ since $2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186342188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43ab7da0-2f07-50d1-831b-c157d66b1de8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186339721} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffb62c9-06bc-54ed-a237-28e9635fc6fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186340121} {:answer/id #uuid "72703321-1679-5b08-aab6-32e985e25b41" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186340146} {:answer/id #uuid "74517a98-ee70-5879-af22-ee6f1cbffcda" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186340165} {:answer/id #uuid "ef265337-e4c5-5117-bd72-923b375019cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186341238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74517a98-ee70-5879-af22-ee6f1cbffcda" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186340165} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa75a06c-11fb-5ef6-a1f6-3f25d8c6ebf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5037faf6-6f8a-5ea2-800f-2c136a3bb59e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74218" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:xyz=0}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:y=-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ since ${0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under vector addition. Indeed, both $a=⟨1,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,1⟩$ belong to $B$ but \n\n$$\na+b=⟨1,0,1⟩+⟨0,1,1⟩=⟨1,1,2⟩\n$$\n\n \ndoes not satisfy the condition $xyz=0$ since $1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=-1$ since $0\\ne -1.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186342191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3171ea47-31ac-55ef-8e65-7c8cc13d17db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186339563} {:answer/id #uuid "4780f3c7-7445-5e20-b679-8d09700f5b1a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186339761} {:answer/id #uuid "69308677-f0aa-523b-9be3-7371c6dc3d65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186340063} {:answer/id #uuid "7458caf1-0896-52f4-b93d-16c6705c4441" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186340166} {:answer/id #uuid "962e504c-ea04-57e3-acb7-34e1af23490d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186340484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69308677-f0aa-523b-9be3-7371c6dc3d65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186340063} :answer-field/id #uuid "df4036f4-0b20-53ba-89f9-e6eee6e466b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb81e4a8-c5a7-59da-b168-b278b747b24a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74255" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}<\\frac{1}{2}\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5x-y-z=0\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5-x-z=0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨\\frac{1}{4},0,0⟩$ is in $A$, since\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nbut, $4u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}>\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $5x-y-z=0$ since $5\\cdot 0-0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $5-x-z=0$ since $5-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186345004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74d47c5c-d9b2-5729-b276-f386649b4836" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186343248} {:answer/id #uuid "78689969-fa12-5d79-b86b-75a95aac6c30" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186343278} {:answer/id #uuid "93f165e9-4fa8-5327-af87-0aecc43a6729" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186343532} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d03412-f273-52d2-a7f8-85526134a1c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186343815} {:answer/id #uuid "f7884f74-457b-566e-ac8e-ae3ebcb6b395" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186344417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93f165e9-4fa8-5327-af87-0aecc43a6729" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186343532} :answer-field/id #uuid "196a3494-8261-5fa3-924a-5e54ae02aac6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5fe00a2-ab56-5281-b3c2-6fe8dad2417a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74964" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}\n$$\n\nI. The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n\nII. The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n\nIII. The limit exists and is equal to $2$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x(2x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{2x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot 0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186345029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9ca939-c7a6-5aeb-98c2-585d48ff8c4a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186342604} {:answer/id #uuid "5716cd24-5a2c-5544-92f6-203e6a676437" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186342963} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2c0063-1ae4-55ca-87d1-b65a1e62305f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id 17592186343173} {:answer/id #uuid "731035c9-6220-52ed-886c-05ae731b34ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186343235} {:answer/id #uuid "cf56f8d6-4634-5189-80bd-37357cd208d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  and III only" :db/id 17592186344062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b9ca939-c7a6-5aeb-98c2-585d48ff8c4a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186342604} :answer-field/id #uuid "51ec1b91-00af-5481-ab92-af724f5e618b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89a636cd-a216-5d73-bd3f-7f9cbe4badf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-75116" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $C?$\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $C,$ we write $C=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form  because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186345030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ada63ec-fa1d-5ff7-b669-92737dcdbd74" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186342330} {:answer/id #uuid "1752c8df-b894-5390-b173-aa620c63a4ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186342443} {:answer/id #uuid "409dfa57-961d-5f83-aa15-10d15babbe22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186342771} {:answer/id #uuid "467b7351-7ecb-539b-ba6e-6ea1995a8925" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186342818} {:answer/id #uuid "98410b31-6e91-581a-a43b-5311f6c98db0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186343573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "467b7351-7ecb-539b-ba6e-6ea1995a8925" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186342818} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c138955-72ce-5ce7-a05a-28705f2a9e68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0305a43d-bf81-5ecb-8a77-49a30ade0147" :question/math-academy-id "q-75117" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:  \n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$  on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal. \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186345033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454ea782-196e-50f9-b1e2-00d2d1bb0539" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186342808} {:answer/id #uuid "4f08aad3-17c0-5aea-a1e3-8bc3cab0aea2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186342888} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2b2691-bd26-5b75-bf4a-0ab5f6a47c36" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186342891} {:answer/id #uuid "567c9971-93d6-5157-ae62-fc0890790260" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186342958} {:answer/id #uuid "9b54534f-b827-5c6c-a649-e71657a1ca05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186343609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "567c9971-93d6-5157-ae62-fc0890790260" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186342958} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc036018-08d5-5220-9a04-6989a4c32339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34143660-ecfa-5585-b65e-76b215171706" :question/math-academy-id "q-75123" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $C?$\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $C,$ we write $C=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry above the main diagonal. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$ on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186345092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f2376a1-da00-5911-b146-c5236752448e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186342890} {:answer/id #uuid "58038b76-6478-52e3-88f8-675c4aa414d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186342974} {:answer/id #uuid "59103f60-0dff-5bf6-af9c-6267f890a47e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186342987} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea948d9-d1d3-5312-9f2b-e7b8089e8034" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id 17592186343638} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7d0947-9c9d-5773-b575-44075078af0d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186343772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea948d9-d1d3-5312-9f2b-e7b8089e8034" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id 17592186343638} :answer-field/id #uuid "f466101d-ee92-54d3-b4e2-a9cca8e95913" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21485ec9-747a-5129-ba2c-794c570827bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-75496" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186345095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dad3104-8a81-5836-a5a9-4e944d9ea89c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186342747} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa425cd-c3c4-550e-9bf6-9b7007b60940" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186343145} {:answer/id #uuid "7230a1e9-8e89-5267-aa7f-a81a5c6cccc2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186343226} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1e7dd8-43cf-517f-b457-c549b0d0e69e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186343606} {:answer/id #uuid "ae816524-bdcc-57d3-b1f0-57315f7a80b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186343773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7230a1e9-8e89-5267-aa7f-a81a5c6cccc2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186343226} :answer-field/id #uuid "5db32577-42b1-5ff0-9984-873d995d7e37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9438fd33-674b-505b-988d-ed78456eace8" :question/math-academy-id "q-75503" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186345097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f9218e3-c5a3-504e-a8aa-4c7a7a889102" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186343047} {:answer/id #uuid "76c4e5d3-bf62-5530-bdeb-b43a0ad3242b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186343269} {:answer/id #uuid "97298d24-d2e5-5c5a-a83a-d3564f5c03e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186343563} {:answer/id #uuid "c98ee987-6e30-5c28-bec6-af41c5351ce3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186344008} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bb85dd-13a5-594c-b825-a8dcdeb42be7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186344427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f9218e3-c5a3-504e-a8aa-4c7a7a889102" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186343047} :answer-field/id #uuid "18674c8a-58e7-537d-943e-33b31c03bca5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8b28abc-ffbd-5f2b-b51a-6be08eb05498" :question/math-academy-id "q-75508" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186345106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18956b73-de0f-5274-ad02-5066c0d2657b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186342456} {:answer/id #uuid "2d821b77-91b8-542f-b59c-7b5c3ebcd4ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186342620} {:answer/id #uuid "4b54bdc7-fb8f-5924-947c-0a287faf9bbe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186342858} {:answer/id #uuid "6348b2b2-8ae7-537e-909c-bb22df4127b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186343078} {:answer/id #uuid "8bce61b5-2b6e-5b57-a1a3-1eef97b998b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186343459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bce61b5-2b6e-5b57-a1a3-1eef97b998b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186343459} :answer-field/id #uuid "40b7d972-25a9-5d09-b9bb-575a2e8ee618" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd25b025-461f-556b-97f3-6071c19d59d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-75595" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},\\,$ ${R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1},\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186345107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59d338f8-b49b-5145-a7ef-1ae582d559cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186342991} {:answer/id #uuid "92dcfa7a-f257-5a95-9893-29619578a1b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186343522} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5a63f9-bd01-5a20-a2a4-c0a864ce4a0e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186344291} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b90c65-9660-55b2-9d45-57ee11b4570d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186344351} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f172f0-b5cc-56f2-9005-34aef17200e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186344385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea5a63f9-bd01-5a20-a2a4-c0a864ce4a0e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186344291} :answer-field/id #uuid "d16727b3-99b0-547b-8c45-25b008d782b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a52696c-f90c-56a7-94f5-6062f59efbae" :question/math-academy-id "q-75599" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},\\,$ ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186345112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c30cee0-847d-5f40-ae43-134afe788817" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186343010} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4716f8-1d1a-5bf6-a69d-02354cf35afa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186343030} {:answer/id #uuid "6557bb3a-d669-5757-bc6e-f43fb74887f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186343098} {:answer/id #uuid "7375a3b9-bff4-58a6-82e9-4245781699f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186343237} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf19162-0472-5764-b148-da9e42eaf104" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186343910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e4716f8-1d1a-5bf6-a69d-02354cf35afa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186343030} :answer-field/id #uuid "736c3de5-9a79-5920-82d2-3cbc3976ab40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f25df3d-4b0c-5b67-901d-8d878d2769ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-75655" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186345113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3da42d2a-fc8c-5560-a05e-8ebb08251ab7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186342746} {:answer/id #uuid "428056c7-8c15-5083-b495-0a5d2db7568d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186342788} {:answer/id #uuid "b05bd9d9-23cf-5926-8e64-3aa606f0b446" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186343789} {:answer/id #uuid "f974dbb8-fa3b-57e5-b4a2-cbf533523461" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186344432} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6841f4-2ac2-55ac-8a6c-00752966eb25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186344488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b05bd9d9-23cf-5926-8e64-3aa606f0b446" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186343789} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c97eb9a-a992-5953-a80c-a71608c9beca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d48ab70-ab46-5142-a432-382f1feb7bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75659" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false."}] [{:db/id 17592186345114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11972855-80a9-5cac-9e70-8a4fcbdccf24" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186342393} {:answer/id #uuid "2bfeba34-6da2-5961-afdc-9d9564e3b098" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186342608} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5b646e-db57-5a8d-9e82-9eb0862b3d92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186343756} {:answer/id #uuid "acba01cf-19c6-57a0-8cf2-d050702ea3a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186343758} {:answer/id #uuid "f9874691-41fb-52a1-b9c0-d3eb10d101ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186344433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5b646e-db57-5a8d-9e82-9eb0862b3d92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186343756} :answer-field/id #uuid "d156fe28-1c7f-5127-9119-2df01429f973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d177800-f6a6-59b3-bf6e-5adc772e358a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75660" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+13{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 13 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(0)+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186345121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1299352e-d2ef-5e23-a6a9-e3484a823eab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186342402} {:answer/id #uuid "6945182e-95b7-5e01-ab43-e486bfa69c53" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186343137} {:answer/id #uuid "cab42dea-25c4-565b-904b-b7e77ae2c127" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186344021} {:answer/id #uuid "e402b43c-fdcf-51ca-9fd7-ca29f619233d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186344240} {:answer/id #uuid "ea45b5bf-09d1-5fd5-a6ce-b44ab9b5150c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186344290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cab42dea-25c4-565b-904b-b7e77ae2c127" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186344021} :answer-field/id #uuid "200fb1e1-dd6d-5c55-afee-57f8fc7300f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a5c031b-68ff-5d30-80f0-79dec42f9067" :question/math-academy-id "q-75729" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is false. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=2v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=2{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $2$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{2}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{1}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186345193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186078487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5900b2ad-c394-58a3-8661-86744ec49f7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186342986} {:answer/id #uuid "71ec907a-fb23-50c4-8b1c-c42f54b462fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186343223} {:answer/id #uuid "80e38216-9a2e-56cf-9bab-79d5b46a98fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186343349} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ee4e70-3d9b-51db-b23d-bc1a59a3bace" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186343800} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ea78ef-a8ff-5431-b650-ef064402d8dd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186344358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5900b2ad-c394-58a3-8661-86744ec49f7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186342986} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf7e708-707f-5e09-821c-37a874528e08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca01d454-a476-5d37-9896-aa972462aa23" :question/math-academy-id "q-76208" :question/problem "![](images/ab/abfd15c8ed134a30ad719f6a857268427511e7f1f15ecfe0d27e5026b5401a83.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)$\n- $x\\in [-2,3)$\n- $x\\in [3,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on the open interval $(-\\infty ,-2).$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-2,3).$ It is continuous on $(-2,3)$ and also right continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[3,\\infty ).$ It is continuous on $(3,\\infty )$ and also right continuous at $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186345195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186078487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26764b32-2c60-587f-b8c5-37fa19d94968" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" :db/id 17592186342564} {:answer/id #uuid "2efc0ccc-6f9e-51e2-9559-c2a8b34966df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186342632} {:answer/id #uuid "a45ef78d-716e-51d4-a3ff-b11c8025f4f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186343688} {:answer/id #uuid "b3675348-452e-5b76-9643-725033643714" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186343813} {:answer/id #uuid "c24a3e56-f567-5ca6-9f4a-165390ecbf5b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186343946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2efc0ccc-6f9e-51e2-9559-c2a8b34966df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186342632} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aaefafc-ce87-53d2-9580-dce1180a6863" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d16a77c7-8df1-5b6e-bc46-f6c511c4b268" :question/math-academy-id "q-76210" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e787dda0cccdafaa6a93176daea5f954b1cd1da0beaff410a6563536459208e.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]$\n- $x\\in [-2,0]$\n- $x\\in [-1,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and also left continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-2,0]$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-1,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(-1,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186345197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186078487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1075d718-40b0-5742-b4f2-dbdbdf2e06b5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186342380} {:answer/id #uuid "15769205-ba90-5639-abae-d9422352b485" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" :db/id 17592186342429} {:answer/id #uuid "39cc683a-00e8-585f-91ea-14d5c5a1dfa0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186342715} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5540c3-8c22-5a59-9e92-56b97da12a15" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186343616} {:answer/id #uuid "d40e7fd5-7f34-56f0-93bc-e8e4ed2a8dc1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186344099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1075d718-40b0-5742-b4f2-dbdbdf2e06b5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186342380} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f8fe9a0-755d-5543-a40d-443c23f5ea83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8275053-44d7-522b-8662-3358231e9c18" :question/math-academy-id "q-76218" :question/problem "![](images/18/186ac941080da34b7c149534a2e255852241b28855c0ef15db2beee2d692007a.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,3]$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,5)$\n- $x\\in (4,6)$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,3].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,3),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,5)$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=3.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $(4,6).$ It is continuous on $[3,\\infty )$ and this contains the interval $(4,6).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186345292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186096691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f5e655-d76e-5618-a17f-2483870f80ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186342404} {:answer/id #uuid "4a960852-e36e-5de9-accc-56acd04910ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186342852} {:answer/id #uuid "6234df6c-15c7-5877-b0fb-75883f2806a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186343071} {:answer/id #uuid "df4e9b9c-c909-5afd-bf7b-e6148fe13a5a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186344196} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e83d4b-c078-5faf-ac16-d2afb50445ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186344411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6e83d4b-c078-5faf-ac16-d2afb50445ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186344411} :answer-field/id #uuid "467ab3d5-e2b3-5afa-a2e2-f13c2a37966a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43864da8-ac06-5dee-a580-c31c2739a8e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-77389" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e17584d96cb8d83ccb1abfbd16c4117f58639b7cd3462ee50ad3f31e4eabe279.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCDEF$ shown above, which of the following equalities are true?\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}-\\overset{\\to }{DC}=\\overset{\\to }{BD}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE}-\\overset{\\to }{FE}=\\overset{\\to }{BF}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{CD}-\\overset{\\to }{ED}-\\overset{\\to }{FE}=\\overset{\\to }{FC}$" :question/worked-solution "- Equality I is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BC}-\\overset{\\to }{DC} & =\\overset{\\to }{BC}+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equality II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE}-\\overset{\\to }{FE} & =\\overset{\\to }{BA}+(\\overset{\\to }{AE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF}) \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BF}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equality III is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD}-\\overset{\\to }{ED}-\\overset{\\to }{FE} & =(\\overset{\\to }{CD}+\\overset{\\to }{DE})+\\overset{\\to }{EF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{CE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{CF} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{FC}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186345293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f96b37-25e2-57e5-a9bd-ce85f77ef622" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186342559} {:answer/id #uuid "6810a70d-fc25-5dc9-9176-1e77c5c64ef1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186343123} {:answer/id #uuid "9b153830-307e-510b-95ec-885456562d1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186343605} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f2fb38-44b4-51a3-ae94-6982dfb498bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186344152} {:answer/id #uuid "f051a762-fe84-5e5b-a57f-76839faef4a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186344349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9f2fb38-44b4-51a3-ae94-6982dfb498bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186344152} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cc90745-e978-5189-b064-c8cfba27da07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dfe3c702-6424-5bb5-8a56-52b5a6e22724" :question/math-academy-id "q-77400" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,-2⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=2 \\\\ 2x+y=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\n- $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|=-6$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|=-12$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|=3$" :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|}{3} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186345294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20bb0734-b113-5738-811e-5ed128d214ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186342507} {:answer/id #uuid "27742a41-54e2-59b5-b786-db849377783d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186342573} {:answer/id #uuid "44122616-845c-5061-9e80-69872573e8f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186342803} {:answer/id #uuid "8d84791f-2e4f-5e1a-8d1d-454662569e37" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186343476} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e5462b-343b-5798-95b7-33b70f0f69a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186343841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d84791f-2e4f-5e1a-8d1d-454662569e37" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186343476} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c1e02f2-9665-5243-aedc-17c100ebdba4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a75a54e5-59f0-5536-9005-ac12974e1a69" :question/math-academy-id "q-77401" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,1⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=k \\\\ x+4y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\n- $|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=5$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-7$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=13$" :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =12+1 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|}{13} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186345308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42e2d978-2c80-5bfd-8184-7620c86b4d9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186342796} {:answer/id #uuid "87caf754-01fe-5135-8e0f-3937db98fe01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186343413} {:answer/id #uuid "aaac6d10-cd8e-529d-b81e-8bac65acd12c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186343740} {:answer/id #uuid "df109891-92e9-56d1-95e6-b29b4c4c96cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186344195} {:answer/id #uuid "f39fd878-ef99-5884-b619-883485188f80" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186344383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaac6d10-cd8e-529d-b81e-8bac65acd12c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186343740} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae19f3c-dbd3-5456-af19-7dc1239a420f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49749c9e-4840-5ef3-a87f-c1ce7810b100" :question/math-academy-id "q-77503" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=2$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $-2\\text{det}({A}^{T})-3\\text{det}(I)=7$\n- $4\\text{det}({A}^{T})=2$\n- $\\text{det}({A}^{T})-\\text{det}(I)=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have,\n\n$$\n-2\\text{det}({A}^{T})-3\\text{det}(I)=-2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 1=-7\\ne 7.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n4\\text{det}({A}^{T})=4\\cdot 2=8\\ne 2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})-\\text{det}(I)=2-1=1\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186345345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13c9b65e-8aab-58e4-9ff8-f146c4b1dfb0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186342412} {:answer/id #uuid "bc16e6f4-a9a7-5ef2-a3e8-5f2cc8fd2073" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186343890} {:answer/id #uuid "dcbb9443-92d4-5c38-8324-856eb59d6047" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186344177} {:answer/id #uuid "e96dfec3-2242-5abf-9843-aa4fbc07fe3c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186344281} {:answer/id #uuid "edf0bc6b-95d8-5574-ae34-66cc789aa7f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186344329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13c9b65e-8aab-58e4-9ff8-f146c4b1dfb0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186342412} :answer-field/id #uuid "35f8c506-59e5-5df7-b6f5-b6b5ef1620ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0acb71b-a2ed-51d8-8bca-93474ee1c192" :question/math-academy-id "q-77864" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]?$\n\n- $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$\n- $g(x)=|{x}^{2}+1|$\n- $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|{x}^{2}+1|$ is equivalent to $p(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ so it is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, the functions I, II, and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$"}] [{:db/id 17592186348109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115300 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68350e0d-84ca-5b50-b05a-1bca3ceea40f" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 3x3 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185619d8-1b91-5d2f-af5e-dee1634d5555" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186345565} {:answer/id #uuid "44f107dd-253f-5b2e-bda0-164d1239fdcf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186345949} {:answer/id #uuid "515c1c66-f6e4-50c0-b52d-198cd74b8a3a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186346047} {:answer/id #uuid "8d916295-fa1a-5ff5-ba9b-5d907aac02a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186346582} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b4ad68-2c01-51fc-89a3-6a26083ddcde" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186347353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3b4ad68-2c01-51fc-89a3-6a26083ddcde" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186347353} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1aaa798-00b3-55fa-9339-75ae07399b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad44d2b4-38e0-5faa-8080-32a8feda902f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78772" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186348154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1ea255-7dc4-54d4-9172-7acf08d05cd0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345453} {:answer/id #uuid "b2253313-0064-565d-aab1-ac081b160a04" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186346906} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c2d995-4b9c-5b32-8ff4-4cd6a59aa059" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186346926} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb08342-c5da-554a-bb78-0962f941c5a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186347141} {:answer/id #uuid "da3e0bea-256a-5720-8bd7-3b470182a9c2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d1ea255-7dc4-54d4-9172-7acf08d05cd0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345453} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0666484-0b92-5c23-994f-2dff9365864f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3b9e269-d2c5-5e09-8e6b-2689345ee964" :question/math-academy-id "q-79039" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+5x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12x+5 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-12 & >0\\,⟹\\,x>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the function is concave up on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $6x-12<0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f70b66-96e9-5aae-93da-647ff3e21f32" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186345413} {:answer/id #uuid "1ccbca84-26a7-531b-bc40-848a39c9d145" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186345601} {:answer/id #uuid "820bdb8d-1b1f-5a23-91ee-e42c0fd7b00c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346488} {:answer/id #uuid "964ca8d1-bc5e-5d1c-a5e6-7d742ff7cbd1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346665} {:answer/id #uuid "b684115e-c70b-5e4f-9a6a-91ed32fef39b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b684115e-c70b-5e4f-9a6a-91ed32fef39b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346942} :answer-field/id #uuid "f84b80f4-3cda-509e-bb73-3c54426e4477" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186348041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d60b7843-0bd3-5e4e-8b85-becdd36b22ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-79047" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2+3x-{e}^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3+{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-x}>0$ for every real value of $x,$ it follows that $-{e}^{-x}<0$ for every real value of $x.$ Therefore, ${f}^{″}(x)$ is never positive, so there are no intervals where $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\nSince ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for every real value of $x,$ the function is concave down on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "406edcd7-dd0d-553f-94d4-59c097c98af4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$G$ only" :db/id 17592186345904} {:answer/id #uuid "465f4a2b-19c0-58df-8ab0-2cc0a8ebc295" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ only" :db/id 17592186345964} {:answer/id #uuid "47dcbc0e-948f-533c-8be5-738050dd7dae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ and $G$ only" :db/id 17592186345967} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f84565-bd95-5131-8e6d-cd3a127bd0b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and $F$ only" :db/id 17592186347089} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ba910b-46fc-5621-8530-c677d52695b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and $G$ only" :db/id 17592186347109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "465f4a2b-19c0-58df-8ab0-2cc0a8ebc295" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ only" :db/id 17592186345964} :answer-field/id #uuid "723ed971-5225-51a2-a32f-826e5f345808" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cd97c58-6193-5fe7-8948-41d6f4bfcd71" :question/math-academy-id "q-7923" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,G=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $D$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(D)=3\\cdot 6-6\\cdot 2=6.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(D)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $D$ is invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $F$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(F)=6\\cdot 6-6\\cdot 6=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(F)=0,$ we conclude that $F$ is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $G$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(G)=(-2)\\cdot 4-4\\cdot 2=-16.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(G)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $G$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $F$ does not have an inverse."}] [{:db/id 17592186348208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "097b8a1b-b44d-5e76-97c8-b4c1a59a0e59" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186345426} {:answer/id #uuid "6baff456-609b-5210-afb9-ca38e5adabcf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186346284} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc14084-3088-5b8a-ba2d-7b0e0a1aadc4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346981} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb60f3a-a601-548c-bc1f-20c9cc148f2f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186347151} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc15575-a017-565f-8f76-b1cadb4fc7dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186347601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbc14084-3088-5b8a-ba2d-7b0e0a1aadc4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346981} :answer-field/id #uuid "458244de-522e-5387-a418-9f5ed1cbeef6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e25ee2d5-6236-5cb7-ae46-36798fde6f33" :question/math-academy-id "q-79334" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ is **not** invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=n$\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is **not** invertible.* $\\,\\times $\n\nBy the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ we must have that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4b612ef-ce7f-5783-848d-d8ef210c9f47" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Argand Diagram Corresponding to a Complex Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c1e4021-3396-5bc6-83ea-5a27c6cabe76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186345596} {:answer/id #uuid "3dea748a-d0d4-5afc-925f-273d667f091e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345875} {:answer/id #uuid "7243bf19-b992-547d-9233-c657639a06c5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346361} {:answer/id #uuid "d13f5329-2ec1-5018-9bce-9ec236dfe908" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186347185} {:answer/id #uuid "d7dc2a08-b945-5e4e-9cd1-679eca7a90c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d13f5329-2ec1-5018-9bce-9ec236dfe908" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186347185} :answer-field/id #uuid "ced0da57-29f7-5224-b3bb-c98987c02847" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8289966f-f636-5804-9edf-c054d1b5acd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79457" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers lie in the second quadrant in the corresponding Argand diagram?\n\n- $-2-2\\text{i}$\n- $-2+\\text{i}$\n- $-1+2\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "- The complex number $-2-2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=-2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,-2)$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-2+\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,1)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-1+2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-1$ and the imaginary part $y=2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-1,2)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n![](images/9f/9f2e7234ba52d14098e0ecfc9695181b425764c110995b76b4b0efddf206252c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186348228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4b612ef-ce7f-5783-848d-d8ef210c9f47" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Argand Diagram Corresponding to a Complex Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d4636db-db02-5863-92b4-8abb34eb2771" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346581} {:answer/id #uuid "a4bb8bff-0b5e-51a9-8760-878220ba9463" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186346788} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f68a00-1742-54f4-9473-c700f4f12b30" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186347028} {:answer/id #uuid "ca930a2a-126d-5edd-aa68-a071673eb20c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186347117} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c0088a-60c2-538f-9ba7-d71e584516e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca930a2a-126d-5edd-aa68-a071673eb20c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186347117} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd69c411-1af3-5cc9-9bc9-5772e6c3d56f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186348048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1861228f-d3da-56c4-af5a-dc5d012713d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-79458" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers lie in the fourth quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n\n- $\\frac{3}{2}-\\text{i}$\n- $-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}$\n- $\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "- The complex number $\\frac{3}{2}-\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=-1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(\\frac{3}{2},-1)$, which lies in the fourth quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$, which lies in the first quadrant.\n\n![](images/d7/d7390ddcc8d338d2fd46bc12b0a4c65f8e6bf6e8c4f6c9fa802e2acea159416b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186348230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13447381-8721-54cc-93ad-ec3451ed8c7a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186345519} {:answer/id #uuid "548a412d-769e-5f37-b287-b5e8a2f3516b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186346069} {:answer/id #uuid "8312aa12-0acb-51e1-b48d-e1dd50ef851e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186346492} {:answer/id #uuid "9c74b04c-077f-5abe-93e4-5b311d8196e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346714} {:answer/id #uuid "e110475f-605d-52d6-9b4e-ec752d3c2333" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186347329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "548a412d-769e-5f37-b287-b5e8a2f3516b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186346069} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea32768f-9a8b-5730-aa6f-faea2f793e5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186348014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38fdf4c6-f5f9-5439-b136-b509926fb43c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79487" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186348239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d847ec9-c379-5013-ab77-a9b92dc8ca27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix With a Given Rank by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fcc3707-5257-56f7-a94a-c16be3c655ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186345482} {:answer/id #uuid "1488839f-b78a-5c71-b12c-fd396aefb4d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186345526} {:answer/id #uuid "79f9061d-b91e-5d06-8205-d8cf49d7a89e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186346419} {:answer/id #uuid "e4865247-2fc7-50f5-8dc6-90b9921dd7ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186347365} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc7ff50-8edf-53da-a902-6aa67ba79a1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186347430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1488839f-b78a-5c71-b12c-fd396aefb4d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186345526} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee01bee-957b-5375-bc24-a42dae9c7573" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7de7e9fe-a671-5464-bba9-92a57599131b" :question/math-academy-id "q-79568" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $5?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a matrix of rank $5$ must have at least $5$ columns and at least $5$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the matrix $A$ since it has only $3$ rows and the matrix $C$ since it has only $4$ columns. \n\nAlso, the matrix must have $5$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $B$ has exactly $5$ pivot columns. So, $\\text{rank}(B)=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186348241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02dd969f-3835-50e2-8bfc-d425532d61e9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186345375} {:answer/id #uuid "90284f75-7093-5853-a1bf-dfd348956afc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186346606} {:answer/id #uuid "a6006a37-315c-535b-84c5-4aae73a3316f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346800} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e5dd56-c7d7-5b38-9d36-b6ef7d0b527a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347087} {:answer/id #uuid "f4150680-d532-528d-904e-713e4f2b8907" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186347513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7e5dd56-c7d7-5b38-9d36-b6ef7d0b527a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347087} :answer-field/id #uuid "43f9a281-44ca-5645-8aea-5eda107eeda2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41288342-236a-5f59-a0ab-b7381746dd7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79575" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=40\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$\n\nII. $y=R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nIII. $y=R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186348242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045d95b9-c578-58ae-804d-b1f18050dded" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186345387} {:answer/id #uuid "1c552b52-c446-5ba7-b15e-630f222699c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186345597} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3e8db4-c9a7-54bf-bb89-13376400df6a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346020} {:answer/id #uuid "9c4634b0-4c75-5c24-a60d-26d06a0b1dbc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346710} {:answer/id #uuid "d32e21c2-215e-513f-b844-3f5720b2a2a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d32e21c2-215e-513f-b844-3f5720b2a2a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347199} :answer-field/id #uuid "420c198c-b60f-5531-84a3-03e2cd3be765" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1d20ac9-bf7d-58e9-8fa7-b423eaa3f414" :question/math-academy-id "q-79578" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(x)=3xy$\n- $g(x,y)={y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}y$\n- $h(x,y)={x}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+3{y}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$  It is a polynomial of degree $2,$ with no linear or constant terms.\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $3.$ However, it also contains a term $({y}^{2})$ that is of degree $2.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $3.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ and all of its terms are of degree $3.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186348244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7e4d86-e681-55dc-851f-626c243db8a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345989} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0f622d-c941-546a-ad1f-8acfacf65b39" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186346006} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6f9093-a9f2-5e62-b0ac-2ba309a44af7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346873} {:answer/id #uuid "edc2ccf5-8acb-5ce7-851c-d8e35173456f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186347450} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8725d2-f3d6-5d3b-b62d-26c66f72440d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a7e4d86-e681-55dc-851f-626c243db8a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345989} :answer-field/id #uuid "9becafd4-a5f8-56dc-8174-c3f2dd0c9c7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b57b422d-c4f7-5a90-ae50-c581e716c943" :question/math-academy-id "q-79586" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+2$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $y={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ Since the external force is a constant, we assume that the particular solution is also a constant,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nOnly II match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186348278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0923514d-c5f5-5824-9559-5b62433d29c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345422} {:answer/id #uuid "236eae5a-9ad6-5197-8d56-b3601675b294" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186345673} {:answer/id #uuid "71bf93d7-35da-5bf1-b1c4-67d62905acf2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346357} {:answer/id #uuid "ad47b4a5-f932-52e5-a826-24224069a452" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186346863} {:answer/id #uuid "ae59bf38-16be-58c7-bd1c-828957c06a49" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186346872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0923514d-c5f5-5824-9559-5b62433d29c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345422} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b8d684-4b0c-5a9a-8ebf-813cbe7bf7a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186348012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fe9a401-2c33-5db4-91d8-f65cf3a85456" :question/math-academy-id "q-79770" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{-2x}+{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{-2x}+{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7622f2e1-4bc7-5052-8059-cb0739d3adba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186346389} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2f1294-18fe-5249-8216-d3b2478a7f61" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346580} {:answer/id #uuid "b47aa73a-cb38-5bf6-a296-c1eae1ea691a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346924} {:answer/id #uuid "e718ee24-6201-580b-b3d7-2418fe4bb5b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347387} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd7344b-824c-54d9-95ae-9528861755ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186347431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebd7344b-824c-54d9-95ae-9528861755ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186347431} :answer-field/id #uuid "b93f0d66-ae9b-56db-8736-14ac1a058bd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc8e08c0-d0ce-557c-9060-08f946a61e52" :question/math-academy-id "q-79774" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=4x{e}^{4x}$\n- $y(x)=2{e}^{4x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=4x{e}^{4x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=2{e}^{4x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d51c4b-d54b-5abd-a602-97d128497895" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186345716} {:answer/id #uuid "2e42bc59-7be3-5fab-8e80-0f3c996bd387" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186345762} {:answer/id #uuid "5743a26a-1810-52e2-8511-82d7ec3b2ccd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186346095} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce8b541-90cf-5ba1-bd89-fbad0ae4d6f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346718} {:answer/id #uuid "f66a870a-6721-5d7a-a5d8-0275b073de0c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ce8b541-90cf-5ba1-bd89-fbad0ae4d6f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346718} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b85d4a3-33e9-554e-bcc5-65fac753f546" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04a3c220-dde3-5739-ac25-163b805528ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-79775" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-9y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=2x{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)=6{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)=-5{e}^{3x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=2x{e}^{3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=6{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=6.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=-5{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "565ac935-420d-5f95-a28d-92456faced76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346084} {:answer/id #uuid "991744d5-719c-562a-98e8-cbd3d093ac57" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346685} {:answer/id #uuid "9afcf3c3-ebc6-5837-b7cb-42137df13251" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346706} {:answer/id #uuid "9f998980-a9b5-5af7-8538-ea271ed86de1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186346745} {:answer/id #uuid "f5cf9711-8831-5d9e-8592-607bdbb062ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f998980-a9b5-5af7-8538-ea271ed86de1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186346745} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a64b2bc-95cd-52af-bee8-0963efc75e7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "107897db-d4be-5207-adc1-b404c9d01f49" :question/math-academy-id "q-79777" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-3.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{2}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1472b95c-69ea-5b14-8dd4-89aef46f7fe9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186345524} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0b76c8-9fcf-5df6-8c74-835bcbbfa612" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186345860} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff6d3eb-665a-5929-b7c0-8b79a662ccb9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346033} {:answer/id #uuid "e652fc59-a495-5990-be19-3d1a1a916abe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347382} {:answer/id #uuid "ed65c49d-dd46-5594-86e2-db77968435c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ff6d3eb-665a-5929-b7c0-8b79a662ccb9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346033} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d56d4c-d604-591b-aee3-dc06c79970cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb140c30-bdb5-5662-90f1-f05ca3e626cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-79800" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fie