ons [{:db/id 17592186059701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64a3922f-7e74-562f-9bb9-c4a41e912ce7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f471ffe-39d9-5cca-af20-8c094c9dcf4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186377881} {:answer/id #uuid "2f25dc99-ae72-5fee-9701-6c5422bb460f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186377882} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e8e06c-d3f8-5d2f-853a-20ce61ba063b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186377883} {:answer/id #uuid "d26a22a7-a8ba-5f39-b63c-1351f792d5f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186377884} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea80694-1e35-5138-9aa9-14406644ce5a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186377885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f471ffe-39d9-5cca-af20-8c094c9dcf4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186377881} :answer-field/id #uuid "61303d7f-a5cf-53cf-80eb-bc0761d300ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186377880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e834c31-c35d-57df-a0ce-02370148728a" :question/math-academy-id "q-82089" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- **If** $2+3<4,$ **then** $1+5<8.$\n- **If** $1+5<8,$ **then** $2+3<4.$\n- **If** $1+5<4,$ **then** $2+3<8.$" :question/worked-solution "An implication $A⇒B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. In other words, the implication is true unless the \"promise\" is broken.\n\n- Statement I is true. The antecedent $2+3<4$ is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. The antecedent $1+5<8$ is true, and the consequent $2+3<4$ is false. So the promise is broken, and the statement is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Again, the antecedent $1+5<4$ is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186377893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "281873c8-e173-5181-8281-96b84d636b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Chinese Remainder Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa3016eb-291f-5ec0-bcf5-22ef16b8043f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186377895} {:answer/id #uuid "14aed1ff-f3f2-56a3-8a5b-4aee72b089a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186377896} {:answer/id #uuid "0c4be289-4fe7-51ce-badb-d5a9568ee976" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186377897} {:answer/id #uuid "a810b8fc-0777-5c13-bf21-17710dfd3e95" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186377898} {:answer/id #uuid "12be2f6e-3c4c-5e44-b5f8-4e550f2e3648" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186377899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12be2f6e-3c4c-5e44-b5f8-4e550f2e3648" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186377899} :answer-field/id #uuid "01ae11c9-f622-50bf-a7c7-2005925c8a44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186377894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b00b20e-2b69-5a9e-a153-226cf102bd50" :question/math-academy-id "q-83978" :question/problem "According to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $48?$ \n\n$$\n\\text{I.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{II.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{III.}\\,{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system I since $6$ and $8$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(6,8)=2>1\n$$\n\n- The theorem applies to system II. The numbers $3,$ and $16$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $3\\cdot 16=48.$\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system III since $4$ and $12$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,12)=4>1\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186377969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f8227e2-8ccf-55e2-9bbf-51d1887099c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186377971} {:answer/id #uuid "4446d93a-94d1-50c2-9cd8-bfbf3a04a0dd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186377972} {:answer/id #uuid "d99e656c-bbf8-5fb3-866d-a3cc6e7277e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186377973} {:answer/id #uuid "5f29f94c-82b2-53f0-84ba-db7dd6cd56c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186377974} {:answer/id #uuid "73531dd9-bdee-5872-8c2d-ee0c25c8789e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id 17592186377975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4446d93a-94d1-50c2-9cd8-bfbf3a04a0dd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186377972} :answer-field/id #uuid "39aa91b6-39c4-5bfc-86c0-4c65c4f7778f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186377970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4951a3e-3ef9-5319-bbf9-d2c571b3026e" :question/math-academy-id "q-86684" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\n- $¬(¬X∧¬Y)$\n- $¬X∨¬Y$\n- $¬(X∧Y)$" :question/worked-solution "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,X\\,$ | $\\,Y\\,$ | $\\,¬(¬X∧¬Y)\\,$ | $\\,¬X∨¬Y\\,$ | $\\,¬(X∧Y)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬X∨¬Y$ and $¬(X∧Y)$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186377976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc20167-567b-5eba-8c5e-d41b0bb593f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186377978} {:answer/id #uuid "2fce39e2-0215-5f04-85bd-f5722d948c69" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186377979} {:answer/id #uuid "a707aa92-d9f7-5cd2-9eae-5036825cc2e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186377980} {:answer/id #uuid "b67a6a05-d7db-505f-94b7-b27bec775d75" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186377981} {:answer/id #uuid "d5483f18-a3e5-5029-941e-4175e51381c6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id 17592186377982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a707aa92-d9f7-5cd2-9eae-5036825cc2e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186377980} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bed9a28-ca77-5562-a870-aaf34e572c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186377977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17f91135-f9d8-56cf-88b5-eaf7c61a4e5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-86690" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\n- $¬X∧(X∨¬Y)$\n- $¬X∨(X∧¬Y)$\n- $¬(X∨(¬X∧Y))$" :question/worked-solution "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,X\\,$ | $\\,Y\\,$ | $\\,¬X∧(X∨¬Y)\\,$ | $\\,¬X∨(X∧¬Y)\\,$ | $\\,¬(X∨(¬X∧Y))\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬X∧(X∨¬Y)$ and $¬(X∨(¬X∧Y))$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186378181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4106fe70-b9df-5621-ba64-66703855aaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Conjunction Absorbs Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c286ba3-90a5-54e4-9eb4-c8a02089e4e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186378183} {:answer/id #uuid "99f71640-1095-56b8-a3fd-7d5b129b057d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186378184} {:answer/id #uuid "bff8657e-afd1-51b1-994d-a0d36032bc77" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186378185} {:answer/id #uuid "14057f5c-8e34-59bf-9689-6d27ce194bb4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186378186} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3f703e-aa28-5e90-93c4-3f328d